1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [http://resources.huygens.knaw.nl/retroboeken/archiefgids_gelre/#page=218&accessor=toc&view=pdfPane&size=634&source=1 In 1397 viel de heerlijkheid Tholen toe aan de grafelijkheid na het overlijden van G wijde van B
2 CONC lois. Het gebied is nadien steeds onderdeel geweest van het grafelijk domein.] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Tussen 1381 en 22 december 1397''' Laatste Graaf''' van Blois, Dunois en Soissons.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guy_II,_Count_of_Blois Op wikipedia wordt hij genoemd "Guy II, Count(graaf van) of Blois" de zoon van Louis/Lodewijk]
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://scholarlypublications.universiteitleiden.nl/access/item%3A2962260/view Vanaf 1323 kende het graafschap Zeeland twee rentmeesterschappen, namelijk dat van
2 CONT Bewestenschelde en dat van Beoostenschelde. Een derde ontstond in '''1397''', nadat Guy de Blois
2 CONT zonder wettig nageslacht '''overleed''' en Tholen en Schakerloo terugvielen aan de graaf.( voetnoot 98)]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guy_II_of_Blois-Ch%C3%A2tillon
2 CONT
2 CONT Guy II of Blois-Châtillon (died December 22, 1397), the youngest son of Louis I of Châtillon and Joan of Avesnes, was count of Blois and lord of Avesnes, Schoonhoven, and Gouda 1381–1397, and lord of Beaumont and Chimay.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1360, he was one of the hostages sent to the Kingdom of England by the terms of the Treaty of Brétigny. He was eventually ransomed by the sale of Soissons and was released on August 15, 1367. He was knighted in 1370 while crusa
2 CONC ding with the Teutonic Knights in Lithuania. In 1374 he married Marie of Namur, daughter of William I, Marquis of Namur, and they had one son:
2 CONT  Louis III of Châtillon (d. 1391)
2 CONT
2 CONT Thereafter he joined in the wars of king Charles VI, and commanded the rearguard at the Battle of Roosebeke. The death of his only son in 1391 prompted him to sell the inheritance of the County of Blois to Louis of Valois, Duke of
2 CONC  Orléans.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was for some time the patron of Jean Froissart: he appointed him his chaplain in 1384 and obtained for him the benefice of Lestines-au-Mont and the canonicate of Chimay. His patronage allowed Froissart to write Book II of his c
2 CONC hronicles.
