1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://nl.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huis_Avesnes
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://nl.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kasteel_van_Blois Kasteel van Blois, In de 13e eeuw werd het kasteel herbouwd door de Bourgondische familie Châtillon]
2 CONT
2 CONT https://nl.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baljuwschap_land_van_Blois 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://openaccess.leidenuniv.nl/bitstream/handle/1887/51172/Scriptie%20Twan%20van%20Dodewaard%2019-6-2017.pdf?sequence=1 De heren van Blois in Holland  Vervreemding in een 14de eeuws graafschap door Twan van Dodewaard]
2 CONT
2 CONT Jan van Beaumont (oorspronkelijk geheten "Jan van Henegouwen(fr. Hainaut)", de zoon van Graaf Jan van Holland welke op geni genoemd wordt " Jean II d'Avesnes ou Jean Ier de Hainaut" Via Jan van Beaumont's dochter Johanna(Jeanne) v
2 CONC an Henegouwen die trouwde met Lodewijk van Blois Chatillon gingen de goederen van grootvader Jan van Beaumont  over op zijn kleinzonen, eerst op Jan(ovl 1381) en daarna op zijn broer Guy(ovl. 1397) De laatste had geen kinderen en 
2 CONC daarom gingen de goederen na Guy's dood terug naar de Grafelijkheid. De goederen werden geerfd door de kleinzonen omdat Jan van Beaumont's dochter Johanna van Henegouwen en haar man Lodewijk de Chatillon al beide waren overleden t
2 CONC oen hij stierf ! Deze kleinzoon Jan (ovl. 1381) had zelf ook weer 2 (bastaard)zonen genaamd Jan(ovl 1435) en Guy(ovl. en begr. 1421 Tholen/NL), wat zeer verwarrend is. Daarnaast waren het ook zoals gezegd bastaaarden verwekt buite
2 CONC n het huwelijk. Hun moeder was Sophia van Dalem uit het huis Arkel, partner van Jan). '''N.b. in principe had Jan van Beaumont, zoals gebruikelijk, zijn testament zo ingericht dat leengoederen zouden gaan  naar de Grafelijkheid al
2 CONC s hijzelf geen konderen(of zonen??)had om aan  te vererven, maar daarnaast beschikt hij vanaf 1309 over persoonlijke goederen door aankoop, vererving e.d., die hij wel op anderen kon laten overgaan, waardoor maar een deel van zijn
2 CONC  bezit terugging naar de Grafelijkheid!''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://home.kpn.nl/f.angevaare/pdf/denkindex.pdf Alles over Jan van Henegouwen, Jan van Beaumont]
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://books.google.be/books?id=YENsc79YwTQC&lpg=PA54&dq=jan%20van%20beaumont%20%22jan%20van%20blois%22&hl=nl&pg=PA54#v=onepage&q=jan%20van%20beaumont%20%22jan%20van%20blois%22&f=false De situatie verder uitgelegd, o.a. dat Jan 
2 CONC geen wettige nakomelingen had (alleen 4 bastaard dochters en zonen) en daarom alles op broer Guy overging die uiteindelijk zelf ook geen nakomelingen had hoewel op geni wel genoemd wordt als zoon Louis III Chatillon] 
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT John of Beaumont was a younger brother of count William III of Holland.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born as John of Hainault, lord of Noordwijk, Beaumont, Gouda and Schoonhoven. He was born most likely in 1288 as son of count John II, Count of Holland and Philippa of Luxembourg. He died on 11 march, 1356.
2 CONT
2 CONT When his uncle John I, Count of Holland died in 1299, he left behind no descendants. As a result, his father inherited the county of Holland and Zeeland as John II, Count of Holland through his mother Adelaide of Holland. From the
2 CONC n on Hainault and Holland were in a personal union. John of Hainault bought the heerlijkheid (comparably to the english Barony) of Beaumont, located in the southern Netherlands, for his son.
2 CONT
2 CONT Count John II of Holland died in 1304 and was succeeded by his eldest son William III of Holland. On 21 june
2 CONT
2 CONT www.celtic-casimir.com
2 CONT
2 CONT d' AVESNES, Seigneur de Beaumont, Valenciennes et Condé 	Born: 1288  	Died: 11 Mar 1356
2 CONT
2 CONT Research Notes:
2 CONT  The Chronologia Johannes de Beke names (in order) "Iohannem primogenitum, Wilhelmum succedentem Hollandie comitem, Iohannem de Bellomonte, dominum Henricum Cameracensem canonicum, domicellum Walravenum, Iohannam abbatissam Fontan
2 CONC alecensem, Margareta comitissam Atrabatensem, Adelheydim comitissam Claromontensem, Mariam comitissam de Berbenoys, Machtildim dominam de Niele" as children of Count Jean & his wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage Information:
2 CONT  Jean married Marguerite de NESLE, Comtesse de Soissons, Dame de Chimay.., daughter of Hugues de NESLE, Comte de Soissons, and Jeanne, Dame de DARGIES et Catheux. (Marguerite de NESLE, Comtesse de Soissons, Dame de Chimay.. was bo
2 CONC rn in 1306 and died in October 1350.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT  Famille et Seigneurs d' Avesnes, Etienne Pattou, 2005, 2007, pp. 7, 9; Foundation for Medieveal Genealogy, Hainaut, Chapter 5: Comtes de Hainaut 1244-1356 (Avesnes),
2 CONT  1308, John received from his brother all the possessions of Gerard van Velsen, Willem van Zanden and Gerard Craaitenhout. This included the heerlijkheid Noordwijk and Beverwijk. On 23 juli 1313 Noordwijk and Beverwijk were raised
2 CONC  to hoge heerlijkheid (literally: high barony), which placed them amongst the most important fiefs in Holland. In 1316 John became lord of Tholen. Goes also came into his possession after it was taken from the Borssele family. His
2 CONC  most important residences were Beaumont and the castle of Schoonhoven in the southern Netherlands. In 1340 he founded a Carmelite monastery in Schoonhoven.
2 CONT
2 CONT John married Margaret of Soissons, which gave him the title of count of Soissons. They had a daughter, named Jeanne of Hainault, countess of Beaumont.
2 CONT
2 CONT John often replaced his absent brother as governor of Holland. In 1326 he led an expedition to England, through which king Edward II of England was driven out and replaced by king Edward III. In 1340 he was regent of Holland and Z
2 CONC eeland for his nephew, count William IV of Holland for a short time. In 1345 he led an expedition to Friesland together with William IV. At the battle of Warns William IV was killed by the frisians while John of Beaumont barely ma
2 CONC naged to escape. He claimed the right of succession to the three counties, but eventually the succession was awarded to the sister of count William IV. As a result, John left the Netherlands and travelled to france, and he was pre
2 CONC sent at the Battle of Crecy. Here his son-in-law Louis II, Count of Blois was killed. This made his grandson John II, Count of Blois heir to the expensive possessions in Holland and Zeeland. Afterwards John resided at the court of
2 CONC  Margaret of Burgundy
2 CONT
2 CONT John had a bastard son for whom he bought the heerlijkheid Treslong in Picardie. From this son decended the Bloys of Treslong.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bloys of Treslong is a family that descended from a bastard son of John of Beaumont. The Bloys' of Treslong included four flag-officers in the dutch marine.
2 CONT
2 CONT William Bloys of Treslong (1529 - 1594), a captain of the Gueux de mer
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacob Arnout Bloys of Treslong (1756 - 1826), also called Jacob Arnold Bastingius, was a Sectretary-General of the Dutch Navy
2 CONT
2 CONT Johan Arnold Bloys of Treslong (1757 - 1824). Schout-bij-nacht at the Battle of Kamperduin.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jhr. William Otto Bloys of Treslong (1765 - 1837), an uncle of Johan Arnold.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cornelius Ysaac Bloys of Treslong (1763 - 1826) made it to Captain in the Batavian and Royal Dutch Navy
2 CONT
2 CONT Ships named after Bloys of Treslong
2 CONT
2 CONT HNLMS Bloys van Treslong, a dutch frigate of the Kortenaer class.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]References
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.historischeverenigingschoonhoven.nl/pdf/BeleningJanvanBeaumont700jaargeleden.pdf (dutch)
2 CONT
2 CONT Vaderlandsch woordenboek, Jacobus Kok, 1789. [[1]]
