1 OCCU Comte de Penthièvre et de Goelo.  Vicomte de Limoges.  Seigneur d´Avaugour, de Landrecies, de Nouvion-en-Thièrache
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Jean I de Blois-Châtillon'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Comte de Penthièvre et de Goelo.  Vicomte de Limoges.  Seigneur d´Avaugour, de Landrecies, de Nouvion-en-Thièrache
2 CONT
2 CONT By virtue of the treaty of Brétigny, Jean II of France had to pay three million gold crowns, to Edward III of England, for his ransom, and would be released after he paid one million. As a guarantee for the payment of his ransom, 
2 CONC John gave as hostages two of his sons, several princes and nobles, four inhabitants of Paris, and two citizens from each of the nineteen principal towns of France.'''Jean Châtillon''', Comte de Porcien, was one of the nobles held 
2 CONC as hostage. This treaty was ratified and sworn to by the two kings and by their eldest sons on 24 October 1360 at Calais.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jean_Ier_de_Ch%C3%A2tillon
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean I de Châtillon (5 February 1340 – 16 January 1404) was a member of the Breton House of Penthièvre and Viscount of Limoges.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT Family origins and education
2 CONT He was the son of Charles de Châtillon-Blois, lord of Guise.
2 CONT
2 CONT His mother was Jeanne de Penthièvre.
2 CONT
2 CONT He has four siblings:
2 CONT
2 CONT Gui, sent as a hostage to England, where he died.
2 CONT Henry, died in 1400.
2 CONT Marie (1345-1404), Countess of Guise and Lady of Mayenne, married in 1360 to Louis, Duke of Anjou (1339 † 1384), Count of Maine, Count of Provence and Forcalquier: hence the succession of the lords of Guise, Mayenne, and Nouvion (
2 CONC → Anjou, Lorraine, Guise) ;
2 CONT Marguerite, married in 1351 to Charles de La Cerda (assassinated in 1354), count of Angoulême and constable of France.
2 CONT English wedding plans
2 CONT She tried to marry him to Margaret of England, daughter of King Edward III of England, but Henry de Grosmont, Earl of Derby and Lancaster, an ally of the Montfort's, broke off the negotiations. The King of England offered him anot
2 CONC her prestigious alliance with Philippa of Lancaster, but he preferred to remain loyal to France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Career
2 CONT A prisoner at Gloucester Castle from 1356, he had been placed in the custody of Robert de Vere, Earl of Oxford and Duke of Ireland. He was freed thanks to the help of Olivier de Clisson who advanced him 60,000 francs for his ranso
2 CONC m and proposed to him, through the intermediary of Jehan Rolland, a gentleman of Brittany, to marry his daughter Marguerite de Clisson.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the death of the King of France Charles V (1380), who supported the Penthièvres against the Montforts, with his mother Jeanne de Penthièvre, he negotiated the second Treaty of Guérande by which he renounced the Duchy of Brittan
2 CONC y in return for a large indemnity (14 April 1381).
2 CONT
2 CONT On 6 January 1384, he gave Olivier V de Clisson the management of all his property in Brittany and Limousin before being handed over to Jehan Rolland on 23 March 1387.
2 CONT
2 CONT Froissart recounts how, in 1391, Duke Jean IV, his great-grand cousin, denounced his unfounded claims to the Duchy of Brittany:
2 CONT
2 CONT "This Count of Penitreuve (Penthièvre), our cousin, is written and named John of Brittany, and bears the arms of Brittany, as well as if he were its heir. We want him to be called Jean, for that is his name, and Count of Penthievr
2 CONC e, but we want him to put the ermines and be written Jean de Blois or de Châtillon and no other. And if he does not do so, we will make him do and take away from him, for he holds it in faith and homage from us; and also to the in
2 CONC heritance of Brittany he has no need ever to think that it returns to him, for we have son and daughter who will be our heirs; if he sees himself (going) to hunt elsewhere, for to our heritage has failed. »
2 CONT
2 CONT — Jean Froissart, Chroniques de Froissart, Volume 12, 1825, p. 359.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1392, at the conference of Tours, he renounced for the Montfort's the right to bear the full arms of Brittany. This was confirmed at Guingamp on 25 October 1395. [unclear]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1397, on the death of Guy II of Blois-Châtillon, his cousin, he inherited the lands of Avesnes, Landrecies, Nouvion-en-Thiérache (inheritance of the Blois in the north of France) and some lands in Flanders.
2 CONT
2 CONT In December 1400 he inherited the property of his brother Henry, who died in England without children. Buried at the Cordeliers in Guingamp.
2 CONT
2 CONT His eldest son, Olivier, succeeded him. He shared the family's inheritance with his 3 brothers: Olivier received Penthièvre, Jean the land of the Aigle in Normandy, Charles the lordship of Avaugour (father of Nicole de Penthièvre)
2 CONC , and finally Guillaume the viscounty of Limoges1.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and descendants
2 CONT He married in Moncontour on 20 January 1387 to Marguerite de Clisson, daughter of the constable Olivier de Clisson, with whom he had several children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Olivier de Blois, dit de Bretagne, count of Penthièvre, viscount of Limoges, lord of Avesnes († 1433 without legitimate issue);
2 CONT Jean de Châtillon, known as Jean de L'Aigle or de Bretagne, Viscount of Limoges, Count of Périgord († 1454 without issue);
2 CONT Charles de Châtillon-Blois, baron of Avaugour, whose daughter Nicole, daughter of his wife Isabeau de Vivonne, inherited: hence the succession of the counts of Penthièvre and the lords of Laigle (→ de Brosse de Boussac, Luxembourg
2 CONC -Martigues, Lorraine-Mercœur, Bourbon-Vendôme) ;
2 CONT Guillaume de Châtillon-Blois, viscount of Limoges († 1455): x Isabeau daughter of Bertrand V de la Tour, count of Auvergne, hence the succession of the viscounts of Limoges, the counts of Périgord, and the lords of Avesnes and Lan
2 CONC drecies (→ Albret, Bourbon-Vendôme) ;
2 CONT Jeanne de Blois, known as de Bretagne, married without issue (1458) Jean III de Harpedane, lord of Belleville and Montaigu, for whom it was his second marriage (he was a widower with posterity of Marguerite de Valois, natural daug
2 CONC hter of Charles VI)
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.familysearch.org/tree/person/details/LT9F-NSJ
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Ch%C3%A2tillon-39
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/118871581/jean-de_ch%C3%A2tillon
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean I de Châtillon was count of Penthièvre, Viscount of Limoges.
2 CONT In 1392, conference Tours, he renounced to the Montfort to bear arms full of Brittany. This was confirmed in Guingamp 25 October 1395.
2 CONT In 1397, the death of Guy II de Châtillon, his cousin, he inherited the lands of Avesne, Landrecies, nouvion and some land in Flanders.
2 CONT He inherited in December 1400 of the property of his brother Henri died in England without child. Buried in the Cordeliers, his son Olivier succeeded him, who shared with his brother Jean de L'Aigle.
2 CONT He was the son of Charles of Blois and Jeanne de Penthièvre, known as the lame. His siblings were:
2 CONT Gui, sent as a hostage in England, where he died.
2 CONT Henry, who died in 1400.
2 CONT Marie (1345 – 1404), Countess of Guise, married in 1360 Louis Duke de Anjou (1339 – 1384), count of Maine, count of Provence and Forcalquier.
2 CONT Marguerite, married in 1351 Charles de La Cerda (died 1354), count of Angoulême and Constable of France.
2 CONT He was married Marguerite of Clisson, daughter of du Connétable Olivier de Clisson, in Moncontour on January 20, 1387.
2 CONT Their children were:
2 CONT Olivier de Blois, or de Brittany, count of Penthièvre, Viscount of Limoges, Lord of Avenes (died 1433).
2 CONT Jean de Châtillon said Jean L'Aigle or Brittany, Viscount of Limoges (died 1454).
2 CONT Charles de Blois-Châtillon, baron Davaugour.
2 CONT William de Châtillon - Blois, Viscount of Limoges (died 1455).
2 CONT Jeanne de Blois, so-called of Brittany, married his second wife (1458) John de Harpedan
2 CONT
2 CONT ______
