1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_de_Namur
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert of Namur, KG (1323 – April 1391) was a noble from the Low Countries close to King Edward III of England. He was made Knight of the Garter in 1369.
2 CONT
2 CONT His was the son of John I, Count of Namur, and Marie, Lady of Merode.
2 CONT
2 CONT As a young man, he participated in crusades in Prussia and The Holy Land. His uncle Robert III of Artois, which had English sympathies, made him journey in 1346 to Calais to meet Edward III of England, who was besieging the city. 
2 CONC Robert made a good impression on the King.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 30 August 1350, Robert and Henry of Grosmont commanded the English flagship Salle du Roy, at the Battle of Les Espagnols sur Mer, off Winchelsea.  http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Les_Espagnols_sur_Mer
2 CONT
2 CONT On 2 February 1354, Robert of Namur married Isabella of Hainault (1323–1361), younger sister of Philippa of Hainault, queen consort of King Edward III of England. Thus Robert became brother-in-law to Edward III. Robert captured th
2 CONC e Escanaffles Castle in 1363. Robert brought Jean Froissart to England, and introduced him to Queen Philippa. In 1369, Robert defended the English camp at Tournehem against French attack. He was made Knight of the Garter in 1369 a
2 CONC fter the death of Robert d'Ufford, 1st Earl of Suffolk.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1370, Robert requested Jean Froissart to write a recent chronicle of the history of England. On 20 August 1371, Robert fought for Wenceslaus I, Duke of Luxembourg and Brabant, commanding 2,000 men at the Battle of Baesweiler, b
2 CONC ut was defeated and released after paying a ransom. In 1373, Jean Froissart completed his first book of the Chronicles and dedicated it to Robert of Namur.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and Children
2 CONT
2 CONT On 2 February 1354, Robert of Namur married Isabella of Hainault (1323–1361), sister of Queen Philippa of England and daughter of William I, Count of Hainaut, and Joan of Valois.
2 CONT
2 CONT  On 4 February 1380, he married Isabeau de Melun (died 1409). Both marriages remained childless, but Robert is said to have had 9 illegitimate children.
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Foundation for Medieval Genealogy Medlands Project page on Namur:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/NAMUR.htm#_Toc285474094
2 CONT
2 CONT JEAN I 1298-1330, JEAN II 1330-1335, GUY II 1335-1336, PHILIPPE III 1336-1337
2 CONT
2 CONT JEAN de Flandre, son of GUY Count of Flanders & his second wife Isabelle de Luxembourg Ctss de Namur (1267-10 Feb 1330, Bruges, église des Cordeliers).  
2 CONT *The Chronique Normande names "Jehan, Guy et Henry" as the three sons of "conte en Flandres…Guy de Dampierre" by his second wife "fille au conte de Luxembourg"[185].  His parentage is confirmed by the Continuatio of the Chronicle 
2 CONC of Guillaume de Nangis which records the marriage in 1308 of "Johannes de Namursio filius Guidonis Flandrensis comitis"[186].  
2 CONT *His father appointed him as governor of the county of Namur at Gent 5 Nov 1297, then ceded his rights to the county 2 Oct 1298, whereby he became JEAN I Comte de Namur.  
2 CONT *His Flanders militia won the battle of Courtrai 11 Jul 1302, but was beaten at Mons-en-Pévèle in 1304.  
2 CONT *Regent of Flanders 1302-1305.  
2 CONT *The necrology of Floreffe records the death "IV Id Feb" in 1330 of "Johannis de Flandria comitis Namurcensis"[187]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Betrothed (Sep 1290) to BLANCHE de France, daughter of PHILIPPE III "le Hardi" King of France & his second wife Marie de Brabant (1278-Vienna 14 Mar 1306, bur Vienna, Minoritenkirche). 
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly (1307) MARGUERITE de Clermont, daughter of ROBERT de France Comte de Clermont et Sire de Bourbon & his wife Béatrix dame de Bourbon (1289-Paris Jan 1309, bur Paris, église des Jacobins).  
2 CONT *The Continuatio of the Chronicle of Guillaume de Nangis records the marriage in 1308 of "Johannes de Namursio filius Guidonis Flandrensis comitis" and "filiam Roberti comitis Clarimontis"[188].  The Continuatio of the Chronicle o
2 CONC f Guillaume de Nangis which records the burial "circa Purificationem beatæ Virginis...Parisius" in 1308 of "filia Roberti comitis Clari-Montis uxor Johannis de Namursio"[189]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT m secondly (contract Paris 6 Mar 1310, confirmed Poissy Jan 1313) MARIE d'Artois, daughter of PHILIPPE d’Artois [Capet] Seigneur de Conches & his wife Blanche de Bretagne (1291-Wijnendael 22 Jan 1365, bur Namur, église des Cordeli
2 CONC ers).  
2 CONT *The Continuatio of the Chronicle of Guillaume de Nangis which records that "Johannis de Namursio" married "filiam dominæ Blanchæ de Britannia" after the death of his first wife[190].  Her husband granted her as dowry the castle o
2 CONC f Wijnendael in Flanders, ratified by the Count of Flanders in 1313.  
2 CONT *She acquired the château de Poilvache from Jean de Luxembourg King of Bohemia 20 Feb 1342, and transferred it to her son Guillaume Comte de Namur 11 Sep 1353.  "Marie d´Artoys contesse de Namur" acknowledged repayment of a loan f
2 CONC rom the mayor of Namur by charter dated 8 Sep 1343[191].  "Guillelmes contes de Namur" acknowledged receiving repayment of a debt due to "nostre…mère madame Marie d´Artois contesse de Namur et dame dele Escluze en Flandre" by the 
2 CONC commune of Namur by charter dated 30 Dec 1356[192]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Comte Jean & his second wife had eleven children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.         JEAN de Namur ([1310/12]-2 Apr 1335, bur Kloster Spaltheim).  
2 CONT *He succeeded his father in 1330 as JEAN II Comte de Namur.  
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.         GUY de Namur ([1311/13]-12 Mar 1336).  
2 CONT *He succeeded his brother in 1335 as GUY II Comte de Namur. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.         HENRI de Namur ([1312/13]-8 Oct 1333).  
2 CONT *Canon at Chartres Cathedral.  
2 CONT *Canon at Cambrai Cathedral 1324.  
2 CONT *Canon at Chalon-sur-Marne and Reims 1325. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.         BLANCHE de Namur (-Copenhagen Autumn 1363).  
2 CONT *She was accused by the noblewoman Birgitta Birgersdatter (St Birgitta) of having poisoned the latter's son, her innocence of the crime only being proved at the end of the 18th century[193].  She lived at Tønsberghus castle in Nor
2 CONC way from 1358, because of the political situation in Sweden, and administered the fiefs of Vestfold and Skienssysla[194].  
2 CONT *m (Bohus Castle 5 Nov 1335) MAGNUS II King of Sweden and Norway, son of ERIK Magnusson of Sweden Duke in Södermanland & his wife Ingeborg of Norway (1316-drowned near Bergen 1 Dec 1374, bur Varnhem Abbey).  He was deposed in 1344
2 CONC  as King of Norway, and in 1363 as King of Sweden.   
2 CONT
2 CONT 5.         PHILIPPE de Namur ([1319]-murdered Famagusta Sep 1337).  
2 CONT *He succeeded his brother in 1336 as PHILIPPE III Comte de Namur.  "Philippus comes Namucensis" donated property to St Alban, in accordance with the testament of "frater noster dominus Guido quondam comes Namucensis", by charter d
2 CONC ated 23 Jun 1336[195]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 6.         MARIE de Namur (1322-before 29 Oct 1357).  
2 CONT *m firstly ([1335/36]) HEINRICH [II] Graf von Vianden, son of PHILIPP [II] Graf von Vianden & his wife Adelheid von Arnsberg (-murdered Famagusta Sep 1337).  
2 CONT *m secondly (1340, dispensation 9 Sep 1342) THIBAUT de Bar Seigneur de Pierrepont, son of ERARD de Bar Seigneur de Pierrepont et d'Ancerville & his wife Isabelle de Lorraine (-[2 Jul 1353/6 Jul 1354]).  
2 CONT
2 CONT 7.         MARGUERITE de Namur ([1323]-13 Sep 1383).  
2 CONT *Nun at Peteghem.  
2 CONT
2 CONT 8.         GUILLAUME de Namur (1324-1 Oct 1391, bur Namur, couvent des Franciscains).  
2 CONT *He succeeded his brother in 1337 as GUILLAUME I "le Riche" Comte de Namur. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT 9.         ROBERT de Namur ([1325]-[1/29] Apr 1391).  
2 CONT *Seigneur de Beaufort-sur-Meuse et de Renaix.  "Guillaumes contes de Namur, Robers ses…frères sires de Bealfort" acknowledged a debt by charter dated 15 Dec 1356[196].  
2 CONT *Marshal of Brabant.  
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly (dispensation 18 Oct 1354) ISABELLE de Hainaut, daughter of GUILLAUME III Comte de Hainaut [WILLEM III Count of Holland] & his wife Jeanne de Valois ([1323]-3 Jun 1361).  
2 CONT *Froissart records that "le conte de Hainau…[sa fille] maisnée Yzabel" married "messire Robert de Namur et fu dame de Renais en Flandres et de Bieaufort sur Meuse" long after the death of her father[197].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m secondly (4 Feb 1380) as her first husband, ISABEAU de Melun heiress of Viane, daughter of HUGUES de Melun Seigneur d'Antoing (-1409).  
2 CONT *She married secondly ([1394]) Bertrand de la Boverie (-5 Apr 1425), who became avoué of Liège 1413/25.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert had two illegitimate children by unknown mistresses: 
2 CONT
2 CONT a)         ROBERT .  1363/94.  
2 CONT *Provost of St Lambert at Liège 1381.  
2 CONT *Canon at Cambrai Cathedral 1387.  
2 CONT
2 CONT b)         PHILIPPE .  1403.  
2 CONT *Canon of St Pierre at Lille and of Ste Marie at Courtrai. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT 10.      LOUIS de Namur ([1325]-[1378/86]).  
2 CONT *Seigneur de Peteghem et de Bailleul.  Flemish counsellor.  Governor of Namur 1351.  
2 CONT *m (17 May 1365) ISABELLE de Roucy Dame de Roucy, daughter and heiress of ROBERT II Comte de Roucy & his wife Marie d'Enghien (-after 1396). 
2 CONT
2 CONT 11.      ELISABETH de Namur (1329-Heidelberg 29 Mar 1382, bur Heidelberg Franciscan Kloster).  
2 CONT *The necrology of Neustadt records the death 29 Mar 1382 of "Elizabeth comitissa de Namen, nata de domo Franciæ, uxor Ruperti senioris, electoris imperii, comitis palatini Reni, fundatoris huius ecclesiæ"[198].  
2 CONT *m ([Autumn 1350/Summer 1358]) as his first wife, RUPPRECHT I "der Rote" Pfalzfgraf bei Rhein, son of RUDOLF I joint Duke of Upper Bavaria and joint Pfalzgraf bei Rhein & his wife Mechtild von Nassau (Wolfratshausen 9 Jun 1309-Neu
2 CONC stadt 16 Feb 1390, bur Neustadt St Aegidius). 
2 CONT
2 CONT References:
2 CONT
2 CONT *[185] Molinier, A. & E. (eds.) (1882) Chronique normande du XIV siècle (Paris) ("Chronique Normande (14th century)"), p. 1. 
2 CONT *[186] RHGF XX, Continuatio Chronici Guillelmi de Nangiaco, p. 597. 
2 CONT *[187] Floreffe Nécrologe, p. 34. 
2 CONT *[188] RHGF XX, Continuatio Chronici Guillelmi de Nangiaco, p. 597. 
2 CONT *[189] RHGF XX, Continuatio Chronici Guillelmi de Nangiaco, p. 598. 
2 CONT *[190] RHGF XX, Continuatio Chronici Guillelmi de Nangiaco, p. 599. 
2 CONT *[191] Borgnet, J. & Bormans, S. (eds.) (1878) Cartulaire de la commune de Namur, Tome II (Namur) ("Namur (Borgnet & Bormans)"), Vol. II, 66, p. 1. 
2 CONT *[192] Namur (Borgnet & Bormans), Tome II, 75, p. 34. 
2 CONT *[193] Imsen, Steinar 'Late Medieval Scandinavian Queenship', Duggan, A. (ed.) (1997) Queens and Queenship in Medieval Europe (The Boydell Press), p. 55. 
2 CONT *[194] Imsen 'Late Medieval Scandinavian Queenship', p. 62. 
2 CONT *[195] Miraeus (1723), Tome I, Diplomata Belgica, Liber II, CXI, p. 322. 
2 CONT *[196] Namur (Borgnet & Bormans), Tome II, 74, p. 27. 
2 CONT *[197] Luce, S. (ed.) (1869) Chroniques de J. Froissart (Paris) ("Froissart"), Tome I, Livre 1, 60, alternative text, p. 398. 
2 CONT *[198] Mone, F. J. (1848) Quellensammlung der badische Landesgeschichte (Karlsruhe) ("Badische Quellensammlung"), p. 221, quoting Totenbuch von Neustadt, Bl. 33 a. 
