1 NOTE {geni:about_me} page 957 https://books.google.ca/books?id=hIyi0OgYMLsC&pg=RA1-PA1137&lpg=RA1-PA1137&dq=heer+van+hoenkoop&source=bl&ots=rj_w13txCb&sig=_KcfVYYqYIUDClw_WZ0rPuG2Lxg&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwjU7c_u86vQAhVQ-GMKHcfjAeoQ6AEI
2 CONC MzAD#v=onepage&q=egmon%201539%20meerenstein&f=false
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Jan van Egmond van Meerestein is not closely related to Lamoraal van Egmont or to his son Philip van Egmont: https://www.geni.com/people/Philip-van-Egmont/6000000159986878903?through=6000000006597081346#/tab/overview
2 CONT
2 CONT See [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philip,_Count_of_Egmont '''Wikipedia...''']
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip, Count of Egmont (1558 – Ivry March 14, 1590) was the fifth Count of Egmont, prince of Gavere and 12th and last Lord of Purmerend, Purmerland and Ilpendam.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the eldest son and successor of Lamoral, Count of Egmont, who was beheaded by the Spanish in 1568 in Brussels.
2 CONT
2 CONT William of Orange was his guardian, and the first years of his military career, Philip fought in the army of the Dutch rebels. He was present in the lost Battle of Gembloux in 1578.
2 CONT
2 CONT But in 1579, Philip broke off all contact with William of Orange and offered his services to King Philip II of Spain, for whom he reconquered several cities. For this he was made a Knight in the Order of the Golden Fleece.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was killed in 1590 in the Battle of Ivry against the new King Henry IV of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip had married on September 27, 1579 with Marie of Horne. They had no children. He was succeeded by his younger brother Lamoral.
