1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blanche_of_Anjou
2 CONT --------------
2 CONT -http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SICILY.htm#Blanchedied1310
2 CONT
2 CONT 6.         BLANCHE of Sicily ([1280]-Barcelona 14 Oct 1310, bur Santa Croce).  The Crónica de San Juan de la Peña records the marriage of King Jaime and "Doña Blanca filia del…rey Carlos" at "Villabeltran en el dia Todos-Santos" i
2 CONC n 1295[861].  She was crowned Queen of Aragon at Zaragoza in 1296.  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Betrothed''' ([1290]) to GIOVANNI I Marchese di Monferrato, son of GUGLIELMO VII Marchese di Monferrato & his third wife Infanta doña Beatriz de Castilla (Milan 1278-[9 Mar 1305]).  The father of his betrothed was his guardian 
2 CONC after his succession in 1290.  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' (Vilabertran, Catalonia 1 Nov 1295) as his second wife, JAIME II King of Aragon, son of PEDRO III King of Aragon & his wife Constanza of Sicily [Hohenstaufen] (Valencia 10 Aug 1267-Barcelona 5 Nov 1327, bur Barcelona churc
2 CONC h of San Francisco, transferred to Monastery of Santa Cruz, prov Tarragona). 
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------
2 CONT ---------
2 CONT --------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanca de Anjou-Sicilia (1280; Nápoles, Italia - 1310; Barcelona, España), Princesa de Nápoles y Reina Consorte de Aragón.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hija del rey Carlos II de Nápoles y de su esposa María de Hungría. En el tratado de Anagni de 1295 se concertó su matrimonio con el rey Jaime II, matrimonio que se celebró el 29 de octubre de 1295 en el Monasterio de Santa María d
2 CONC e Vilabertrán. Fue una mujer de gran religiosidad que acompañó a su esposo en numerosas ocasiones; llegó a acompañarle en algunas expediciones militares.
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanca de Nápoles tuvo su propia cancillería. Falleció en Barcelona y está enterrada en el monasterio de Santes Creus.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Descendencia  Blanca y el rey Jaime tuvieron un total de diez hijos.
2 CONT
2 CONT Infante Jaime de Aragón (1296-1334), que renunció a sus derechos reales y a casarse con Leonor de Castilla. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Infante Alfonso IV de Aragón (1299-1336), conde de Barcelona y rey de Aragón 
2 CONT
2 CONT Infanta Maria de Aragón y de Anjou (1299-1311), casada con Pedro de Castilla, hijo de Sancho IV de Castilla 
2 CONT
2 CONT Infanta Constanza de Aragón (1300-1327), casada con Don Juan Manuel. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Infanta Isabel de Aragón y de Anjou (1302-1330), que casó en 1315 con Federico I de Austria. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Infante Juan de Aragón (1304-1334), arzobispo de Toledo, de Tarragona y patriarca de Alejandría. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Infante Pedro de Aragón (1305-1381), conde de Ribagorza de Ampurias y de Prades. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Infanta Blanca de Aragón, (1307-1348), religiosa. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Infante Ramón Berenguer, (1308-1364),Conde de Prades y Señor de la Villa de Elche]]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Infanta Violante de Aragón (1310-1353), casada con Felipe, déspota de Rumania e hijo de Felipe I de Tarento. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Obtenido de "http://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blanca_de_N%C3%A1poles"
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanche of Anjou
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanche of Anjou (1280 – October 14, 1310 in Barcelona) was the second Queen consort of James II of Aragon.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the second daughter of Charles II of Naples and Maria Arpad of Hungary.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her paternal grandparents were Charles I of Sicily and Beatrice of Provence. Her maternal grandparents were Stephen V of Hungary (d. 1272) and his wife, queen Elisabeth, who was daughter of Zayhan of Kuni, a chief of the Cuman tri
2 CONC be and had been a pagan before her marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was buried at Santa Croce.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT On 29 October or 1 November 1295 at Villabertran, Blanche married James II of Aragon. They had ten children:
2 CONT
2 CONT James (Jaume) (b. 29 September 1296 - d. Tarragona, July 1334). James renounced his right to the throne in 1319 to become a monk. He refused to consummate his marriage to Leonor of Castille, who later become the second wife of his
2 CONC  brother Alfonso.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso IV of Aragon (1299 – 24 January 1336). He became the King of Aragon in 1327 and ruled until his death. He married twice: first to Teresa d'Entença and then to Eleanor of Castile after his first wife died.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria (b. 1299 - d. as a nun in Sijena, 1316). She married Pedro of Castile, son of Sancho IV of Castile.
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance (Constança) (b. Valencia, 1 April 1300 - d. Castillo de Garcia Munoz, 19 September 1327). Constance married Juan Manuel, Duke of Penafiel, nephew of Alfonso X of Castile.
2 CONT
2 CONT John (Juan) (b. 1304 - d. Pobo, Zaragoza, 19 August 1334). John became the first Archbishop of Toledo and Tarragona in 1318, and Patriarch of Alexandria in 1328.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabel of Aragon (b. 1305 - d. Styria, 12 July 1330). Elizabeth married Frederick I of Austria.
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter (Pere) (b. 1305 - d. Pisa, 4 November 1381), Count of Ribagorça and Prades. Peter married Jeanne, daughter of Gaston I of Foix.
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanca (b. 1307 - d. Barcelona, 1348), Prioress of Sixena.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ramon Berenguer (b. August 1308 - d. a priest at Barcelona, 1366), Count of Empúries and Baron of Ejerica.. Ramon married firstly with Blanca, daughter of Philip I of Taranto, and secondly with Maria, daughter of Jaime of Aragon.
2 CONT
2 CONT Violante (b. Barcelona, October 1310 - d. Pedrola, 19 July 1353). She first married Philip, Despot of Romania, son of Philip I of Taranto. Her second marriage was with Lope de Luna, Lord of Segorbe.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Blanche of Anjou (1280 – 14 October 1310) was Queen of Aragon as the second wife of King James II. She was a member of the Capetian House of Anjou, she is also known as Blanche of Naples
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanche was the eleventh child of Charles II of Naples and Maria of Hungary. Among her siblings were Robert I of Naples, Philip I of Taranto, titular Emperor of Constantinople, Charles Martel, titular King of Hungary, Eleanor, Que
2 CONC en of Sicily, and Maria, Queen of Majorca. She was originally betrothed in 1290 to John I, Marquess of Montferrat.[1] Her father was helping him defend Montferrat, hoping to make John his vassal. However, the engagement was broken
2 CONC  off and John died childless in 1305.
2 CONT
2 CONT The new Pope Boniface VIII, elected in 1294 at Naples under the auspices of King Charles, mediated between the latter and James II of Aragon, and the Treaty of Anagni was signed: James was to marry Blanche and was promised the inv
2 CONC estiture by the pope of Sardinia and Corsica, while he was to leave the Angevin a free hand in Sicily and even to assist him if the Sicilians resisted.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 29 October or 1 November 1295 at Vilabertran, Blanche and James were married. They had ten children:
2 CONT
2 CONT     James (b. 29 September 1296 – d. Tarragona, July 1334). James renounced his right to the throne in 1319 to become a monk. He refused to consummate his marriage to Eleanor of Castile, who later become the second wife of his bro
2 CONC ther Alfonso.
2 CONT     Alfonso IV of Aragon (1299 – 24 January 1336). He became the King of Aragon in 1327 and ruled until his death. He married twice: first to Teresa d'Entença and then to Eleanor of Castile after his first wife died.
2 CONT     Maria (b. 1299 – d. as a nun in Sijena, 1316). She married Infante Peter of Castile, son of Sancho IV of Castile.
2 CONT     Constance (b. Valencia, 1 April 1300 – d. Castillo de Garcia Munoz, 19 September 1327). Constance married Juan Manuel, Prince of Villena, nephew of Alfonso X of Castile.
2 CONT     Isabella (b. 1302 – d. Styria, 12 July 1330), she married Frederick I of Austria.
2 CONT     John (b. 1304 – d. Pobo, Zaragoza, 19 August 1334). John became the first Archbishop of Toledo and Tarragona in 1318, and Patriarch of Alexandria in 1328.
2 CONT     Peter (b. 1305 – d. Pisa, 4 November 1381), Count of Ribagorça and Prades. Peter married Jeanne, daughter of Gaston I of Foix. Peter was the father of Eleanor, Queen of Cyprus.
2 CONT     Blanche (b. 1307 – d. Barcelona, 1348), Prioress of Sixena.
2 CONT     Ramon Berenguer (b. August 1308 – d. a priest at Barcelona, 1366), Count of Empúries and Baron of Ejerica.. Ramon married firstly Blanca, daughter of Philip I of Taranto, and secondly Maria, daughter of Jaime of Aragon.
2 CONT     Violante (b. Barcelona, October 1310 – d. Pedrola, 19 July 1353). She first married Philip, Despot of Romania, son of Philip I of Taranto. Her second marriage was to Lope de Luna, Lord of Segorbe.
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanche died on the 14 October 1310, her death was probably linked to the birth of her daughter Violante, who was born in October 1310. Blanche was buried at Santes Creus. She was survived by her husband, children and mother.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ancestry
2 CONT --------------------
