2 CONT    Blanche, Duchess of Austria 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Divine Comedy, Dante sees Philip's spirit outside the gates of Purgatory with a number of other contemporary European rulers. Dante does not name Philip directly, but refers to him as "the small-nosed" and "the father of th
2 CONC e Pest of France."
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Marriage and children
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip with MarieOn 28 May 1262, Philip married Isabella of Aragon, daughter of James I of Aragon and his second wife Yolande of Hungary, daughter of Andrew II of Hungary, and had the following children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.Louis (1265 – May 1276). He was poisoned, possibly by orders of his stepmother.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.Philip IV (1268 – 29 November 1314), successor as king.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.Robert (1269 - 1271).
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.Charles (12 March 1270 – 16 December 1325), Count of Valois.
2 CONT
2 CONT 5.Stillborn son (1271).
2 CONT
2 CONT After Isabella's death, he married on 21 August 1274, Maria of Brabant, daughter of Henry III of Brabant and Adelaide of Burgundy. Their children were:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.Louis (May 1276 – 19 May 1319), Count of Évreux.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.Blanche (1278 – 19 March 1305, Vienna), married Rudolf III of Austria on 25 May 1300.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.Margaret (1282 – 14 February 1317), married Edward I of England
2 CONT
