1 NOTE [FAVthomas.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT Crowned at Rheims 15 Aug 1271
2 CONT    Byname Philip The Bold, French Philippe Le Hardi king of France(1270/85), in whose reign the power of the monarchy was enlarged and theroyal domain extended, though his foreign policy and military ventureswere largely unsuccess
2 CONC ful.
2 CONT    Philip, the second son of Louis IX of France (Saint Louis), becameheir to the throne on the death of his elder brother Louis (1260).Accompanying his father's crusade against Tunis in 1270, he was in Africawhen Louis IX died. He
2 CONC  was anointed king at Reims in 1271.
2 CONT    Philip continued his father's highly successful administration bykeeping in office his able and experienced household clerks. Mathieu deVendôme, abbot of Saint-Denis, whom Louis IX had left as regent inFrance, remained in contr
2 CONC ol of the government. The death in 1271 ofAlphonse of Poitiers and his wife, heiress of Toulouse, enabled Philipearly in his reign to annex their vast holdings to the royal demesne.Nevertheless, in 1279 he was obliged to cede the 
2 CONC county of Agenais toEdward I of England. The marriage in 1284 of Philip's son, the futurePhilip IV, to Joan, the heiress of the crown of Navarre and thecountships of Champagne and Brie, brought these important areas alsounder Cape
2 CONC tian control. In addition Philip over the years made numeroussmall territorial acquisitions.
2 CONT    Philip was less successful militarily. In 1276 he declared war tosupport the claims of his nephews as heirs in Castile but soon abandonedthe venture. In 1284, at the instigation of Pope Martin IV, Philiplaunched a campaign agai
2 CONC nst Peter III of Aragón, as part of the War ofthe Sicilian Vespers, in which the Aragonese opposed the Angevin rulersof Sicily. Philip crossed the Pyrenees with his army in May 1285, but theatrocities perpetrated by his forces pro
2 CONC voked a guerrilla uprising. Aftera meaningless
2 CONT victory at Gerona and the destruction of his fleet at Las Hormigas,Philip was forced to retreat. He died of fever on the way home.
2 CONT
2 CONT To cite this page: "Philip III" Encyclopædia Britannica
2 CONT <http://www.britannica.com/eb/article?eu=61163&tocid=0&query=philip%20iii>
