2 CONT
2 CONT Philip IV (April-June 1268 – November 29, 1314), called the Fair (French: le Bel), son and successor of Philip III, reigned as King of France from 1285 until his death. He was the husband of Joan I of Navarre, by virtue of which h
2 CONC e was King of Navarre (as Philip I) and Count of Champagne from 1284 to 1305. The nickname Philip "the Fair" comes from his handsome appearance; it had nothing to do with his actions as King.
2 CONT
2 CONT The children of Philip IV and Jeanne of Navarre were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Marguerite (1288–c.1294) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis X - (October, 1289–June 5, 1316) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanche (died c.1294) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip V - (1292/93–January 3, 1322) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles IV - (1294–February 1, 1328) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabelle - (c. 1295–August 23, 1358) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert (born 1297, died 1308 at St-Germaine-en-Laye) 
2 CONT
2 CONT All three of his sons reaching adulthood would become kings of France, and his surviving daughter, as consort of Edward II, was queen of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY: b. 1268, Fontainebleau, Fr.
2 CONT
2 CONT d. Nov. 29, 1314, Fontainebleau 
2 CONT
2 CONT byname PHILIP THE FAIR, French PHILIPPE LE BEL, king of France from 1285 to 1314 (and of Navarre, as Philip I, from 1284 to 1305, ruling jointly with his wife, Joan I of Navarre). His long struggle with the Roman papacy ended with
2 CONC  the transfer of the Curia to Avignon, Fr. (beginning the so-called Babylonian Captivity, 1309-78). He also secured French royal power by wars on barons and neighbours and by restriction of feudal usages. His three sons were succe
2 CONC ssively kings of France: Louis X, Philip V, and Charles IV. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Early years.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born at Fontainebleau while his grandfather was still ruling, Philip, the second son of Philip III the Bold and grandson of St. Louis (Louis IX), was not yet three when his mother, Isabella of Aragon, died on her return from the c
2 CONC rusade on which Louis IX had perished. The motherless Philip and his three brothers saw little of their father, who, stricken by Isabella's death, threw himself into campaigning and administrative affairs. His troubled childhood a
2 CONC nd the series of blows he suffered explain in some measure the conflicting elements in his adult personality. In 1274 his father married Marie de Brabant, a beautiful and cultivated woman, and, with her arrival at court, intrigue 
2 CONC began to flourish. In the same year, the two-year-old Joan, heiress of Champagne and Navarre, was welcomed as a refugee. Reared with the royal children, she would, when she was 12, become the bride of Philip the Fair.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1276 Philip's older brother, Louis, died, and the shock of this event, which suddenly made Philip heir of the kingdom, was compounded by persistent rumours of poisoning and suspicions that Philip's stepmother intended to see Is
2 CONC abella's remaining sons destroyed. Vague allegations were circulated that Louis's death was linked with certain unspecified "unnatural acts" of his father. These rumours, never satisfactorily put to rest, together with the unexpec
2 CONC ted change in Philip's fortunes, apparently served to arouse in him feelings of insecurity and mistrust.
2 CONT
2 CONT Consequently, Philip turned elsewhere in search of a model for his own conduct. He found it in Louis IX, whose memory was increasingly venerated as the number of miracles attributed to him mounted. Reports of Louis's exacting stan
2 CONC dards of rulership and his saintly virtues were reinforced by the precepts of the religious advisers who surrounded the adolescent Philip. A more self-confident person might have been able to discriminate realistically among the s
2 CONC ometimes artificially exaggerated stories and the utopian ideals. Philip, however, became convinced that it was his God-given duty to attain the lofty goals of his grandfather.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Philip was 16, he was knighted and married to Joan of Navarre. In 1285 he accompanied his father to the south on a campaign to install Philip's brother Charles on the throne of Aragon. He had no sympathy with the enterprise, 
2 CONC however, which was backed by his stepmother and aimed against the King of Aragon, his late mother's brother. When his father died in October 1285, Philip immediately abandoned the venture.
2 CONT
2 CONT Conflict with the papacy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip's rupture with Boniface VIII can be conside
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Philip IV, called the Fair, was King of France from 1285 until his death. He was the husband of Joan I of Navarre, by virtue of which he was, as Philip I, King of Navarre and Count of Champagne from 1284 to 1305.
2 CONT
