1 NOTE _P_CCINFO 1-7369
1 NOTE Philippa`s tomb in Westminster Abbey cost her husband, Edward III, 300 pounds, making the monument one of the most expensive in the Abbey.
1 NOTE No. 546629 George Washington Ahnentafel !SOURCE: International Society of the Descendants of Charlemagne, Inc.
2 CONT
2 CONT No. 546630 George Washington Ahnentafel !SOURCE: International Society of the Descendants of Charlemagne, Inc.
2 CONT
2 CONT No. 546631 George Washington Ahnentafel !SOURCE: International Society of the Descendants of Charlemagne, Inc.
1 NOTE Philippa of Hainault was first considered as a possible bride for the future Edward III when she was only eight.  Bishop Stapeldon of Exeter was sent to inspect her on behalf of Edward's parents and reported back in minute detail:
2 CONT The lady...has not uncomely hair, betwixt
2 CONT  blue-black and brown.  Her head is clean-shaped;
2 CONT  her forehead high and broad, and standing somewhat  forward.  Her face narrows between the eyes, and the
2 CONT  lower part of her face is still more narrow and slender
2 CONT  than the forehead.  Her eyes are blackish -brown and
2 CONT  deep.  Her nose is fairly smooth and even, save that
2 CONT  it is somewhat broad at the tip and flattened, yet it is
2 CONT  no snub-nose.  Her nostrils are also broad, her mouth
2 CONT  fairly wide.  Her teeth which have fallen and grown
2 CONT  again are white enough, but the rest are not so white.
2 CONT  The lower teeth project a little beyond the upper; yet
2 CONT  this is but little seen.  Her ears and chin are comely  enough.  Her neck, shoulders, and all her body and  lower limbs are reasonably well shapen; all her limbs
2 CONT  are well set and un maimed; and nought is amiss so far
2 CONT  as a man may see.  Moreover, she is of brown skin all
2 CONT  over, and much like her father; and in all things she is  pleasant enough, as it seems to us.The bishop goes on to add that she was 'neither too tall nor too short' for her age, and that she was 'offair carriage, and well taught 
2 CONC in all that becometh her rank.'
2 CONT Edward was able to check the truth this report for himself when he and his mother were guests at the Court of Hainault in 1326.  Philippa was then fifteen and Edward fourteen.  Evidently he liked what he found and after a papal di
2 CONC spensation for the marriage ( the couple being second cousins) had been sought and obtained from Avignon in September 1327, Philippa set out for England arriving in London two days before Christmas.  Edward was in the north with h
2 CONC is army and Philippa, with a large suite in attendance, rode to meet him.  The marriage took place in York Minster on 24 January 1328.  They dallied in York until after Easter, then moved south to the royal manor of Woodstock, whi
2 CONC ch was to become Philippa's favorite residence and the birthplace of four of her twelve children.  The Queen's coronation at Westminster was delayed until March 1330, only three months before Philippa gave birth to her first child
2 CONC , the Black Prince.  She chose to feed the baby herself going against the current fashion for wet-nurses.
2 CONT Like other medieval queens, Philippa accompanied her husband on his campaigns and the birthplaces of her children testify that she was not to be deterred by pregnancy.  She had a kindly nature and was able to restrain her husband 
2 CONC and eldest son on many occasions when their tempers got the better of them.  She interceded for the lives of the carpenters whose stand collapsed at the great tournamentin Cheapside held to celebrate the birth of the Black Prince 
2 CONC and later, in an episode celebrated as a subject for pictures and statues, pleaded successfully for the lives of the six burghers of Calais who came to surrender the town to Edward III.
2 CONT The Queen's great amiability gained her a popularity not enjoyed by any of her predecessors.  Her homely features and comfortable motherly figure are well apparent in Maste Hennequin of Liege's fine alabaster effigy on her tomb in
2 CONC  Westminster Abbey.  A lively carving of the younger Philippa, wearing an elaborate crown, is to be seen on one of the misericordes in the Chapel of the Royal Foundationof St Katherine in east London.
2 CONT Philippa died in her late fifties of 'a dropsical malady' which had afflicted her for about two years.  Froissart describes her deathbed, with her hand in that of the King, and her youngest son Thomas, a boy of fourteen, standing 
2 CONC by her bed.
2 CONT Queen's College, Oxford was founded in Philippa's honor by her chaplain Robert d'Eglesfield and established by Royal Charter in 1341.
2 CONT Agnes Strickland, ever one to point a moral, ends her life of Philippa with this paragraph:
2 CONT The close observer of history will not fail to notice
2 CONT that with the life of Queen Philippa the happiness,
2 CONT the good fortune, and even the respectability of
2 CONT Edward III and his family departed; and scenes of
2 CONT strife, sorrow and folly distracted the court where she
2 CONT had once promoted virtue, and presided with
2 CONT well-regulated magnificence.
1 NOTE Source:
2 CONT Stuart Roderick, W.
2 CONT Royalty for Commoners, 3rd Edit. Published, Genealogical Publishing Co, Inc. Baltomore, MD. 1998,
2 CONT ISBN-0-8063-1561-X Text 324-40
2 CONT Source II
2 CONT Alison Weir, Britains Royal Family A Complete Genealogy 1999, ppg 41-44
