2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Guy IV, Count of Saint-Pol
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Guy III of Châtillon, Count of Saint Pol (1277 – April 6, 1317), French nobleman, was the son of Guy II of Châtillon and Matilda of Brabant.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1292, he married Marie of Brittany, daughter of John II, Duke of Brittany and Beatrice of England. They had eight children:
2 CONT
2 CONT John of Châtillon (d. 1344), Count of Saint Pol
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacques of Châtillon (d.s.p. 1365), Lord of Ancre
2 CONT
2 CONT Mahaut of Châtillon (1293–1358), married Charles of Valois
2 CONT
2 CONT Beatrix of Châtillon, married in 1315 Jean de Dampierre, Lord of Crevecoeur
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabeau of Châtillon (d. May 19, 1360), married in May 1311 Guillaume I de Coucy, Lord of Coucy
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie of Châtillon, married Aymer de Valence, 2nd Earl of Pembroke
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleanor of Châtillon, married Jean III Malet, Lord of Granville
2 CONT
2 CONT Jeanne of Châtillon, married Miles de Noyers, Lord of Maisy
2 CONT
2 CONT He held the office of Grand Bouteiller of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was placed in joint command (with Robert VI of Auvergne) of one of the two reserve "battles" of the French troops at the Battle of the Golden Spurs. He was able to escape when the French were routed by the Flemings, but his bro
2 CONC ther Jacques and many of his relatives were killed.
