1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Robert II of Artois
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert II (September 1250 – 11 July 1302) was the Count of Artois, the posthumous son and heir of Robert I and Matilda of Brabant.
2 CONT
2 CONT An experienced soldier, he took part in the Aragonese Crusade in 1284 and defeated the Flemings in 1297 at the Battle of Furnes. He was again sent into Flanders in July 1302, where he began to ravage the countryside and attempted 
2 CONC to take the town of Kortrijk. He then met the Flemish army at the Battle of the Golden Spurs. His infantry advanced with great success against the Flemings (mostly city militia), but he ordered their recall to allow his cavalry to
2 CONC  make the final, victorious charge. But on the broken, marshy ground, his knights were unable to gain enough momentum to break the Flemish shieldwall, and they were knocked down and slaughtered. Robert led some of the reserves in 
2 CONC a second charge in an attempt to reverse their fortunes, but was cut down by the Flemish infantry.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1262 in Paris Robert married Amicie de Courtenay (1250–1275), by whom he had three children: Mahaut (1268–1329), Philip (1269–1298), and Robert (born 1271, died young). After Amicie's death, Robert married twice more: first, in
2 CONC  1277, to Agnes of Dampierre (1237–1288), heiress of Bourbon, and then, on 18 October 1298 to Margaret (died 1342), daughter of John II, Count of Hainaut. After Robert's death, his daughter Mahaut inherited Artois, but his grandso
2 CONC n Robert III unsuccessfully tried to claim it.
