1 OCCU Ridder, heer van Arkel, Noordeloos, Bergenambacht, Dalem, Heukelom, Hoog Blokland, Slingelandt, Walwijk et Willige Langerak ; "Leenheer" (seigneur-vassal from Count Willem II of Holland, heer van arkel, Ridder
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://nl.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jan_I_van_Arkel_(1233-1272)
2 CONT Jan Herbaren I van Arkel (c. 1233 – 15 May 1272) was lord of Arkel from 1253 until his death. Other possessions were Noordeloos, Bergenambacht, Heukelom, Hoog Blokland, Slingelandt, Stolwijk and Willige Langerak.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lifespan
2 CONT He was a son of Herbaren II van der Lede, founder of the lordship of Arkel.
2 CONT
2 CONT John is mentioned for the first time in a Latin chronicle from 1253, in which he is mentioned as Johannes miles dominus de Arkele (John, knight, Lord of Arkel). After that, he is mentioned several times in deeds about loans. In 12
2 CONC 53 he and his brother Herbaren appear in a deed as a witness for Jan I van der Lede. On 25 June 1254, Jan witnessed an alliance between Jan van der Lede and Hugo van Arkel to grant Floris van Dalem the possession of Dalem as a fie
2 CONC f. [1] He took part in the wars with the rebellious West Frisians, led by his liege lord William II of Holland. Around 1260 he was granted the fief of den Berghe (present-day Bergambacht) by the County of Holland, which he gave on
2 CONC  loan to his brother Herbaren. On 29 October 1263, Jan bequeathed a certain Otto to Slingelandt. On 23 August 1264, together with Willem van Brederode, he granted Hendrik van Alblas the right to dig a moat or canal (Jan is then me
2 CONC ntioned for the last time in a document). [2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Jan I was nicknamed The Strong, he is said to have pulled himself up with his horse at the gate of Gorinchem as a joke. In 1267 he started the construction of the castle of Gorinchem.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and children
2 CONT Jan had four children:
2 CONT
2 CONT =John II=
2 CONT =Arnoud, lord of Noordeloos=
2 CONT =Margretha (1312 †), married Hubrecht van Beusichem, lord of Culemborg=
2 CONT =IJsbrand or Isebrant, mentioned in 1303 as a canon in Cambrai[3]=
2 CONT =Jan van Arkel and his wife were buried in a tomb in the church of Gorinchem. In 1604 the bones were removed and buried elsewhere.=
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
