2 CONT
2 CONT Personal data Jan I “de Sterke” "Lord of Arkel" van Arkel heer van den Berghe 
2 CONT Nickname is Lord of Arkel.
2 CONT He was born about 1218Arkel
2 CONT Zuid Holland Nederland.
2 CONT Profession: Heer van Arkel, ridder, heer van Arkel, Noordeloos, Bergenambacht, Dalem, Heukelom, Hoog Blokland, Slingelandt, Walwijk et Willige Langerak ; "Leenheer" (seigneur-vassal from Count Willem II of Holland, heer van arkel,
2 CONC  Ridder.
2 CONT =He died on May 15, 1272Arkel=
2 CONT =Zuid Holland Nederland.=
2 CONT =He is buried on May 29, 1272 in DomkerkZuid Holland Nederland.=
2 CONT A child of Herbaren ll Florisz. van der Lede and Aleidis van Heusden
2 CONT This information was last updated on December 30, 2021.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Noble Ancestry of Van Arkel-Van Grevenbroek by Martin W.J. de Bruijn''' 
2 CONT To be quoted as: M.W.J. de Bruijn, 'Noble ancestry Van Arkel-van Grevenbroek' (www.broerendebruijn.nl/ArkelGrevenbroek.html, version of [date], consulted on [date]). Adellijk voorgeslacht Van Arkel-Van Grevenbroek door Martin W.J.
2 CONC  de Bruijn
2 CONT
2 CONT On 2 August 1739, my paternal ancestor Adriaan Janszoon de Bont (quarter no. 140) married Pieternel Dirksdochter van Grevenbroek (no. 141) in Tilburg. Perhaps without her knowing it herself, this farmer's daughter descended from a
2 CONC ncestors who not only derived their name from a manor Grevenbroek (near Hamont in Belgium, near the Dutch border), but who were actually lords of Grevenbroek and in turn descended from the noble family Van Arkel from the middle Du
2 CONC tch river area. It is not exceptional that people of modest origin, to which I count myself, have noble ancestors. If there is no loss of quarter (inbreeding), everyone has 128 ancestors after eight generations, 256 after nine gen
2 CONC erations, 512 after ten and so on. It is exceptional when there are no noble ancestors among them. The problem is often to find a connection with that in your genealogy. The advantage of such ancestors is that a lot of data from a
2 CONC nd about them are usually preserved. And that is often not the case with people with modest origins, although, for example, through well-preserved judicial archives, the history of Brabant farmers can often be followed back to the
2 CONC  Middle Ages (see, for example, the ► Lineage Series Van Heyst-de Bruijn and the ►Stamreeks Leijten-Mutsaers on this internet presentation). With this they are considerably better archived than many 'modern' Dutch nobility (just l
2 CONC ook at the so-called Red Book, the Netherlands' Adelboek). I want to pay attention here in a general sense to some aspects concerning my noble ancestors in the male line from the aforementioned Pieternel van Grevenbroek - 'in a ge
2 CONC neral sense' because it is only an overview and 'some aspects' because it is impossible to be complete. I have only been able to derive a number of data from what has now appeared on the internet without the origin being indicated
2 CONC . That also indicates a certain limitation. Of course, as always, I keep myself recommended for every supplement and / or improvement (mwjdebruijn@casema.nl). 
2 CONT
2 CONT To begin with, I would like to pay attention to a source that is not only of great importance for the data on the Van Grevenbroek family, but also has a curious tradition, which indicates how many coincidences such a tradition som
2 CONC etimes depends on. This concerns a cartularium, a register containing copies of certificates (cartae) that relate to a certain institution, problems, families or persons. In this case it concerns a copy of a copy that is now house
2 CONC d in the archive of the lordship of Mierlo in the Brabants Historisch Informatie Centrum, the former State Archives in North Brabant (BHIC, Heerlijkheid Mierlo 1a). The surviving copy dates from 1886 (BHIC, Aanw. 1886/15) and is w
2 CONC ritten in a not very beautiful, not to say ugly and not very accurate handwriting. It was in turn written off from a cartularium that was made in 1633 by the Mierlose notary Dirk Snoecx on behalf of the then lord of Mierlo, Johan 
2 CONC van Grevenbroeck. This cartularium was owned in 1881 by the mayor of Hoensbroek, E. Slangen. Don't ask how he got it and how he lost it again. In any case, in 1933 it was in the possession of a high clergyman, Mgr. Dr. Petrus Jose
2 CONC phus Maria van Gils, who would then have ceded it to the University Library of Nijmegen, where it would have been lost by fire in 1944. Whatever the case may be, we will have to make do with the ugly nineteenth-century copy for th
2 CONC e time being and probably definitively. But we can be glad that the texts have at least been more or less preserved (for some of them see the appendices below). [Literature: Charter book of Noord-Brabant I, ed. H.P.H. Camps (The H
2 CONC ague 1979) 266; A.M. Frenken, 'Altar foundations in the church and the castle chapel of Mierlo', Bossche Bijdragen 12 (1933-1934) 34-40.] The cartularium contains certificates about Mierlo from the thirteenth century and about the
2 CONC  Van Grevenbroek family from the fourteenth. Together with many other sources, they make it possible to compile an uninterrupted lineage that leads to the noble family Van Arkel, which in the past was considered important enough t
2 CONC o attribute mythological ancestors to it. Jan I van Arkel received the lordship on loan from the Count of Holland in 1253. Jan's son Jan van Arkel II also received Bergambacht (den Berghe) on loan in 1260. The Van Arkels in turn d
2 CONC escended from The Lords Van der Lede, named after the river Lede (Leerdam = Lederdamme). 
2 CONT
2 CONT VAN ARKEL - First I will tell you how a Van Arkel came into possession of the glory Grevenbroek, after which he and his descendants started calling themselves Van Grevenbroek. Jan I van Arkel (in my pedigree generation XXII, no. 2
2 CONC .310.144) was buried on 15 May 1272 in Gorinchem in the Grote Kerk (see the image of his tomb). His successor Jan II (generation XXI, no. 1.155.072) bought this settlement of farmers and fishermen near Arkel on the Merwede and the
2 CONC  Linge from the Count of Bentheim, a relative of the Dutch count, and built a castle there. After the construction of ramparts, Gorinchem would henceforth be the wonderful city of the Van Arkels. Jan III van Arkel (generation XX, 
2 CONC no. 577.536), died in 1324, had a son Robbrecht (generation XIX, no. 288.768), born around 1318, married circa 1346 to Aleid van Asperen, but shortly afterwards died far from home in a battle near Liège (Hanut and Walef). He fough
2 CONC t there on the side of his brother Jan, bishop of Utrecht, against the people of Liège. In the Sticht Utrecht, the lordship of the bishop, he fulfilled important functions, including that of marshal of the Nedersticht (approximate
2 CONC ly the present province of Utrecht). Because of his brother the bishop, he was also beleend here with the lordship of Renswoude.  In 1346 Robbrecht and his brother had bought the northern part of its 'common' (common) land from th
2 CONC e city of Rhenen. This area was called Renswoude in 1363, 'the forest of Rhenen'. Between the city and the Rhenense forest there was still a large unexplored peatland, from which later, after the reclamation, the village of Veenen
2 CONC daal arose.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT https://translate.google.ca/translate?hl=en&sl=nl&u=https://nl.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jan_I_van_Arkel_(13e_eeuw)&prev=search
2 CONT
2 CONT John I of Arkel (13th century) 
2 CONT
2 CONT   Jan I  
2 CONT  1233 - 1272   Grote Kerk Gorinchem (17) .JPG  
2 CONT  Mr van Arkel  
2 CONT  Period   1253 - 1272  
2 CONT  Predecessor   Herbaren II van der Lede  
2 CONT  Successor   Jan II van Arkel  
2 CONT
2 CONT  Father   Herbaren II van der Lede  
2 CONT  Mother   Aleid Heusden  
2 CONT
2 CONT  Jan Herbaren I Arkel, nicknamed Strong (ca. 1233 - Gorinchem, 15 May 1272 ) was Mr van Arkel from 1253 until his death.  Other possessions were Noordeloos bergambacht, Heukelom, Hoogblokland, Slingelandt, Stolwijk and Willige Lan
2 CONC gerak. 
2 CONT
2 CONT  Life [ edit ] 
2 CONT
2 CONT  He was a son of Herbaren II van der Lede , founder of the glory of Arkel. 
2 CONT
2 CONT  Jan is first mentioned in a Latin chronicle from 1253, in which he listed as John Miles Lavinius the Arkele (Jan, knight, Lord of Arkel).  Then he is still mentioned several times in acts of individuals creations.  In 1253 he joi
2 CONC ns his brother Herbaren for in a document as a witness for Jan I van der Lede .  On June 25, 1254, Jan witness to a covenant of Jan van der Lede and Hugo van Arkel to Floris van Dalem forgive hold Dalem as loan management.  [1] He
2 CONC  participated in the war with the rebellious West Frisians under the guidance of his liege William II of Holland .  Gets around 1260 the fief van den Berghe (contemporary Bergambacht ) assigned to the County of Holland , which he 
2 CONC gives in loan to his brother Herbaren.  On October 29, 1263 beleent Jan Otto with one Slingelandt.  On August 23, 1264, he confers with Willem van Brederode the right to Hendrik van Alblas to dig a moat or canal (Jan will be the l
2 CONC ast time mentioned in a document. [2] 
2 CONT
2 CONT  John I was nicknamed Hardy, he would have even erected a joke at the gate of Gorinchem with his horse.  In 1267 he began building the castle of Gorinchem. 
2 CONT
2 CONT  Marriage and children [ edit ] 
2 CONT
2 CONT  Jan had three children: 
2 CONT  John II 
2 CONT  Arnoud, lord of Noordeloos 
2 CONT  Margretha († 1312), married Hubrecht of Beusichem , Lord of Culemborg 
2 CONT  IJsbrand or Isebrant, named in 1303 as canon in Cambrai [3] 
2 CONT
2 CONT  Jan van Arkel was buried with his wife in a tomb in the Church of Gorinchem .  In 1604 the bones were removed and buried elsewhere. 
2 CONT
