1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Treslong, from the city of Den Briel, was just like Willem van der Marck active against the Spaniards. From several sources he appears to have a more respectable background than Van der Marck. Treslong was active i
2 CONC n the royal navy since 1558. In 1568 he served under Lodewijk van Nassau at the battles near Heiligerlee and Jemgum. Early on in 1572 he served with the "Watergeuzen". Earlier on, the geuzen were forced to give way to the governor
2 CONC -general Don Fernando Alvarez de Toledo (1507-1582) and were then leading the lives of privateers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Treslong commanded a ship that became stuck in ice when it was on it's way from Vlie to the island of Texel. His ship was then attacked by 4 companies of soldiers who had been warned by the citizens of the island Wieringen. He man
2 CONC aged to defeat them but lost his sword as well as seventeen crewmembers in the battle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Together with Willem van der Marck he led the the Watergeuzen when they conquered Den Briel on the April 1st 1572. This attack was not their original intention. The watergeuzen were on their way to the Isle of Texel to attack the 
2 CONC Spanish fleet they believed to be stationed there. After this they would try to establish one or more strongholds in one or more of the ports in the North-Western area of the Netherlands.
2 CONT
2 CONT En route they captured several Spanish merchant ships. When they passed the Flemish sands the wind turned and they decided to change their course to Den Briel. At first they merely intended to sack Den Briel, but Van Oranje ordere
2 CONC d it to be held against the Spaniards.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1573 Treslong became admiral of Holland and in 1576 also admiral of Zeeland. In 1585 he got into an argument with members of the Admiralty of Zeeland about the way in which the beleaguered Antwerpen was to be relieved. As a res
2 CONC ult he was imprisoned at the city of Middelburg and relieved of his office. Sometime later in 1591 he was released, because of the support of the Count of Leicester.
