2 CONT
2 CONT Béatrice de Provence, née en 1231, morte à Nocera le 23 septembre 1267, comtesse de Provence et de Forcalquier, fille de Raimond Bérenger IV, comte de Provence et de Forcalquier, et de Béatrice de Savoie.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elle épouse en 1246 Charles Ier (1226 † 1285), roi de Sicile, comte d'Anjou et du Maine, et eut :
2 CONT
2 CONT #Louis (1248 † 1248)
2 CONT
2 CONT #Blanche (1250 † 1269), mariée en 1265 avec Robert III de Dampierre (1249 † 1322), comte de Flandre
2 CONT
2 CONT #Béatrice (1252 † 1275), mariée en 1273 à Philippe Ier de Courtenay (1243 † 1283), empereur titulaire de Constantinople
2 CONT
2 CONT #Charles II de Naples (1254 † 1309), comte d'Anjou et du Maine, roi de Naples
2 CONT
2 CONT #Philippe (1256 † 1277), prince d'Achaïe, marié en 1271 avec Isabelle de Villehardouin (1263 † 1312), princesse d'Achaïe et de Morée
2 CONT
2 CONT #Robert (1258 † 1265)
2 CONT
2 CONT #Élisabeth (1261 † 1300), marié à Ladislas IV (1262 † 1290), roi de Hongrie
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Beatrice of Provence (1234–23 September 1267, Nocera) was the first wife and Queen of Charles I of Sicily.
2 CONT
2 CONT The youngest daughter of Raymond Berenguer IV of Provence and Beatrice of Savoy, Beatrice was married in 1246 to Charles of France, Count of Anjou and Maine, the youngest brother of King Louis IX of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Raymond Berenguer, the Count of Provence and Forcalquier, had four daughters, but no sons. Of his daughters, apart from Beatrice, the eldest, Marguerite, had married King Louis and was Queen of France; the second, Eleanor, had mar
2 CONC ried King Henry III of England, and the third, Sanchia, had married the wealthy Richard, Earl of Cornwall, brother of King Henry. Louis had originally been married to Marguerite by his mother, Blanche of Castile, in the belief tha
2 CONC t he would inherit Provence and Forcalquier; however, Raymond Berenguer decided to bequeath them instead to Charles, whom he viewed as having the least prospects.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles and Beatrice had the following children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis (1248-1248) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanche (1250-1269), married in 1265 Robert III of Flanders (1249-1322) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Beatrice (1252-1275), married in 1273 Philip of Courtenay (1243-1283), titular emperor of Constantinople 
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles II of Naples (1254-1309), Count of Anjou and of Provence, King of Naples 
2 CONT
2 CONT Philippe (1256-1277), prince of Achaïea, married in 1271 Isabella of Villehardouin (1263-1312), princess of Achaïea and Morea 
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert (1258-1265) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Élisabeth (1261-1300), married to Ladislaus IV of Hungary (1262-1290) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Beatrice, like her sister Sanchia, would eventually become a Queen before her death: Sancha became 'Queen of the Romans' after the Earl of Cornwall was elected as King; Beatrice became Queen consort of Sicily, after that realm was
2 CONC  bestowed upon Charles by the Pope.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Beatrice of Provence (c.1234–23 September 1267, Nocera Inferiore) was Queen consort of Sicily as the first wife of King Charles I of Sicily.
2 CONT
2 CONT The youngest daughter of Raymond Berenguer IV of Provence and Beatrice of Savoy, Beatrice was married on 31 January 1246 to Charles of France, Count of Anjou and Maine, the youngest brother of King Louis IX of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents [hide]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 Family
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT 3 Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT 4 Death
2 CONT
2 CONT 5 Ancestry
2 CONT
2 CONT 6 Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Raymond Berenguer, the Count of Provence and Forcalquier, had four daughters, but no sons. His eldest daughter, Margaret, had married King Louis IX of France and was Queen consort of France; his second daughter, Eleanor, had marri
2 CONC ed King Henry III of England, and his third, Sanchia, had married the wealthy Richard, Earl of Cornwall, brother of King Henry. King Louis IX's marriage to Marguerite had been arranged by his mother, Blanche of Castile, with the h
2 CONC opes that he would inherit Provence and Forcalquier when Berenguer died. Berenguer, however, left everything to Beatrice. James I of Aragon, in the hopes of uniting Provence and Toulouse, had planned to marry Beatrice but when her
2 CONC  father died the French court intervened, by getting the Pope to refuse the marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT When Berenguer had died on 19 August 1245, Beatrice became one of the most attractive heiresses in medieval Europe. Various suitors had tried to seize her, so her mother, Beatrice of Savoy, placed the younger Beatrice in a safe fo
2 CONC rtress, secured the trust of its people then went to the Pope for his protection. In Cluny during December 1245, a secret discussion, between Pope Innocent IV, Louis IX of France, his mother Blanche of Castile and his brother Char
2 CONC les of Anjou, took place. It was decided that in return for Louis IX supporting the Pope militarily, the Pope would allow Charles of Anjou, youngest brother to the French King, to marry Beatrice of Provence. Provence, however, was
2 CONC  to never go to France outright through Charles. It was agreed that if Charles and Beatrice had children, the county would go to them; if there was no issue, then the county would go to Sanchia of Provence. If Sanchia died without
2 CONC  an heir, Provence would go to the King of Aragon.
2 CONT
2 CONT Beatrice of Savoy who had been granted the usufruct of the county for her lifetime according to her husband's will, agreed to the marriage between Charles of Anjou and her youngest daughter, Beatrice. Charles, along with Philip of
2 CONC  Savoy and five hundred knights, rode from Lyon to Provence. On their way, they ran into Raymond VII Count of Toulouse, who also had an army on the way to Provence. However, Raymond of Toulouse had been deceived by knights in favo
2 CONC ur of Charles and for that reason he had brought fewer men and Charles and his army were quicker. When Charles got to Aix-en-Provence, James I of Aragon, who had been there all along but was not allowed to see Beatrice, had his so
2 CONC ldiers surrounding the castle in which the young Beatrice and her mother were. There was a brief struggle, but the King of Aragon retreated with dignity. To the young Beatrice, Charles, who was described as "an admirable young man
2 CONC ", was a satisfactory resolution to her problems. The marriage took place in Aix-en-Provence. They had soldiers on guard and the bride was escorted down the aisle by her uncle, Thomas of Savoy.
2 CONT
2 CONT As soon as Charles became Count of Provence, he brought in his own team of French lawyers and accountants. He excluded his mother in law from the running of the county and began taking castles, power and fees away from the nobles 
2 CONC who had previously enjoyed a certain degree of independence in the running of their cities. Charles made himself very unpopular. Beatrice of Savoy moved herself to Forcalquier in protest and in Marseille, Charles's officials were 
2 CONC thrown out of the city. In the family conflict the younger Beatrice sided with her husband.
2 CONT
2 CONT In May 1247, Charles and Beatrice were recorded as being in Melun where Charles was knighted by his brother Louis. Beatrice accompanied Charles on the seventh crusade in 1248. Led by Louis IX, the crusaders made an extended proces
2 CONC sion through France. Before they left, Charles and Beatrice met with her mother in Beaucaire to try to come to some terms of agreement concerning Provence. Whilst the more important matters were left until Charles and Beatrice ret
2 CONC urned, it was decided that Beatrice of Savoy would give up the rights to 'the castle at Aix in exchange for a percentage of the county's revenue.'
2 CONT
2 CONT Beatrice gave birth to her first child in Nicosia, 'a very elegant and wellformed son', which Robert of Artois wrote home to his mother, Blanche of Castile, about. Beatrice stayed with her sister Margaret in Damietta, when they lo
2 CONC st contact with the King and his army, both women gave birth whilst in Damietta. Later in 1250, they were reunited with the rest of the crusade at Acre, were the King's ransom was paid. Charles and Beatrice, along with several oth
2 CONC er nobles, left soon after. They journeyed to the court of Emperor Frederick II, to ask him to send the King of France more men for his crusade. However, the Emperor, who had been excommunicated, needed his army to fight the Pope,
2 CONC  and refused. Charles and Beatrice were then forced to go Lyon to meet with the Pope.
2 CONT
2 CONT By the time they returned to Provence in 1251, open rebellion had broken out, spurred on by Beatrice's mother, who felt Charles had failed to respect her claims in Provence. However, by July 1252 Charles had managed to defeat the 
2 CONC revolt and was in the process of exercising his power as Count of Provence, when in November of the same year, Blanche of Castile died, and Charles and Beatrice had to go Paris, where Charles co-ruled France with his brother, Alph
2 CONC onse. The Pope offered Charles the Kingdom of Sicily in 1252, but Charles had to turn the offer down, as he was preoccupied with other affairs and he also did not have sufficient funds.
2 CONT
2 CONT The crusade returned in 1254. Charles and Beatrice spent Christmas in Paris that year, where all of Beatrice's sisters and their mother were present, it was noted that the other four women treated the younger Beatrice coldly, due 
2 CONC to Raymond Berenguer's will.
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret, the Queen of France and Beatrice's sister, publicly offended her in 1259, by not seating her at the family table; she claimed because Beatrice was not a queen like her sisters, she could not sit with them. Margaret had h
2 CONC oped to provoke her sister in treacherous behaviour so she would have a valid reason to invade Provence. Beatrice "with great grief", went to Charles and he reportedly told her " Be at peace, for I will shortly make thee a greater
2 CONC  Queen than them".
2 CONT
2 CONT When the new Pope granted Charles the Kingdom of Sicily, all he had to do was defeat Manfred of Sicily. Another contender to win the throne of Sicily was Beatrice's nephew, Edmund Crouchback, but it soon became clear that Charles 
2 CONC was the more promising candidate. In order to achieve his goal, Charles needed an army and Beatrice helped her husband raise one. She called on all her knights as well as the young men of France, and she pledged all her jewels, to
2 CONC  make sure they joined her husbands army.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Beatrice, to aid him (Charles) in the gratification of her ambition, sold all her jewels and personal ornaments, and expended her private treasure in collecting round her standard, not only her own vassals, but the chivalric yout
2 CONC h of France , who were attracted to her service not less by her personal solicitations than by her rich gifts." Angelo da Constanzo
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles went first to Rome, and Beatrice followed with the remaining army through the treacherous Alpine passes, during Autumn. It took them nearly six weeks to reach Rome, but once both Charles and Beatrice were in Rome, they wer
2 CONC e crowned King and Queen consort of Sicily, on 6 January 1266 .As soon as the coronation festivities had ended, Beatrice stayed in Rome with a small force to hold the city, whilst Charles rode out to the battle of Benevento. After
2 CONC  her husbands victory, she chose the castle of Melfi as their residence.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles and Beatrice had the following children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis (1248 - 1248 Nicosia).
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanche (1250 - July 1269), married in 1265 Robert III of Flanders (1249-1322), by whom she had a son, Charles, who died young.
2 CONT
2 CONT Beatrice (1252 - 1275), married in 1273 Philip of Courtenay (1243-1283), titular emperor of Constantinople, by whom she had one daughter, Catherine I of Courtenay, titular Empress of Constantinople.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles II of Naples (1254 - 1309), Count of Anjou and Provence, King of Naples, married Maria Arpad of Hungary, by whom he had issue. English Queen consort Philippa of Hainaut descended from their daughter, Marguerite of Anjou an
2 CONC d Maine.
2 CONT
2 CONT Philippe (1256 - 1 January 1277), titular King of Thessalonica from 1274 and Prince of Achaïea, married in 1271 Isabella of Villehardouin (1263-1312), Princess of Achaïea and Morea
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert (1258 - 1265).
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth (1261-1300), married to Ladislaus IV of Hungary (1262-1290). Their marriage was childless.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Death
2 CONT
2 CONT Beatrice died in 1267, a little over a year after becoming Queen. The cause of her death was not recorded. She was initially buried at Nocera, but Charles later moved her body to Aix-en-Provence were she was laid to rest with her 
2 CONC father.
2 CONT
2 CONT Beatrice, like her sisters, mother and grandmother was known for her beauty. A description of Beatrice said she "set men's hearts thumping and the fingers of troubadours to fevered twanging of lyres. Two of the balladists at the P
2 CONC rovencal court were temporarily deprived of reason for love of the entrancing Beatrice", and like her sister Sanchia, she would eventually become a Queen before her death: Sanchia became 'Queen of the Romans' after the Earl of Cor
2 CONC nwall was elected as King; Beatrice became Queen consort of Sicily, after that realm was bestowed upon Charles by the Pope.
2 CONT
2 CONT Preceded by
2 CONT
2 CONT Helena Angelina Doukaina Queen Consort of Sicily
2 CONT
2 CONT 26 February 1266–23 September 1267 Succeeded by
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret of Burgundy 
2 CONT
