2 CONT
2 CONT 1270-72 magyar kiraly
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Stephen V (Hungarian: V. István, Croatian: Stjepan VI., Slovak: Štefan V) (before 18 October 1239, Buda, Hungary – 6 August 1272, Csepel Island, Hungary), King of Hungary from 1270 to 1272.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early years
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the elder son of King Béla IV of Hungary and his queen, Maria Laskarina, a daughter of the Emperor Theodore I Lascaris of Nicaea.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the second year following his birth, on 11 April 1241, the Mongolian troops defeated his father's army in the Battle of Mohi. After the disastrous battle, the royal family had to escape to Trau, a well-fortified city in Dalmati
2 CONC a. They could only return to Hungary after the unexpected withdrawal of the Mongol forces from Europe..
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Junior King of Hungary
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1246 Stephen was crowned as junior King and his father entrusted him with the government of Croatia, Slavonia and Dalmatia, but the three provinces were de facto governed by the Ban Stephen Gut-Keled. Stephen's father, attempti
2 CONC ng to bind the powerful but pagan Cuman tribes more closely to the dynasty, arranged for Stephen's marriage, as a youth (about 1253), to Elizabeth, the daughter of a Cuman chieftain Köten.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1257, Stephen demanded that his father divide the kingdom between themselves and recruited an army against the senior king. Finally, in 1258, King Béla IV was obliged to cede to him the government of Transylvania.
2 CONT
2 CONT Duke of Styria
2 CONT
2 CONT Stephen took part in his father's military campaign against the Styrians, who had rebelled against the rule of the King of Hungary, in 1258. After the successful campaign, King Béla IV appointed him to Duke of Styria.
2 CONT
2 CONT His government, however, was unpopular among his new subjects, who rebelled against him with the support of King Otakar II of Bohemia. Stephen and his father started an attack against Otakar II's lands, but their troops were defea
2 CONC ted on 12 July 1260 in the Battle of Kroissenbrunn. Following the battle, the two Kings of Hungary ceded the Duchy of Styria to the King of Bohemia in the Peace of Pressburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Struggles with his father
2 CONT
2 CONT Shortly after the peace, Stephen took over the government of Transylvania again. In 1261, Stephen and his father conducted a joint military campaign against Bulgaria, but their relationship became more and more tense, because the 
2 CONC senior king had been favouring his younger son, Duke Béla of Slavonia and his daughter, Anna, the mother-in-law of the King of Bohemia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Finally, with the mediation of Archbishops Fülöp of Esztergom and Smaragd of Kalocsa, Stephen and his father signed an agreement in the summer of 1262 in Pozsony. Based on their agreement, Stephen took over the government of the p
2 CONC arts of the kingdom East of the Danube. However, the two kings' reconciliation was only temporary, because their partisans were continuously inciting them against each other. In 1264, Stephen seized his mother's and sister's estat
2 CONC es in his domains, but his father sent troops against him. Stephen's wife and son were captured by his father's partisans, and he had to retreat to the castle of Feketehalom. However, he managed to repeal the siege and to commence
2 CONC  a counter-attack.
2 CONT
2 CONT In March 1265, he gained a strategic victory over his father's army in the Battle of Isaszeg. After his victory, he concluded a peace with King Béla IV. Based on the provisions of the peace, he received back the government of the 
2 CONC Eastern parts of the kingdom. On 23 March 1266, father and son confirmed the peace in the Convent of the Blessed Virgin on the Nyulak szigete ('Rabbits' Island'). Shortly afterwards, Stephen V led his army to Bulgaria and forced D
2 CONC espot Jakob Svetoslav of Vidin to accept his overlordship.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1267, the "prelates and nobles" of the Kingdom of Hungary held a joint assembly in Esztergom, and their decisions were confirmed by both Stephen and his father.
2 CONT
2 CONT To secure foreign support, he formed a double matrimonial alliance with the Angevins, chief partisans of the pope. The first of these was the marriage, in 1270, of his daughter Maria to the future King Charles II of Naples[2] The 
2 CONC second alliance was the marriage of Stephen's infant son, Ladislaus to Charles II's sister Elisabeth.
2 CONT
2 CONT King of Hungary
2 CONT
2 CONT After his father's death (3 May 1270), Stephen inherited the whole Kingdom of Hungary, although the deceased senior king had entrusted his daughter, Anna and his followers to King Otakar II of Bohemia in his last will, and they ha
2 CONC d escaped to Prague before Stephen arrived to Esztergom.
2 CONT
2 CONT Before his (second) coronation, Stephen granted the County of Esztergom to the Archbishop. In August 1270, Stephen had a meeting with his brother-in-law, Prince Bolesław V of Poland in Kraków where they concluded an alliance again
2 CONC st the King of Bohemia. Stephen also had a meeting with King Otakar II on 16 October on an island of the Danube near Pozsony where they concluded a truce for two years.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, following smaller skirmishes on the border, the war broke out soon after and the King of Bohemia lead his armies against Hungary. Stephen was defeated in two smaller battles, but finally won a decisive victory on 21 May 1
2 CONC 271 over the Czech and Austrian troops of Otakar II. In the subsequent peace the King of Bohemia handed back the fortresses occupied during his campaign, while Stephen renounced his claim to the Hungarian royal treasury that his s
2 CONC ister, Anna had brought to Prague after their father's death.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the summer of 1272, Stephen left for Dalmatia, where he wanted to meet King Charles I of Sicily, when he was informed that Joachim Gut-Keled had kidnapped his infant son, Ladislaus. Stephen was planning to raise an army to resc
2 CONC ue his infant son when he died suddenly.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Marriage and children
2 CONT
2 CONT # around 1253: Elisabeth (1240 – after 1290), daughter of a chieftain of the Cuman tribes settled down in Hungary
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Elisabeth (1255 – 1313/1326), wife firstly of Záviš of Falkenštejn and secondly of King Stefan Uroš II Milutin of Serbia
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Catherine (1255/1257 – after 1314), wife of King Stefan Dragutin of Serbia
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Maria (c. 1257 – 25 March 1325), wife of King Charles II of Naples
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Anna (c. 1260 – c. 1281), wife of the Emperor Andronikos II Palaiologos
2 CONT
2 CONT     * King Ladislaus IV (August 1262 – 10 July 1290)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Andrew, Duke of Slavonia (1268 – 1278)
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Stephen V (Hungarian: V. István, Croatian: Stjepan VI., Slovak: Štefan V) (before 18 October 1239, Buda, Hungary – 6 August 1272, Csepel Island, Hungary), King of Hungary and Croatia (1246-1272), and Duke of Styria (1258-1260).
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the elder son of King Béla IV of Hungary and his queen, Maria Laskarina, a daughter of the Emperor Theodore I Lascaris of Nicaea.
2 CONT
