1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_of_Artois,_Count_of_Eu
2 CONT
2 CONT John of Artois (29 September 1321 – 1387), called "sans Terre" (Lackland), was the son of Robert III of Artois and Jeanne of Valois. The confiscation of his father's goods for attempted fraud in 1331 had left him without an inheri
2 CONC tance.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1352, he was created Count of Eu, a title earlier forfeited at the execution of the previous holder, Raoul II of Brienne. He was badly wounded at the Battle of Poitiers on 19 September 1356, and was captured there by the Englis
2 CONC h. Enormously rich his ransom was sold to the Black Prince by his captor Élie de Pommiers for 30,000 old écus.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Isabeau of Melun (1328–1389), daughter of John I of Melun, Count of Tancarville, on 11 July 1352 and had the following issue:
2 CONT
2 CONT  Jeanne (1353–1420), married at the Château d'Eu on 12 July 1365 Simon de Thouars (d. 1365), Count of Dreux
2 CONT
2 CONT  John of Artois (1355–1363), Lord of Peronne
2 CONT
2 CONT  Robert IV of Artois, Count of Eu (1356–1387)
2 CONT
2 CONT  Philip of Artois, Count of Eu (1357–1397)
2 CONT
2 CONT  Charles (1359 – 15 April 1368)
2 CONT
2 CONT  Isabeau (1361 – 26 June 1379)
