2 CONT
2 CONT Death and legacy:
2 CONT
2 CONT During his second crusade, Louis died at Tunis, August 25, 1270, from what was traditionally believed to be bubonic plague but is thought by modern scholars to be dysentery. The local tradition of Sidi Bou Said claims that the fut
2 CONC ure Saint Louis did not die in 1270, but converted to Islam under the name of Sidi Bou Said, died at the end of the 13th century, and was buried as a saint of Islam in Djebel-Marsa.
2 CONT
2 CONT Christian tradition states that some of his entrails were buried directly on the spot in Tunisia, where a Tomb of Saint-Louis can still be visited today, whereas other parts of his entrails were sealed in an urn and placed in the 
2 CONC Basilica of Monreale, Palermo, where they still remain. His corpse was taken, after a short stay at the Basilica of Saint Dominic in Bologna, to the French royal necropolis at Saint-Denis, resting in Lyon on the way. His tomb at S
2 CONC aint-Denis was a magnificent gilt brass monument designed in the late 14th century. It was melted down during the French Wars of Religion, at which time the body of the king disappeared. Only one finger was rescued and is kept at 
2 CONC Saint-Denis. Pope Boniface VIII proclaimed the canonization of Louis in 1297; he is the only French monarch ever to be made a saint.
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis IX was succeeded by his son, Philippe III
2 CONT
2 CONT Veneration as Saint Louis: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis IX of France was revered as a saint and painted in portraiture well after his death (such portraits may not accurately reflect his appearance). This portrait was painted by El Greco ca 1592–95. 
2 CONT
2 CONT King of France, Confessor 
2 CONT
2 CONT Born 25 April 1214(1214-04-25)/1215, Poissy, France 
2 CONT
2 CONT Died 25 August 1270 (aged 56), Tunis in what is now Tunisia 
2 CONT
2 CONT Venerated in Roman Catholic Church 
2 CONT
2 CONT Canonized 1297 by Pope Boniface VIII 
2 CONT
2 CONT Feast 25 August 
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis IX is often considered the model of the ideal Christian monarch. Because of the aura of holiness attached to the memory of Louis IX, many Kings of France were called Louis, especially in the Bourbon dynasty (Louis XIII to Lo
2 CONC uis XVIII).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Congregation of the Sisters of Saint Louis is a Roman Catholic religious order founded in 1842 and named in his honour.
2 CONT
2 CONT Places named after Saint Louis:
2 CONT
2 CONT The cities of San Luis Potosí in Mexico, Saint Louis, Missouri, Saint-Louis du Sénégal in Senegal, Saint-Louis in Alsace, as well as Lake Saint-Louis in Quebec, and the Mission San Luis Rey de Francia in California are among the m
2 CONC any places named after the king.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Cathedral Saint-Louis in Versailles, Basilica of St Louis, King of France in St. Louis, Missouri, the Cathedral-Basilica of St. Louis in St. Louis, Missouri, and the French royal Order of Saint Louis (1693–1790 and 1814–30) we
2 CONC re also created after the king. The Saint Louis Cathedral in New Orleans is also named after the king.
2 CONT
2 CONT Many places in Brazil called São Luís in Portuguese are named after Saint Louis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sidi Bou Said in Tunisia is said to have been named for this very Catholic French king [3]. Tunisian legend tells the story of King Louis falling in love with a Berber princess, changing his name to Abou Said ibn Khalef ibn Yahia 
2 CONC Ettamini el Beji (nicknamed "Sidi Bou Said") for which a quaint town on the Tunisian coast is named. He became, according to this legend, an Islamic saint.
2 CONT
2 CONT Famous portraits:
2 CONT
2 CONT Coin of Saint Louis, Cabinet des Médailles.A portrait of St. Louis hangs in the chamber of the United States House of Representatives.
2 CONT
2 CONT Saint Louis is also portrayed on a frieze depicting a timeline of important lawgivers throughout world history in the Courtroom at the Supreme Court of the United States.
2 CONT
