2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert,_Count_of_Clermont
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert of France (1256 – 7 February 1317) was made Count of Clermont in 1268. He was son of King Louis IX of France and Margaret of Provence.[1]  In 1272, Robert married Beatrice of Burgundy, heiress of Bourbon[2]  and had the fol
2 CONC lowing issue:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Louis I, le Boiteux (1279–1342), first Duke of Bourbon
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Blanche (1281–1304), married in 1303 in Paris Robert VII, Count of Auvergne and Boulogne, grandmother of Joan I, Countess of Auvergne
2 CONT
2 CONT     * John (1283–1316), Baron of Charolais, married c. 1309 Jeanne d'Argies and had issue
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Mary (1285–1372, Paris), Prioress of Poissy
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Peter (1287 – aft. 1330), Archdeacon of Paris
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Margaret (1289–1309, Paris), married firstly in 1305 Raymond Berengar of Andria and secondly in 1308 John I, Marquis of Namur
2 CONT
2 CONT During his first joust, in 1279, Robert suffered head injuries which rendered him an invalid for the remainder of his life.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert is considered the founder of the House of Bourbon, a family which, with the passing of centuries came to govern as Kings of France (1589–1848), Kings of the Two Sicilies (1735–1860), dukes of Parma (1748–1796 and 1847–1859)
2 CONC , grand dukes of Luxembourg (1964–present) and Kings of Spain (1700–present).
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert is mentioned in the prologue of the Coutumes de Beauvaisis by Philippe de Beaumanoir[4].
2 CONT
2 CONT See also
2 CONT
