2 CONT King Jaime I & his second wife had ten children:
2 CONT
2 CONT >2.         Infanta doña VIOLANTE de Aragón ([1236]-Roncevalles 1301, bur Seville).  The Crónica de San Juan de la Peña names "la primera…Violant…la otra Costancia…et Isabel…la quarta…Maria" as the four daughters of King Jaime and
2 CONC  his second wife, stating that Violante married "Don Alfonso filio primogenitor del Rey de Castiella"[394].  The betrothal contract of “dominus Infans Alfonsus...regis Castellæ...primogenitus” and “domina Violante filia domini Jac
2 CONC obi...regis Aragonum” is dated 26 Nov 1246[395].  Pope Innocent IV issued a dispensation for the marriage of “rege Aragonum...Yola nata ipsius” and “Alphonsus primogenitus regis Castellæ”, related “quarta consanguinitatis linea”, 
2 CONC dated 23 Jan 1249[396].  Under the testament of "Jacobus…Rex Aragoniæ, Majoricarum et Valenciæ, Comes Barchinonæ et Urgelli, et Dominus Montispessulani", dated 26 Aug 1272, the king made bequests to "…filiam nostram Domnam Yolant…
2 CONC "[397].  After her eldest son died, she fled with his infant children to Aragon.  She died returning from a pilgrimage to Rome.  The Nobiliario of Pedro Conde de Barcelos records that "la Reina D. Violante" was buried "en Sevilla"
2 CONC  with her husband[398].  m (Betrothed 26 Nov 1246, Papal dispensation 23 Jan 1249, Valladolid 29 Jan 1249) Infante don ALFONSO de Castilla, son of FERNANDO III "el Santo" King of Castile & his first wife Elisabeth von Hohenstaufen
2 CONC  (Toledo 23 Nov 1221-Seville 4 Apr 1284, bur Seville, Cathedral Santa María).  He succeeded his father in 1252 as ALFONSO X “el Sabio” King of Castile and León. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >3.         Infante don PEDRO de Aragón (1239-Villafranca del Penedés 2 or 11 Nov 1285, bur Monasterio de Santa Cruz).  The Crónica de San Juan de la Peña names "Pedro, Jayme et Sancho" as the three sons of King Jaime and his seco
2 CONC nd wife[399].  Under the testament of "Jacobus…Rex Aragoniæ, Majoricarum et Valenciæ, Comes Barchinonæ et Urgelli, et Dominus Montispessulani", dated 26 Aug 1272, the king made bequests to "…filium nostrum primogenitum Infantem P…
2 CONC "[400].  He succeeded his father in 1276 as PEDRO III "el Grande" King of Aragon.   
2 CONT
2 CONT >4.         Infanta doña CONSTANZA de Aragón ([1240]-before 1266, bur Uclés).  The Crónica de San Juan de la Peña names "la primera…Violant…la otra Costancia…et Isabel…la quarta…Maria" as the four daughters of King Jaime and his s
2 CONC econd wife, stating that Constanza married "Don Manuel hermano del…Alfonso rey de Castiella"[401].  The Nobiliario of Pedro Conde de Barcelos records that "El Infante D. Manuel" married "D. Costança de Aragon"[402].  Under the tes
2 CONC tament of "Jacobus…Rex Aragoniæ, Majoricarum et Valenciæ, Comes Barchinonæ et Urgelli, et Dominus Montispessulani", dated 26 Aug 1272, the king made bequests to "…filios Infantis Domnæ Constantiæ quondam filiæ nostræ et Infantis d
2 CONC omini Emanuelis fratris…Regis Castellæ, nepotes nostros"[403].  m (Calatayud 1260) as his first wife, Infante don MANUEL de Castilla, Señor de Escalona, Peñafiel y Villena, son of FERNANDO III "el Santo" King of Castile & his firs
2 CONC t wife Elisabeth von Hohenstaufen (Carrión de los Condes 1234-Peñafiel 25 Dec 1283, bur Uclés, Santiago convent). 
2 CONT
2 CONT >5 Infante don JAIME de Aragón (Montpellier 31 May 1243-Palma de Mallorca 29 May 1311).  The Crónica de San Juan de la Peña names "Pedro, Jayme et Sancho" as the three sons of King Jaime and his second wife[404].  The Chronicle of
2 CONC  the Hôtel de Ville de Montpellier records the birth in 1243 "in vigilia Pentecosti" of "D. rex Jacobus et regina eius uxor...Jacobus filius" in Montpellier[405].  Under the projected partition of 1244, Jaime [Jaume] would receive
2 CONC  the Balearic Islands, Valencia, and Montpellier.  Under the further projected partition of 1262, Jaume would receive the Balearic Islands, Montpellier, Roussillon and Cerdaña.  Under the testament of "Jacobus…Rex Aragoniæ, Majori
2 CONC carum et Valenciæ, Comes Barchinonæ et Urgelli, et Dominus Montispessulani", dated 26 Aug 1272, the king made bequests to "filius noster Infans Jacobus…"[406].  He succeeded his father in 1276 as JAIME II King of Mallorca, Comte d
2 CONC e Roussillon et de Cerdagne/Cerdaña, Seigneur de Montpellier, Barón de Ompelas. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >6.         Infante don FERNANDO de Aragón ([1245]-1250).  The primary source which confirms his parentage has not yet been identified.  
2 CONT
2 CONT >7.         Infante don SANCHO de Aragón ([1246]-the Holy Land 1251).  The primary source which confirms his parentage has not yet been identified.  
