2 CONT Wikipedia, la enciclopedia libre
2 CONT Violante de Hungría, nacida alrededor de 1216 en Esztergom, Hungría y fallecida en Huesca en octubre de 1251, fue reina de Aragón tras casarse en 1235 con Jaime I de Aragón, el Conquistador, de quien fue la segunda esposa.
2 CONT
2 CONT Era hija del rey Andrés II de Hungría y de Violante de Courtenay. Su dote significaba una aportación de dinero y territorios, que nunca se hicieron efectivos.
2 CONT
2 CONT Impulsó de forma decisiva la conquista del reino de Valencia en 1238 y participó activamente en la política real. Se implicó sobre todo en las particiones de la herencia que quedarían a los hijos que tuvo con Jaime I, tratando de 
2 CONC enemistar al rey con Alfonso, hijo de su primer matrimonio con Leonor de Castilla.
2 CONT
2 CONT Violante y Jaime I tuvieron cuatro hijos y cinco hijas:
2 CONT #Fernando, muerto joven.
2 CONT #Sancho, arzobispo de Toledo.
2 CONT #Violante, que se casaría con Alfonso X el Sabio, rey de Castilla.
2 CONT #Constanza, que se casaría con Manuel de Portugal.
2 CONT #María, que se haría religiosa.
2 CONT #Sancha, que moriría en peregrinaje a Tierra Santa.
2 CONT #Isabel, que se casaría con Felipe III de Francia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sus restos y los de una hija, Sancha, yacen en el presbiterio del monasterio cisterciense de Santa María de Vallbona en Cataluña.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Violant of Hungary (Kingdom of Hungary, c. 1216 – October 1251 in Huesca) was Queen consort of James I of Aragon. She is also called Yolanda or Violante de Hungría in Spanish and Iolanda or Violant d'Hongria in Catalan .
2 CONT
2 CONT Violant was a daughter of Andrew II of Hungary and Violant of Courtenay.
2 CONT
2 CONT She married James I in 1235, being his second wife, and had several children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Violant of Aragon (1236-1301), queen of Castile by her marriage to Alfonso X.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Constance of Aragon (1239-1269), infanta of Castile by her marriage to Juan Manuel of Castile, son of Ferdinand III of Castile.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Peter III of Aragon (1240-1285).
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. James II of Majorca (1243-1311).
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Ferdinand of Aragon (1245-1250).
2 CONT
2 CONT 6. Sancha of Aragon (1246-1251).
2 CONT
2 CONT 7. Isabella of Aragon (1247-1271), married Philip III of France
2 CONT
2 CONT 8. Maria of Aragon (1248-1267), nun.
2 CONT
2 CONT 9. Sancho, Archbishop of Toledo (1250-1279)
2 CONT
2 CONT 10. Elionor of Aragon (1251-?, young)
2 CONT
2 CONT Violant and her daughter Sancha's remains are at the monastery of Santa María de Vallbona in Catalonia.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Violant_of_Hungary
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Violant of Hungary (Esztergom, Kingdom of Hungary, c. 1216 – 1253) was Queen consort of James I of Aragon. She is also called Jolánta  in Hungarian, Iolanda or Violant d'Hongria in Catalan and Yolanda or Violante de Hungría in Spa
2 CONC nish. Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Violant was a daughter of Andrew II of Hungary and Violant of Courtenay. Her paternal grandparents were Béla III of Hungary and his first wife Agnes of Antioch. Her maternal grandparents were Peter II of Courtenay and his second w
2 CONC ife Yolanda of Flanders.
2 CONT
2 CONT Violant was a half-sister of Anne Marie, Empress of Bulgaria, Béla IV of Hungary, Saint Elisabeth of Hungary and Coloman of Lodomeria.
2 CONT
2 CONT Violant's mother died in 1233, when Violant was seventeen years old. Her father remarried, to Beatrice d'Este, they had a son called Stephen. Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT Violant married James I in 1235, being his second wife. By the marriage, Violant became Queen Consort of Aragon. James already had one son, Alfonso by his first marriage to Eleanor of Castile. James however divorced Eleanor and de
2 CONC cided to remarry, he chose Violant.
2 CONT
2 CONT James and Violant had ten children:
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. Violant of Aragon (1236-1301), queen of Castile by her marriage to Alphonse X.
2 CONT    2. Constance of Aragon (1239-1269), infanta of Castile by her marriage to Juan Manuel of Castile, son of Ferdinand III of Castile.
2 CONT    3. Peter III of Aragon (1240-1285).
2 CONT    4. James II of Majorca (1243-1311).
2 CONT    5. Ferdinand of Aragon (1245-1250).
2 CONT    6. Sancha of Aragon (1246-1251).
2 CONT    7. Isabella of Aragon (1247-1271), married Philip III of France
2 CONT    8. Maria of Aragon (1248-1267), nun.
2 CONT    9. Sancho, Archbishop of Toledo (1250-1275)
2 CONT   10. Eleanor of Aragon (1251-?, young)
2 CONT
2 CONT Violant's daughter, Isabella became Queen of France by her marriage to Philip III of France. Isabella was mother of Philip IV of France and Charles of Valois.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles of Valois was father of Philip VI of France, Isabella, Duchess of Bourbon and Blanche, Queen of Germany.
2 CONT
2 CONT Violant died in 1253. Violant and her daughter Sancha's remains are at the Real Monasterio de Santa Maria de Vallbona in Vallbona de les Monges, Catalonia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her husband remarried one more time, to Teresa Gil de Vidaure, who was once James' mistress.
2 CONT --------------------
