1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''PEDRO de Aragón (1239-Villafranca del Penedés 2 or 11 Nov 1285, bur Monasterio de Santa Cruz) PEDRO III "el Grande" King of Aragon, Conde de Barcelona and King of Valencia'''
2 CONT s/o JAIME I "el Conquistador" King of Aragon & Iolanda of Hungary 
2 CONT
2 CONT x  (Montpellier 15 Jul 1262) CONSTANZA of Sicily, d/o MANFREDO [von Hohenstaufen] King of Sicily & Béatrice de Savoie ([1249]-Barcelona [8] Apr 1300)
2 CONT 1. Infante don ALFONSO de Aragón (Valencia 4 Nov 1265-Barcelona 18 Jun 1291, bur Barcelona Franciscan Monastery).
2 CONT 2. Infante don JAIME de Aragón (Valencia 10 Aug 1267-Barcelona 2 Nov 1327, bur Barcelona church of San Francisco, transferred to Monastery of Santa Cruz, prov Tarragona).
2 CONT 3. Infanta doña ISABEL de Aragón (Zaragoza 4 Jan 1271-Estremos 4 Jul 1336, bur Coimbra)
2 CONT 4. Infante don FADRIQUE de Aragón (Barcelona 1272-Palermo 25 Jun 1337, bur Catania Cathedral)
2 CONT 5.Infanta doña VIOLANTE de Aragon (1273-Termini 19 Aug 1302, bur Marseille église des Frères mineurs)
2 CONT 6.Infante don PEDRO de Aragón ([1275]-Tordehumos 30 Aug 1296). 
2 CONT
2 CONT xx JUANA (-after 27 Oct 1285).
2 CONT 7. ALFONSO Pérez de Aragón ([1255/56]-after 1269).
2 CONT 8. TERESA Pérez de Aragón (before 1260-after 27 Oct 1285). 
2 CONT
2 CONT xxx ([<1260]) MARÍA Nicolosa (-after 1292).
2 CONT 9.PEDRO de Aragón 
2 CONT 10.FERNANDO de Aragón
2 CONT 11.CONSTANZA de Aragón
2 CONT 12.TERESA Alfonso de Aragón
2 CONT
2 CONT xxxx ([1264/70]) INÉS Zapata (-after 26 Aug 1284)
2 CONT 13.JAIME Pérez de Aragón ([1265?]-after 9 Feb 1296).
2 CONT 14.JUAN de Aragón 
2 CONT
2 CONT xxxxx (early 1270s?) MUÑINA
2 CONT 15.  JUAN Benet, from Daroca (-1315)
2 CONT
2 CONT xxxxxx ([Sep 1282/May 1283]) MACALDA Scaletta, wife of ALAIMO da Lentini
2 CONT 16.MELIADÚS (Messina [late 1283/early 1284]-after 21 Apr 1347).
2 CONT
2 CONT xxxxxxx ([10 Jun/end-Aug] 1283]) MARÍA Pérez, in Logroño, (-after 15 Dec 1292).
2 CONT 17. SANCHO Pérez de Aragón ([Mar/May] 1284-[20 Sep 1334/end 1335]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Infante don PEDRO de Aragón, son of JAIME I "el Conquistador" King of Aragon & his second wife Iolanda of Hungary (1239-Villafranca del Penedés 2 or 11 Nov 1285, bur Monasterio de Santa Cruz).  Under the testament of "Jacobus…Rex 
2 CONC Aragoniæ, Majoricarum et Valenciæ, Comes Barchinonæ et Urgelli, et Dominus Montispessulani", dated 26 Aug 1272, the king made bequests to "…filium nostrum primogenitum Infantem P…"[515].  He was promised the succession of the Bale
2 CONC aric Islands, Valencia and Montpellier 1241, and received Roussillon and Cerdaña after the death of Nuño Sancho de Aragón in 1242.  A new projected partition was agreed 1244, under which Pedro would receive Catalonia on the death 
2 CONC of his father.  Appointed procurator of Catalonia 1257.  Under a further projected partition 1262, Aragon, Catalonia and Valencia were destined for Pedro.  He succeeded his father in 1276 as PEDRO III "el Grande" King of Aragon, C
2 CONC onde de Barcelona and King of Valencia, crowned at Zaragoza Nov 1276.  He suppressed the revolt in Valencia, ending with the capture of Montesa in 1277.  He confirmed his protectorate over Tunis.  He landed at Trapani in Sicily 31
2 CONC  Aug 1282, after the Sicilian revolt against the Angevins, and declared himself PIETRO I King of Sicily.  He was excommunicated by Pope Martin IV, who supported the Angevins, in Nov 1282.  In the course of the escalating dispute, 
2 CONC Philippe III King of France was persuaded to accept the Kingdom of Aragon for his second son Charles in Feb 1284.  In Sep 1284, Pedro crushed the rebellion of Juan Núñez de Lara who attempted to establish an independent lordship o
2 CONC f Albarracín.  King Philippe III invaded Aragon in early 1285 and briefly captured Girona 7 Sep 1285.  The French retreated to Perpignan (where King Philippe III died 5 Oct) after their fleet was destroyed in the Bay of Roses 3-4 
2 CONC Sep by admiral Ruggiero di Loria.  On his deathbed, Pedro renounced Sicily as the price of his peace with the church.  King Pedro’s testament is dated 26 Dec 1282, naming his legitimate children according to Bofarull’s summary[516
2 CONC ].  The Crónica de San Juan de la Peña records the death "III Id Nov" in 1285 of King Pedro and his burial "en el monasterio de Santas Cruçs del Orden de Cistells"[517].  The Chronicle of Ramon Muntaner records the death "le jour 
2 CONC de Saint-Martin" in 1285 of "le roi Pierre" and his burial "au monastère de Sainte-Croix"[518].  A manuscript chronicle records the death "circa festum S. Martini" in 1285 of "Petrus rex Aragonum" and his burial "in ecclesia beatæ
2 CONC  Mariæ sanctarum Crucum ordinis Cisterciensis"[519].  His epitaph at Santa Cruz records his death 11 Nov 1285[520]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT m (Montpellier 15 Jul 1262) CONSTANZA of Sicily, daughter of MANFREDO [von Hohenstaufen] King of Sicily & his first wife Béatrice de Savoie ([1249]-Barcelona [8] Apr 1300).  The Historia Sicula of Bartolomeo di Neocastro names "Co
2 CONC nstancia" as the daughter of "Manfredus" and his wife "dominam Beatricem", adding that she married "Petro regi Aragonum"[521].  The Chronicle of Ramon Muntaner records the marriage of "le seigneur infant Pierre" and "la fille de M
2 CONC ainfroi roi de Sicile…Constance", adding that the bride was aged 14[522].  "Infans Petrus filius…Jacobi…Regis Aragonum, Majoricarum et Valentiæ, Comitis Barchinonæ et Urgelli, et domini Montispessulani" granted dowry to "dominæ Co
2 CONC nstantiæ filiæ…Manfredi Regis Siciliæ…uxori nostræ" by charter dated 13 Jun 1262, which names "domino Bonifacio de Anglano…Comite Montisalbani…avunculo dicti domini Regis Siciliæ", countersigned 13 Nov 1264[523].  Jaime II King of
2 CONC  Aragon notified the death “dia Veneris Sancta, quasi in media nocte” of “mater nostra domina Constancia, fidelis recordationis Aragonum regina” by charter dated 9 Apr 1300 at Barcelona[524]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mistress (1):  JUANA, daughter of --- (-after 27 Oct 1285).  Cingolani records that she was the mother of the king’s daughter Teresa Pérez, suggesting that “Tal vez...debía de proceder de alguna familia de cierto nivel social, si 
2 CONC consideramos que Teresa se casó con dos nobles aragoneses”[525].  A document dated 27 Oct 1285 records payments, guaranteed from income from Lérida, to “dompne Iohanne, matri dompne Taresie Petri, uxor nobilis Artaldi de Alagone”[
2 CONC 526]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mistress (2): ([before 1260]) MARÍA Nicolosa, daughter of --- (-after 1292).  Bofarull names “una Señora llamada doña María” as mother of the king’s illegitimate children “D. Jaime Perez, D. Juna y Da Beatriz que casó con D. Ramon
2 CONC  de Cardona” (no sources cited, the last named incorrect as shown below)[527].  Salazar y Acha names her “María Nicolau, muerta después de 1292” (no sources cited)[528].  Cingolani cites a document in “los registros de Cancellería
2 CONC ” which names her “María Nicolosa”, indicating that this was “un apellido claramente italiano, aunque no puedo precisar de qué parte de la península”, and suggests her relationship with the future king “algo antes de 1262, o inclu
2 CONC so de 1260” given the appointment of her son Jaime Pérez 1 Jul 1280 as ”almirante...cargo que dificilmente se justificaría en alguien demasiado joven”[529].  In another article, Cingolani cites numerous other sources relating to M
2 CONC aría Nicolosa[530]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mistress (3): ([1264/70]) INÉS Zapata, daughter of --- (-after 26 Aug 1284).  The Nobiliario of Pedro Conde de Barcelos names "D. Ines Zapata" as the mother of "D. Pedro de Aragon", son of "el Rey D. Pedro"[531].  Cingolani sugges
2 CONC ts her relationship with the future king “aproximadamente en los años 1264-1270” given the birth of the couple’s four children, and that she was “una noble aragonesa afincada en el reino de Valencia” in light of a document dated 1
2 CONC 4 Sep 1279 in which King Pedro named her “nobilis Agnesi Çabate”[532].  IN another article, Cingolani cites numerous other sources relating to Inés Zapata[533]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mistress (4): (early 1270s?) MUÑINA, daughter of ---.  Cingolani suggests the future king’s relationship with “una mujer llamada Muñina, de Daroca. Posiblemente...en los primeros años 1270”, noting a claim in late 1304 made by “Ju
2 CONC an Benet de Daroca” that he was the king’s son and the resulting inquiry dated 23 Feb 1306 by “el Justicia de Aragón, Jimeno Pérez de Salanova [the king’s illegitimate son, see below]”[534].  In his other article, Cingolani discus
2 CONC ses Muñina and her son in more detail[535]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mistress (5): ([Sep 1282/May 1283]) MACALDA Scaletta, wife of ALAIMO da Lentini, in Messina, daughter of ---.  Cingolani records the king’s relationship with “una anónima mujer siciliana...entre septiembre de 1282 y mayo de 1283..
2 CONC .cuando el rey Pedro estuve en Sicilia”, noting that in early 1325 “un llamado Meliadús” came from Siciliy and presented himself in Barcelona to King Jaime II who “acepta la validez de las pruebas...y se le dirige como hermano”, a
2 CONC lthough “no obtuvo nada de su medio hermano” and returned to Sicily where he was recorded “entre los prisoneros de la batalla de Lípari en 1339”[536].  In his other article, Cingolani discusses this Sicilian lady and her son in mo
2 CONC re detail[537].  Sciascia records “Macalda Scaletta, moglie del capitano [di Messina] Alaimo da Lentini” as the mother of Meliadús[538]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mistress (6): ([10 Jun/end-Aug] 1283]) MARÍA Pérez, in Logroño, daughter of --- (-after 15 Dec 1292).  Cingolani records a document dated 15 Dec 1292, in which King Jaime II ordered payments to “dompna María Pérez de lo Gronyo, ma
2 CONC ter Sancii, filii...domini regis Petri, clare memorie patris nostri”, and dates King Pedro’s presence in Logroño “entre el 10 de junio y finales del mes de agosto de 1283” and Sancho’s birth “entre marzo y mayo de 1284”[539].  In 
2 CONC his other article, Cingolani discusses María Pérez and her son in more detail[540]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT King Pedro III & his wife had six children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.         Infante don ALFONSO de Aragón (Valencia 4 Nov 1265-Barcelona 18 Jun 1291, bur Barcelona Franciscan Monastery).  The Crónica de San Juan de la Peña names "el primero Alfonso et el otro Jayme et el otro Frederico" as the 
2 CONC sons of King Pedro and his wife Constanza[541].  The Historia Sicula of Bartolomeo di Neocastro names "Alfonsus, Elisabeth regina Portugalli…Rex Iacobus, Dominus Fridericus, domina Violanta et dominus Petrus" as the children of "P
2 CONC etro regi Aragonum" and his wife[542].  The Chronicle of Ramon Muntaner names "Alfonse, Jacques, Frédéric et Pierre" as the four sons of "le seigneur infant Pierre" and "la fille de Mainfroi roi de Sicile…Constance"[543].  He succ
2 CONC eeded his father in 1285 as ALFONSO III "el Liberal" King of Aragon and Valencia, Count of Barcelona.  After he succeeded, he continued his father’s campaign against the Balearic Islands to punish his uncle Jaime II King of Mallor
2 CONC ca, for the latter’s support of the French during the dispute over Sicily.  He recaptured  Mallorca city and Ibiza 1286, and captured Minorca from the Moors Jan 1287.  However, diplomatic tensions with France and the Papacy worsen
2 CONC ed, and eventually Alfonso was forced to withdraw his support for his brother at Tarascon Feb 1291 as the price for lifting sanctions.  He was forced to grant further privileges to the Aragonese Union 1287 (“el privilegio de la un
2 CONC ión”), after the latter invaded Valencia and placed an embargo on royal revenues.  The Crónica de San Juan de la Peña records the death in 1292 of King Alfonso aged 27 and his burial "en el monasterio de los frayres menores de Bar
2 CONC chinona"[544].  The Chronicle of Ramon Muntaner records the death of King Alfonso, dated to 1291, his burial "à l´ordre des frères mineurs à Barcelona", and adds that "il était parfaitement vierge, n´ayant jamais approché de femme
2 CONC "[545].  m (Betrothed [8 Oct 1272], by proxy Westminster Abbey 15 Aug 1290, not consummated) as her first husband, ELEANOR of England, daughter of EDWARD I King of England & his first wife Infanta doña Leonor de Castilla (Windsor 
2 CONC Castle before 17 Jun 1269-Ghent 12 Oct 1297, bur Westminster Abbey).  The marriage contract between “Edwardus...rex Angliæ...filiam suam majorem” and “infans Petrus...regis Aragonum primogenitus” is dated 8 Oct 1272[546].  Despite
2 CONC  the error of name, it is likely that this betrothal relates to the king´s known eldest son Alfonso, whom Eleanor later married by proxy, rather than an otherwise unrecorded older son named Pedro: no case has been found in the fam
2 CONC ily of the kings of Aragon where the oldest son of the king was named after his father.  A charter dated 19 Jun 1281 confirmed the marriage contract between “rex...filiæ nostræ” and “rege Aragoniæ...primogeniti sui”[547].  The Chr
2 CONC onicle of Ramon Muntaner records that Edward I King of England sent "Jean d´Agrilli" to Barcelona to negotiate the marriage of his daughter to Alfonso III King of Aragon, dated to 1286, and records the betrothal later the same yea
2 CONC r[548].  She married secondly (20 Sep 1393) Henri III Comte de Bar.  The Continuator of Florence of Worcester records the marriage "apud Bristoll vigilia S Matthaæi Apostoli" 20 Sep [1293] of "Alienora regis Angliæ flia primogenit
2 CONC a" and "domino Henrico comitis de Baroduc"[549].  Mistress: DULCE, daughter of BERNAT de Caldes, of Barcelona, & his wife ---.  Cingolani records her parentage and relationship with King Alfonso, citing a codicil dated 13 Jun 1291
2 CONC  under which the king requested that his heir “beneficiat Dulce” and take care of any child if she were pregnant, commenting that he found no record of any child being born[550]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.         Infante don JAIME de Aragón (Valencia 10 Aug 1267-Barcelona 2 Nov 1327, bur Barcelona church of San Francisco, transferred to Monastery of Santa Cruz, prov Tarragona).  The Crónica de San Juan de la Peña names "el prime
2 CONC ro Alfonso et el otro Jayme et el otro Frederico" as the sons of King Pedro and his wife Constanza[551].  The Chronicle of Ramon Muntaner names "Alfonse, Jacques, Frédéric et Pierre" as the four sons of "le seigneur infant Pierre"
2 CONC  and "la fille de Mainfroi roi de Sicile…Constance"[552].  He succeeded his father in 1285 as GIACOPO King of Sicily.  He succeeded his brother in 1291 as JAIME II “el Justo” King of Aragon and Valencia, Conde de Barcelona. 
2 CONT
2 CONT -        see below. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.         Infanta doña ISABEL de Aragón (Zaragoza 4 Jan 1271-Estremos 4 Jul 1336, bur Coimbra).  The Historia Sicula of Bartolomeo di Neocastro names "Alfonsus, Elisabeth regina Portugalli…Rex Iacobus, Dominus Fridericus, domina 
2 CONC Violanta et dominus Petrus" as the children of "Petro regi Aragonum" and his wife[553].  Her parentage and marriage are confirmed by the Chronicon Conimbricensi which names “Rex Aragoniæ…Dñs Joannes filius Regis Domni Petri” as “f
2 CONC rater…Dñæ Elizabeth Reginæ Portugaliæ”[554].  The marriage by proxy of “Dionysius...Rex Portugalliæ et Algarbii” and “P...Rege Aragon...Elisabeth filiam maiore” is dated 11 Feb 1282[555].  The first testament of “Dona Isabel...Rai
2 CONC nha de Portugal e do Algarve”, dated 19 Apr 1314, chose burial “em Alcobaça”, and names “Infante D. Affonço meu filho primeiro herdeiro...Dom Pedro meu irmão...”[556].  Her husband banished her to a fortress when she supported her
2 CONC  son in his revolt against his father.  The testament of “D. Diniz...Rey de Portugal e do Algarve”, dated 20 Jun 1322, appointed “a Raynha Donna Isabel minha mulher e Affonso Sanches meu Filho...” among his executors[557].  After 
2 CONC her husband died she retired to a house at Coimbra near a Poor Clare convent which she had founded and devoted her life to good works.  The second testament of “Dona Isabel...Rainha de Portugal e do Algarve”, dated 22 Dec 1327, ch
2 CONC ose burial “em o meu mosteiro de Santa Clara e de Sancta Isabel de Coimbra” with “Ifante Dona Isabel minha neta”, made bequests to “Rainha Dona Breatis minha filha...Ifanta Dona Maria sa filha...Iffanta Dona Maria minha neta...Iff
2 CONC anta Dona Leanor minha neta...minha sobrinha Dona Izabel...Dom Affonso filho de Dõ Pedro meu irmaõ...el Rey meu filho...Dona Vataça”, and appointed “El Rey Dom Affonço meu filho e a Rainha Dona Breatis minha filha sa mulher,e o If
2 CONC ante Dom Pedro meu neto filho primeiro herdeiro do dito Rey Dom Affonço e a Ifanta Dona Maria minha neta...Dona Vataça” as her executors[558].  The Breve Chronicon Alcobacense records that "rex Dyonisius…Elisabeth uxor sua Regina"
2 CONC  was buried "Colimbrie in monasterio Sancte Clare quod ipsa construxit"[559].   She was canonised 25 May 1625, feast day 8 July[560].  m (by proxy Barcelona 2 Feb 1282, in person Trancosa 24 Jun 1282) DINIZ King of Portugal, son o
2 CONC f AFONSO III "o Restaurador" King of Portugal & his second wife Beatriz de Castilla (Lisbon 9 Oct 1261-Santarem 7 Jan 1325, bur Odivelas).
