2 CONC b=millind&h=10954011&ti=5538&indiv=try&gss=pt 150px-Retour_d_Isabelle_de_France_en_Angleterre http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=image&guid=17a60758-3dea-4d33-802a-10c8ec4ac5c8&tid=6870384&pid=-1088322242
1 NOTE "OF FRANCE"; KNOWN LATER IN LIFE AS "THE SHE-WOLF" FOR HER RUTHLESS TREATMENT
2 CONT OF HER HUSBAND
1 NOTE Isabella (1295-1358), daughter of Philip the Fair of France, married Edward II of England at Boulogne in 1308. She and the king did not agree, and she often sided with his enemies. She was probably privy to his murder, and she and
2 CONC  her paramour, Mortimer, ruled England for some time after the ascession of her son, Edward III. [World Wide Illustrated Encyclopedia, 1935]
2 CONT
2 CONT May have been born in 1295. {Iain Kerr January 1995} [GADD.GED]
1 NOTE
2 CONT 1.  Isabelle and Welsh rebel Roger de Mortimer (1287-1330), Eighth Baron of Wigmore and First Earl March, raised an army to invade England from France in 1326.  In 1330, when Edward, III seized power, Mortimer was condemned to dea
2 CONC th by Parliament.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.  The following is a somewhat differing record of Edward, II's queen:
2 CONT
2 CONT      'Isabella, 1296-1358, queen consort of Edward, II of England, daughter of
2 CONT      Philip, IV of France, neglected and mistreated by her husband, hated the
2 CONT      royal favorites, the Despensers, who had seized her lands.  While in France
2 CONT      (1325), she formed a liaison with Roger de Mortimer.  They invaded England
2 CONT      in 1326, forced Edward to abdicate, and caused his murder. They ruled
2 CONT      corruptly until Edward, III seized power in 1330.'
2 CONT
