2 CONT ROBERT (DE HOLAND), LORD HOLAND, son and heir, was under age at hisfather's death, being then aged 16. His marriage was quickly granted tohis mother, who offered 500 marks. He received the custody of Chelveston,Northants, in 1333 
2 CONC in reward for his services in the war with Scotland.He proved his age and had livery of his lands in 1335, and next year isdescribed as a knight. He fought in the wars with France, and, being inthe retinue of the Earl of Warwick i
2 CONC n April 1346, was probably present atCrécy, 26 August 1346, and in Nov. 1347 he is stated to have served inthe King's retinue. In March 1347 he assisted Sir John de Dalton toabduct Margery de la Beche from Beaumes Manor, near Read
2 CONC ing. In 1355 heagain went overseas. He was summoned to a Council 25 February 1341/2, andto Parliament from 1 June 1363 till his death. He appears to have residedchiefly in Northants, where he was appointed on various Commissionsju
2 CONC dicial and military, and in 1360 was a guardian of Garendon Abbey. Headded to the endowment of Upholland Priory in 1367.
