2 CONT     4. Espolin (GS #12462, pt. 1, p. 98-99)
2 CONT
2 CONT !HISTORICAL NOTES:
2 CONT     Edward III ruled England for 50 years, from 1327 to 1377. He is the ancestorof 32 of the families in Royal Ancestors and is the latest king from whom any of these families descend.
1 NOTE Edward of Windsor, the eldest child of Edward II and Isabella of France, was the first English king to have the time of his birth noted exactly.  He was born at 5:40am on Monday 13 November 1312 and baptized four days later in the
2 CONC  old chapel of St Edward in Windsor Castle.  The names of seven godfathers are recorded, but no godmother.  A few days later his father created him Earl of Chester, but not, for some reason, Prince of Wales.
2 CONT Aged only 14 at accession, he began his active rule in 1330 after killing Roger Mortimer who had ruled in his name.  The principal preoccupation during his long reign of fifty years was to be his claim to the throne of France, whi
2 CONC ch began the Hundred Years' War.  Edward assumed the title of King of France in 1340, claiming the crown through his mother, as heir of her brother Charles IV, who had died in 1328.  The French barons, however, did not recognize t
2 CONC he principle of inheritance through the female line and Charles had been succeeded by his cousin Philip VI, the nearest male heir.  Edward did homage to him for his French fiefs in 1329 and 1331.  Some years later, English commerc
2 CONC ial interests connected with the wool trade in Flanders precipitated a commercial crisis.  Flanders had passed under French administration in 1328 and it was the powerful Flemish weavers who persuaded Edward to advance his claim t
2 CONC o France after concluding a commercial treaty with him in 1338.
2 CONT Philip's answer to Edward's pretensions was to declare his French fiefs forfeited and invade Guienne.  Edward, no less a warrior than his grandfather Edward I, took up arms to defend his title by seaand land.  The great naval batt
2 CONC le of Sluys in 1340 gave England control of the Channel and this was followed by the land victories of Crecy (1346) and Poitiers (1356), in which the superiority of theEnglish long bowmen over the heavily armored French cavalry wo
2 CONC n the day.  Calais was taken after a long siege in 1347, thus giving the English an important economic and military base.
2 CONT The hero of the wars was Edward's eldest son, Edward, Prince of Wales, later known as 'The Black Prince', who came to be regarded as the 'model of chivalry', but who was in reality bad-tempered, foul-mouthed and cruel.  It was per
2 CONC haps a mercy that he died a year before his father and never lived to become king.
2 CONT In the years 1348-50 northern Europe was ravaged by the Black Death, an outbreak of bubonic plague, which is said to have halved the population of England and served greatly to undermine her militarystrength.
2 CONT The Treaty of Bretigny in 1360 ended the wars for a time and Edward renounced his claim to the French crown.  King John of France, the son and successor of Philip, was released from prison in England, where he had been held since 
2 CONC his capture at Poitiers, and allowed to return home.
2 CONT In 1369 Edward renewed his claim and resumed the title of King of France after the Black Prince had refused to appear at the court of Charles V (John's successor) to answer complaints brought againsthim by the Count of Armagnac.  
2 CONC Poitou was re conquered but the French regained control of the Channel, with Castilian aid, at the battle of La Rochelle (1372) and successfully blocked English transport.  When the Black Prince died of dysentery in 1376, English 
2 CONC fortunes were at their lowest ebb and all that remained were the five fortified towns of Bordeaux, Bayonne, Brest, Calais, Cherbourg and their coastal land.  France, however was in a ruinous state.
2 CONT At home, Edwards III's reign saw many changes.  English replaced French as the official language of the law courts (and Chaucer commenced writing his Canterbury Tales in English0, the office of Justice of the Peace was created, an
2 CONC d Parliament divided into two houses.
2 CONT In 1348Edward founded the Order of the Garter which was to become (and still remains) one of the leading orders of chivalry in Europe.
2 CONT Edward was a tall, dignified man of regal bearing.  Though sometimes given to violent outbursts of temper, a Plantagenet characteristic, his charm, generosity, and affinity with the baronial classes helped him to retain his popula
2 CONC rity for most of his reign.  He was happily married to the plump and somewhat stolid Philippa for over forty years.
2 CONT After her death in 1369, he acquired a rapacious mistress, Alice Perrers - 'that wanton baggage' as a contemporary account describes her - who rendered the declining years of the senile though lecherous King a thorough misery.  Sh
2 CONC e and her daughter Isabella were in the habit of sleeping with the King together and it seems highly probable tht one or the other of them infected the old man with gonorrhea.  Edward was taken ill in September 1376 and only parti
2 CONC ally recovered in the following spring when an abscess (the locality of which is not specified) burst and gave him some relief.  He was able to attend Parliament, but towards the end of May suffered a stroke and died at Sheen Pala
2 CONC ce in June.  His body was left unattended for several hours and Alice Perrers is said to have robbed it of its rings and personal jewelry.  She lived comfortably on her ill-gotten gains until her death in 1400.
2 CONT Edward was buried in Westminster Abbey beside Philippa.  The wooden head of the effigy carried on the coffin at his funeral in accordance with custom is preserved in the Abbey museum and appears to have been modelled from a death 
2 CONC mask as the mouth shows signs of the characteristic distortion suffered by some stroke victims.  The impressive effigy on his tomb was probably derived from the same source although it is more stylized.
1 NOTE !SOURCES:
2 CONT     1. The Royal Dau of Eng., Eng. 120, v. 1, tab. II, pt. I, II, p. 63, 224-25,228-32, 241-42
