1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Jutta was born in Prague, Bohemia on 20 May 1315, the second eldest daughter of John the Blind of Luxembourg, King of Bohemia and Elisabeth of Bohemia. Jutta was an elder sister of the future Holy Roman Emperor Cha
2 CONC rles IV, who was crowned six years after Jutta's death in 1349. Bonne's maternal grandparents were, Wenceslaus II of Bohemia and his first wife, Judith of Habsburg. Her paternal grandparents were, Henry VII, Holy Roman Emperor and
2 CONC  his wife Margaret of Brabant. When Bonne was about fifteen, her mother died. Her father decided to remarry, he married Beatrice of Bourbon. They had a son, Wenceslaus, who succeeded his father in Luxembourg. John and Beatrice als
2 CONC o had daughter, who was also called Bonne, but she died young.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jutta was born in Prague, Bohemia on 20 May 1315, the second eldest daughter of John the Blind of Luxembourg, King of Bohemia and Elisabeth of Bohemia. Jutta was an elder sister of the future Holy Roman Emperor Charles IV, who was
2 CONC  crowned six years after Jutta's death in 1349. Bonne's maternal grandparents were, Wenceslaus II of Bohemia and his first wife, Judith of Habsburg. Her paternal grandparents were, Henry VII, Holy Roman Emperor and his wife Margar
2 CONC et of Brabant. When Bonne was about fifteen, her mother died. Her father decided to remarry, he married Beatrice of Bourbon. They had a son, Wenceslaus, who succeeded his father in Luxembourg. John and Beatrice also had daughter, 
2 CONC who was also called Bonne, but she died young.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bonne_of_Bohemia
2 CONT
2 CONT Bonne of Luxemburg (also Bona), Duchess of Normandy, Countess of Anjou and of Maine (May 20, 1315 – September 11, 1349), was born Jutta, the daughter of John the Blind of Luxemburg, king of Bohemia and his first wife Elisabeth of 
2 CONC Bohemia. She was the first wife of King John II of France; however, as her death occurred a year prior to his coronation, she was never a French queen consort. Jutta was referred to in French historiography as Bonne de Luxembourg.
2 CONC  She was a member of the House of Luxembourg. King Charles V of France, and Jeanne of Valois, Queen of Navarre were two of her ten children.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Bonne of Bohemia
2 CONT
2 CONT Bonne of Luxemburg (also Bona) (May 20, 1315 – September 11, 1349), was born Jutta, the daughter of John the Blind of Luxemburg, king of Bohemia and his first wife Elisabeth of Bohemia. Jutta was referred to in French historiograp
2 CONC hy as Bonne de Luxembourg. Jutta was the eldest sister of the future Holy Roman Emperor Charles IV, who was crowned such six years after Jutta's death in 1349. Bonne was married to the future John II of France on July 28, 1332 in 
2 CONC Melun. She was 17 years old, and the future king was 13. The name for Jutta (or Guta) translatable into English as Good (in the feminine case), was changed by the time of marriage to Bonne (French) or Bona (Latin). Upon marriage B
2 CONC onne was the wife of the heir to the French throne, becoming Duchess of Normandy and Countess of Anjou and of Maine.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Bonne of Bohemia
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Bonne of Luxemburg (also Boner) (May 20, 1315 – September 11, 1349), was born Jutta, the daughter of John the Blind of Luxemburg, king of Bohemia and his first wife Elisabeth of Bohemia. Jutta was referred to in French historiogra
2 CONC phy as Bonne de Luxembourg. Jutta was the eldest sister of the future Holy Roman Emperor Charles IV, who was crowned such six years after Jutta's death in 1349.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was married to the future John II of France on July 28, 1332 in Melun. She was 17 years old, and the future king was 13. The name for Jutta (or Guta) translatable into English as Good (in the feminine case), was changed by the
2 CONC  time of marriage to Bonne (French) or Bona (Latin). Upon marriage Bonne was the wife of the heir to the French throne, becoming Duchess of Normandy and Countess of Anjou and of Maine.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their children were:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1) Charles V of France (January 21, 1338 - September 16, 1380)
2 CONT
2 CONT 2) Louis I of Naples (July 23, 1339 - September 20, 1384)
2 CONT
2 CONT 3) John, Duke of Berry (November 30, 1340 - June 15, 1416)
2 CONT
2 CONT 4) Philip II, Duke of Burgundy (January 17, 1342 - April 27, 1404)
2 CONT
2 CONT 5) Jeanne de Valois, Queen of Navarre. Queen consort of Charles II of Navarre (June 24, 1343 - November 3, 1373)
2 CONT
2 CONT 6) Marie Valois (September 12, 1344 - October, 1404)
2 CONT
2 CONT 7) Agnès of Valois (1345 - 1349)
2 CONT
2 CONT 8) Marguerite (1347 - 1352)
2 CONT
2 CONT 9) Isabelle (October 1, 1348 - September 11, 1372)
2 CONT
2 CONT She died in 1349 of the bubonic plague in Maubisson, France. This was one year and fifteen days prior to the coronation of her husband as King, John II of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Bonne of Luxemburg (also Bona) (May 20, 1315 – September 11, 1349), was born Jutta, the daughter of John the Blind of Luxemburg, king of Bohemia and his first wife Elisabeth of Bohemia. Jutta was referred to in French historiograp
2 CONC hy as Bonne de Luxembourg. She was a member of the House of Luxembourg
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://www.familysearch.org/eng/search/PRF/individual_record.asp?recid=1341544826
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Bonne de Luxembourg, née le 20 mai 1315, morte le 11 septembre 1349 à l'abbaye de Maubuisson, fut l'épouse de Jean II dit le Bon.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fille de Jean Ier l'Aveugle, comte de Luxembourg et roi de Bohême, et d'Élisabeth de Bohême, elle épouse le dauphin Jean le 28 juillet 1332 à Melun. De cette union naquirent onze enfants :
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. Blanche (1336-1336)
2 CONT
2 CONT    2. Charles V (1337-1380), futur roi de France de 1364 à 1380.
2 CONT
2 CONT    3. Catherine (1338-1338)
2 CONT
2 CONT    4. Louis (1339-1384) duc d'Anjou épouse Marie de Blois-Châtillon (1345-1404)
2 CONT
2 CONT    5. Jean (1340-1416) duc de Berry épouse en 1360 Jeanne d'Armagnac (?-1387)
2 CONT
2 CONT    6. Philippe II le Hardi (1342-1404) duc de Bourgogne épouse en 1369 Marguerite III de Flandre (1350-1405)
2 CONT
2 CONT    7. Jeanne (1343-1373) épouse en 1352 Charles II le Mauvais, roi de Navarre (1332-1387)
2 CONT
2 CONT    8. Marie (1344-1404) épouse Robert Ier, duc de Bar.
2 CONT
2 CONT    9. Agnes de Valois (1345-1349)
2 CONT
2 CONT   10. Marguerite (1347-1352)
2 CONT
2 CONT   11. Isabelle (1348-1372) épouse Jean-Galéas Visconti (1351-1402) duc de Milan
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
