1 NOTE Charles V the Wise (French: Charles V le Sage) (January 31, 1338 – September 16, 1380) was king of France (1364 to 1380) and a member of the Valois Dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born at Vincennes, Île-de-France, France, son of King Jean II and Bonne of Luxembourg.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the first French heir to use the title dauphin after the region of Dauphiné was acquired by his father. He was crowned King of France in 1364 at the cathedral at Reims, France.
2 CONT
2 CONT His reign was marred by the Hundred Years' War, but Charles' army scored some victories and defeated the army of the King of Navarre. Despite the influence of his advisor, Philippe de Mézières, he declined to be drawn into a crusa
2 CONC de. Nonetheless, dissatisfaction with his rule was such that at one point the Mayor of Paris, Etienne Marcel, led a revolt against Charles that forced the king to flee the city. This matter was resolved but to protect Paris from t
2 CONC he English, Charles V rebuilt the Left Bank wall and built a new wall on the Right Bank that extended to a new fortification called the Bastille. A strong supporter of the arts, Charles had the Louvre restored and improved and in 
2 CONC 1367 created the first royal library in France. Charles V le Sage kept up a circle of intellectuals like Nicole Oresme, Philippe de Mézières, Raoul de Presle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles V died on September 17, 1380 at Beauté-sur-Marne, France and was interred with his wife, Jeanne de Bourbon in Saint Denis Basilica.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was succeeded by his son, Charles VI.
1 NOTE Svenska Wikipedia.
1 NOTE KNOWN AS "THE WISE"; KING OF FRANCE 1364-1380 (ACCEDED 4/8/1364, CROWNED AT
2 CONT RHEIMS 5/19/1364; DIED OF GOUT
1 NOTE Charles V (1337-80), surnamed the Wise, King of France, son of John the Good. Succeeding his father in 1364, he cleared the country of numerous bands of mercenaries by dispatching them under Du Guesclin against the English in Spai
2 CONC n, employed the same able captain in the successful expulsion of the English from France, except at Calais, Bordeaux, Bayonne, and Cherbourg, and effectually curbed the power of the feudal nobility. Besides this, he established th
2 CONC e administration of justice, and laid the foundations of the National Library by collecting mss. in the Louvre. See Benoist�s La Politique du Roi Charles V (1886). [World Wide Illustrated Encyclopedia, 1935]
1 NOTE Premier  áa porter le titre de Dauphin de France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Il assure la râegence pendant la captivitâe de son páere, Jean II le Bon. Il doit âegalement râeprimer la râevolte d'Etienne Marcel et la Jacquerie.
2 CONT
2 CONT C'est lui qui signe le traitâe de Brâetigny avec l'Angleterre (voir note du roi Jean II le Bon).
2 CONT
2 CONT Il s'entoure d'hommes de valeur : Jean et Guillaume de Dormans, Pierre  d'Orgemont et Nicolas Oresme pour les finances.
2 CONT
2 CONT Grãace áa Du Guesclin, il met fin áa la lutte contre le roi de Navarre, Charles II le Mauvais et dâebarrasse le royaume des Grandes Compagnies.
2 CONT
2 CONT A sa mort, les possessions anglaises sont tráes restreintes : Bordeaux, Calais et quelques places fortes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Il fonde la Bibliotháeque royale, reconstruit le Louvre, âedifie l'hãotel de Saint-Pol et la Bastille áa Paris ainsi que le chãateau de Beautâe-sur-Marne.
1 NOTE Charles V (of France), called The Wise (1337-80), king of France 
2 CONT (1364-80), born in Vincennes. He was the son of King John II. When John 
2 CONT was captured in September 1356 by the English at Poitiers during the 
2 CONT Hundred Years' War, Charles assumed the regency. The most significant 
2 CONT events of his regency were the Jacquerie (a peasant revolt) and the 
2 CONT insurrection of Paris under Étienne Marcel(circa 1315-58). Upon the death 
2 CONT (1364) of his father, Charles ascended the throne. War with the English 
2 CONT continued for a number of years, but with results highly favorable to 
2 CONT Charles, who stripped them of most of their conquests in France and 
2 CONT strengthened the power of the monarchy. A generous patron of literature 
2 CONT and the arts, Charles founded (1367) the first royal library in France. 
2 CONT During his reign the Bastille was added to the fortifications of Paris. He 
2 CONT was succeeded by his son, Charles VI.
1 NOTE Karl den V "den vise"( från Charles den V le Sage) 1338-1380 Kung av Frankrike 1364-1380.
1 NOTE Karl var son till Johan II, och när johan tillfångatogs av engelsmännen efter slaget vid Poitiers 
2 CONT år 1356,blev Karl regent. Under sina tidiga år som regent i Frankrike, tvingades Karl bekämpa 
2 CONT uppror och resningar i många delar av det drabbade riket (la Jacquerie). Fred slöts år 1360 
2 CONT med England i Brétigny.
1 NOTE År 1364 dog Johan II i Engelsk fångenskap och Karl tog offeciellt tronen. Karl återupptog snart 
2 CONT fientligheterna med Engelsmännen (hundraårskriget) och franska arméerna gick fram med stora 
2 CONT segrar under Karls begåvade General Bertrand du Guesclin.
1 NOTE Karl var Kulturelt intresserad och samlade kring sig lysande sällskap av 
2 CONT vetenskapsmän,författare,konstnärer och andra interlektuella från alla hörn av Europa. Han 
2 CONT byggde en ny armé och en ny flotta. Vid hans död år 1380 hade Frankrike åter rest sig efter de 
2 CONT inledande katasroferna i hundraårskriget.
2 CONT Gift Jeanne de Bourbon.
