1 NOTE Philippe, demeurâe aupráes de son páere, Jean Le Bon, áa la bataille de Poitiers, reste câeláebre par les conseils qu'il lui donne : "Páere, gardez-vous áa droite, Páere, gardez-vous áa gauche..."
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean le Bon crâee l'apanage du duchâe de Bourgogne pour Philippe en 1361.
1 NOTE He was the founder of the second and last ducal house of Burgundy. For his 
2 CONT actions in the Battle of Poitiers (1356), he won the nickname The Bold. He 
2 CONT shared his father's captivity in England, and on returning to France in 
2 CONT 1360 he received the duchy of Touraine. Three years later, after the 
2 CONT death, without heirs, of the duke of Burgundy, Philip renounced Touraine 
2 CONT and received Burgundy from his brother, Charles V of France. Between 1369 
2 CONT and 1373 he fought against the English in Normandy and Poitou, and in 1382 
2 CONT he helped to suppress the revolt of the Flemish towns against their count, 
2 CONT Louis of Male, who was his father-in-law. On the death of his 
2 CONT father-in-law in 1384, Philip inherited Flanders. As one of the regents 
2 CONT for the young Charles VI, king of France, from 1380 until the time when 
2 CONT Charles came of age eight years later, Philip was the virtual ruler of 
2 CONT France. When Charles became insane in 1392, Philip was again appointed 
2 CONT regent for him.
1 NOTE Duke of Burgundy. [GADD.GED]
