1 OCCU Conde de Châtillon, Blois e St.Pol, Count of Blois, comte de Saint -Pol et comte de Blois, Conde de Saint Pol, Graaf van Saint-Pol en Blois, heer van Châtillon
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Hugh I of Châtillon, Count of Blois, member of the prominent crusading family.
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hugh_I_of_Ch%C3%A2tillon  
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh I of Châtillon (died April 9, 1248), was Count of Blois from 1230 to 1241, and Count of Saint Pol from 1226 to 1248.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was son of Gaucher II of Châtillon and Elisabeth of Saint Pol.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1226, Hugh married Marie of Avesnes, daughter of Walter of Avesnes and Margaret of Blois. They had 5 children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.John (d. 1280), Count of Blois
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.Guy (d. 1289), Count of Saint Pol
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.Gaucher (d. 1261), lord of Crécy and Crevecoeur
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.Hugh (d. 1255)
2 CONT
2 CONT 5.Basile (d. 1280), became Abbess of Notre Dame du Val in 1248
2 CONT
2 CONT Through his marriage Hugh became the first count of Blois from the house of Châtillon. It marked the end of the first dynasty of Blois that lasted over 400 years. After the death of Marie, Hugh married Mahaut, daughter of the Coun
2 CONC t of Guînes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh followed the pious king Louis IX when he started on the Seventh Crusade in 1248. Passing Avignon, there was a skirmish with some villagers in which Hugh was killed. Most of the 50 knights of his retinue returned home after th
2 CONC e incident.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh I, Count of Blois
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT   (Redirected from Hugh I of Châtillon)
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh I of Châtillon (died April 9, 1248), was Count of Blois from 1230 to 1241, and Count of Saint Pol from 1226 to 1248.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was son of Gaucher II of Châtillon and Elisabeth of Saint Pol.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1226, Hugh married Marie of Avesnes, daughter of Walter of Avesnes and Margaret of Blois. They had 5 children:
2 CONT
2 CONT John (d. 1280), Count of Blois
2 CONT
2 CONT Guy (d. 1289), Count of Saint Pol
2 CONT
2 CONT Gaucher (d. 1261), lord of Crécy and Crevecoeur
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh (d. 1255)
2 CONT
2 CONT Basile (d. 1280), became Abbess of Notre Dame du Val in 1248
2 CONT
2 CONT Through his marriage Hugh became the first count of Blois from the house of Châtillon. It marked the end of the first dynasty of Blois that lasted over 400 years. After the death of Marie, Hugh married Mahaut, daughter of the Coun
2 CONC t of Guînes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh followed the pious king Louis IX when he started on the Seventh Crusade in 1248. Passing Avignon, there was a skirmish with some villagers in which Hugh was killed. Most of the 50 knights of his retinue returned home after th
2 CONC e incident.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]External links
2 CONT
2 CONT Counts of Blois
2 CONT
2 CONT **Before 1216 he married Agnès de Bar, daughter of Thibaud I, Count de Bar & Luxembourg and Ermesinde de Bar. In 1225 Hugh became notorious for his treatment of Agnès; from horseback, he dragged her by the hair to the town of Coui
2 CONC lly to punish her for an adulterous relationship with a page. She died of her wounds. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In April 1226 Hugh married Marie D’Avesnes, Countess de Blois, daughter and heiress of Gautier II D’Avesnes, seigneur D’Avesnes, Guise & Conde, and Marguerite de Blois. To show repentance for his abuse of Agnès, Hugh founded, with
2 CONC  Marie's assistance, the first abbey of Pont aux Dames, near the place of his misdeed. He and Marie had four sons of whom three, Jean, Guy II and Gaucher IV had progeny. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Between 1225 and 1230 Hugh strengthened the ramparts around Crécy which he had built with his father. His second wife Marie died in 1241, and he then married Mahaut de Guînes, daughter of Arnold II, Count de Guines and Béatrix de 
2 CONC Bourbourg. This marriage produced no offspring.  Hugh set off for the Holy Land with the Seventh Crusade, which left France in 1248. Passing Avignon, there was a skirmish with some villagers in which Hugh was killed on 9 April. Mo
2 CONC st of the 50 knights of his retinue returned home after the incident. -The Plantagenet Ancestry, Baltimore, 1975, Turton, Lt.Col. W. H. 167.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Châtillon-23
