1 OCCU Comte d'Artois, "The Good"; Crusader KIA, COUNT OF ARTOIS, Count of Artois, Comte d' Artois, descendant of King Phillippe of France, soldier?, King of France
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Robert de France, Comte d'Artois
2 CONT
2 CONT * [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_I,_Count_of_Artois '''Robert I, Count of Artois''']
2 CONT * https://www.familysearch.org/en/tree/person/9HPG-8RZ
2 CONT and in French:  [http://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Ier_d%27Artois '''Robert I d'Artois''']
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert I (1216 – 8 February 1250), called the Good, was the first Count of Artois, the fifth (and second surviving) son of [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louis_VIII_of_France '''Louis VIII of France'''] and [http://en.wikipedia.org
2 CONC /wiki/Blanche_of_Castile '''Blanche of Castile'''].
2 CONT
2 CONT ====Life====
2 CONT He received Artois as an appanage, in accordance with the will of his father (died 1226) on attaining his majority in 1237 (aged twenty-one). In 1240 Pope Gregory IX, in conflict with the Emperor Frederick II, offered to crown Rob
2 CONC ert as emperor in opposition to Frederick, but the French count refuse to pretend to such a title.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT On 14 June 1237 Robert married Matilda, daughter of Henry II of Brabant and Marie of Hohenstaufen.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT They had two children:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Blanche (1248–1302)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Robert II (1250–1302), who succeeded to Artois after Robert I was killed in Egypt during the Seventh Crusade, led by his brother Louis IX of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Death
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert died while leading a reckless attack on Al Mansurah. He and the Templars accompanying the expedition charged into the town and became trapped in the narrow streets. According to Jean de Joinville, he defended himself for so
2 CONC me time in a house there, but was at last overpowered and killed. According to Matthew Paris, he fled in disgrace at the height of the battle , and drowned while trying to cross a river named Thanis (a branch of the Nile).
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert de Valois
2 CONT
2 CONT Count of Artois
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse 	Matilda of Brabant
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanche of Artois
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert II of Artois
2 CONT
2 CONT Noble family 	Valois
2 CONT
2 CONT Father 	Louis VIII of France
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother 	Blanche of Castile
2 CONT
2 CONT Born 	September 25, 1216
2 CONT
2 CONT Died 	February 8, 1250
2 CONT
2 CONT Un article de Wikipédia, l'encyclopédie libre.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Ier_d%27Artois
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Ier d'Artois est né en 1216 et mort à Mansourah le 9 février 1250. Comte d'Artois (1237-1250), il est le troisième fils de Louis VIII et de Blanche de Castille et le frère de Saint Louis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marié à Mathilde, fille d'Henri II de Brabant et de Marie de Hohenstaufen, il eut :
2 CONT
2 CONT #Blanche (1248 † 1302), mariée avec le roi Henri Ier de Navarre puis avec Edmund de Lancastre, un prince cadet anglais.
2 CONT
2 CONT #Robert II (1250 †1302)
2 CONT
2 CONT Suivant les dispositions testamentaires de son père, mort en 1226, il reçoit en 1237, c'est-à-dire à sa majorité, le comté d'Artois en apanage. En 1240, le pape Grégoire IX, en lutte contre Frédéric II de Hohenstaufen, lui offre l
2 CONC a couronne impériale, mais il refuse de prétendre à l'empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT En 1249, il prend part à la septième croisade aux côtés de son frère Louis IX et participa à la prise de Damiette, le 6 juin 1250. Puis l'armée croisée marche sur Mansourah. Le 8 février 1250, elle traverse un gué sur le Bahr al-S
2 CONC aghîr. Robert d'Artois commande l'avant-garde, composée des Templiers, et défait le corps de troupe musulman qui défend la rive. Au lieu d'attendre le gros des troupes, et contre l'avis de Guillaume de Sonnac, maître du Temple, il
2 CONC  se lance à l'assaut de Mansourah, commence à mettre l'armée égyptienne en déroute et fait irruption dans Mansourah. Mais Baybars, un chef mamelouk, organise une contre attaque, bloque les rues de Mansourah avec des barricades et 
2 CONC massacre les 280 chevaliers de l'avant-garde, y compris Robert d'Artois.
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://homepages.rootsweb.com/~cousin/html/p395.htm#i5149
2 CONT
2 CONT Arms: D'azur semé de fleurs-de-lis d'or; au lambel de gueules en chef, chaque pendant chargé de trois tours d'or, rangées en pal.4 Robert I, comte d' Artois was born in 1216. He was the son of Louis VIII "le Lion", roi de France a
2 CONC nd Blanche Alfonsez de Castilla.3 Robert I, comte d' Artois was born in September 1216.5 He witnessed the will of Louis VIII "le Lion", roi de France in June 1225; Provided for his brother, Philippe Hurepel (Domfront and Mortain),
2 CONC  and sons Robert (Artois), Jean (Anjou and Maine), Alphonse (Poitou and Auvergne). Introduced for the first time the restriction that the lands return to the Crown in the absence of direct heir. The restriction was not consistentl
2 CONC y inserted in the actual letters patent at first, but the Parliment of Paris in 1284 denied Charles d'Anjou's right to inherit his brother's domain of Poitou as collateral heir. Thus the apanage was born.6 Comte d'Artois in 1237.7
2 CONC  Robert I, comte d' Artois married Mahaud de Brabant, daughter of Henri II "le Magnanime", duc de Brabant and Maria von Schwaben, on 14 June 1237 at Campiegne; Her 1st. From this union descend the Counts of Artois.8,9 Robert I, co
2 CONC mte d' Artois was joined the Eqyptian Crusade of his brother, St. Louis, in 1249. 7th Crusade: To Muslim Egypt. Between 1249 and 1250.10 He died on 9 February 1250 at al-Mansurah, Egypt, at age 34 years. Robert of Artois led a sur
2 CONC prise attack on the Egyptian camp two miles (three kilometres) from al-Mansurah and, rejecting the advice of more experienced campaigners, impetuously moved ahead, only to be trapped within the city. Many knights lost their lives.
2 CONC 11 He was ancestor of the House of Artois (to 1472).7
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert I "the Good" (1216 – February 8, 1250) was Count of Artois. He was the fifth (and second surviving) son of King Louis VIII of France and Blanche of Castile.
2 CONT
2 CONT On June 14, 1237, Robert married Matilda of Brabant, daughter of Henry II, Duke of Brabant and Marie of Hohenstaufen. They had two children:
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. Blanche of Artois (1248–1302)
2 CONT
2 CONT    2. Robert II of Artois (1250–1302), Count of Artois
2 CONT
2 CONT He was killed in Egypt during the Seventh Crusade of his brother Louis IX of France, while leading a reckless attack on Al Mansurah. He and the Templars accompanying the expedition charged into the town and became trapped in the n
2 CONC arrow streets. According to Jean de Joinville, he defended himself for some time in a house there, but was at last overpowered and killed. According to Matthew Paris, he fled in disgrace at the height of the battle , and drowned w
2 CONC hile trying to cross a river named Thanis ( a branch of the Nile ).
2 CONT
