2 CONT
2 CONT Robert I de France, Comte d'Artois 
2 CONT
2 CONT M, #102536, b. September 1216, d. 9 February 1250
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert I de France, Comte d'Artois|b. Sep 1216\nd. 9 Feb 1250|p10254.htm#i102536|Louis VIII, Roi de France|b. 5 Sep 1187\nd. 8 Nov 1226|p10239.htm#i102383|Blanca de Castilla|b. c 1183\nd. 1 Dec 1252|p10239.htm#i102384|Philippe I. 
2 CONC A., Roi de France|b. 21 Aug 1165\nd. 14 Jul 1223|p10310.htm#i103091|Isabelle de Hainaut|b. c 1170\nd. 15 Mar 1190|p10312.htm#i103117|Alfonso V., Rey de Castilla|b. 11 Nov 1155\nd. 22 Sep 1214|p10217.htm#i102162|Eleanor Plantagenet
2 CONC |b. 13 Oct 1162\nd. 31 Oct 1214|p10202.htm#i102020|
2 CONT
2 CONT Last Edited=23 Oct 2007
2 CONT
2 CONT Consanguinity Index=0.61%
2 CONT
2 CONT      Robert I de France, Comte d'Artois was born in September 1216. He was the son of Louis VIII, Roi de France and Blanca de Castilla. He married Matilde de Brabant, daughter of Henri II, Duc de Brabant and Marie von Hohenstaufen
2 CONC , in 1237.1 He died on 9 February 1250 at age 33 at Mansourah, Egypt, killed.
2 CONT
2 CONT      Robert I de France, Comte d'Artois gained the title of Comte d'Artois.2
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Robert I de France, Comte d'Artois and Matilde de Brabant
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanche d'Artois+ b. bt 1245 - 1250, d. 2 May 1302 
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert, Comte d'Artois+ b. 1250, d. 1302 
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT
2 CONT [S16] Jirí Louda and Michael MacLagan, Lines of Succession: Heraldry of the Royal Families of Europe, 2nd edition (London, U.K.: Little, Brown and Company, 1999), table 64. Hereinafter cited as Lines of Succession. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [S11] Alison Weir, Britain's Royal Family: A Complete Genealogy (London, U.K.: The Bodley Head, 1999), page 74. Hereinafter cited as Britain's Royal Family. 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert I "the Good" (1216 – February 8, 1250) was Count of Artois. He was the fifth (and second surviving) son of King Louis VIII of France and Blanche of Castile.
2 CONT
2 CONT On June 14, 1237, Robert married Matilda of Brabant, daughter of Henry II, Duke of Brabant and Marie of Hohenstaufen. They had two children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanche of Artois (1248–1302) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert II of Artois (1250–1302), Count of Artois 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was killed in Egypt during the Seventh Crusade of his brother Louis IX of France, while leading a reckless attack on Al Mansurah. He and the Templars accompanying the expedition charged into the town and became trapped in the n
2 CONC arrow streets. According to Jean de Joinville, he defended himself for some time in a house there, but was at last overpowered and killed.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert I "the Good" (1216 – February 8, 1250) was Count of Artois. He was the fifth (and second surviving) son of King Louis VIII of France and Blanche of Castile.
2 CONT
2 CONT On June 14, 1237, Robert married Matilda of Brabant, daughter of Henry II, Duke of Brabant, and they had two children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanche of Artois (1248–1302) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert II of Artois (1250–1302), Count of Artois 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was killed in Egypt during the Seventh Crusade of his brother Louis IX of France, while leading a reckless attack on Al Mansurah. He and the Templars accompanying the expedition charged into the town and became trapped in the n
2 CONC arrow streets. According to Jean de Joinville, he defended himself for some time in a house there, but was at last overpowered and killed.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert I of Artois
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert I "the Good" (1216 – February 8, 1250) was Count of Artois. He was the fifth (and second surviving) son of King Louis VIII of France and Blanche of Castile.
2 CONT
2 CONT On June 14, 1237, Robert married Matilda of Brabant, daughter of Henry II,
2 CONT
2 CONT Duke of Brabant, and they had two children:
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. Blanche of Artois (1248–1302)
2 CONT
2 CONT    2. Robert II of Artois (1250–1302), Count of Artois
2 CONT
2 CONT He was killed in Egypt during the Seventh Crusade of his brother Louis IX of France, while leading a reckless attack on Al Mansurah. He and the Templars accompanying the expedition charged into the town and became trapped in the n
2 CONC arrow streets. According to Jean de Joinville, he defended himself for some time in a house there, but was at last overpowered and killed.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_I_of_Artois
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT  Descendant of Charlemagne
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert I (1216 – 8 February 1250), called the Good, was the first Count of Artois , the fifth (and second surviving) son of Louis VIII of France and Blanche of Castile . He received Artois as an appanage , in accordance with the w
2 CONC ill of his father (died 1226) on attaining his majority in 1237 (aged twenty-one). In 1240 Pope Gregory IX , in conflict with the Emperor Frederick II , offered to crown Robert as emperor in opposition to Frederick, but the French
2 CONC  count refuse to pretend to such a title.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 14 June 1237 Robert married Matilda , daughter of Henry II of Brabant  and Marie of Hohenstaufen . They had two children: Blanche (1248–1302) and Robert II  (1250–1302), who succeeded to Artois after Robert I was killed in Egyp
2 CONC t  during the Seventh Crusade , led by his brother Louis IX of France . Robert died while leading a reckless attack on Al Mansurah . He and the Templars  accompanying the expedition charged into the town and became trapped in the 
2 CONC narrow streets. According to Jean de Joinville , he defended himself for some time in a house there, but was at last overpowered and killed. According to Matthew Paris , he fled in disgrace at the height of the battle , and drowne
2 CONC d while trying to cross a river named Thanis (a branch of the Nile ).
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert I (1216 – 8 February 1250), called the Good, was the first Count of Artois, the fifth (and second surviving) son of Louis VIII of France and Blanche of Castile.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT
2 CONT He received Artois as an appanage, in accordance with the will of his father (died 1226) on attaining his majority in 1237 (aged twenty-one). In 1240 Pope Gregory IX, in conflict with the Emperor Frederick II, offered to crown Rob
2 CONC ert as emperor in opposition to Frederick, but the French count refuse to pretend to such a title.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT On 14 June 1237 Robert married Matilda, daughter of Henry II of Brabant and Marie of Hohenstaufen.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT They had two children:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Blanche (1248–1302)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Robert II (1250–1302), who succeeded to Artois after Robert I was killed in Egypt during the Seventh Crusade, led by his brother Louis IX of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert died while leading a reckless attack on Al Mansurah. He and the Templars accompanying the expedition charged into the town and became trapped in the narrow streets. According to Jean de Joinville, he defended himself for so
2 CONC me time in a house there, but was at last overpowered and killed. According to Matthew Paris, he fled in disgrace at the height of the battle , and drowned while trying to cross a river named Thanis (a branch of the Nile).
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Floris IV (24 June 1210 The Hague–19 July 1234, Corbie, France), Count of Holland from 1222 to 1234. He was a son of William I of Holland and Adelaide of Geldern.
2 CONT
2 CONT Floris IV succeeded his father in 1222. His regent was Baldwin of Bentheim. He acquired the Land of Altena. He had constant disputes with the bishop of Utrecht, Otto of Lippe, but helped him against the peasants of Drenthe in 1227
2 CONC . Floris was a real knight, who went on crusade against the Stedingers north of Bremen in 1234. He died at a French tourney.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Family and children
2 CONT
2 CONT Coats of Arms of the Counts of Holland.He married before 6 December 1214 Matilde of Brabant, daughter of Duke Henry I of Brabant and Maud of Boulogne and Alsace. Their children were:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.William II, Count of Holland (1227–1256).
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.Floris (ca. 1228 - 1258), Regent of Holland in 1256-1258.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.Adelaide of Holland (ca. 1230–1284), married, in Frankfurt am Main in 1246, Jean I of Avesnes, Count of Hainaut. Their descendants eventually inherited Holland. She was Regent of Holland in 1258-1263.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.Margaret (d. 1277), married ca. 1249 to Count Hermann I of Henneberg.
2 CONT
2 CONT 5.Mechtild
2 CONT
