1 NOTE Albert I of Austria, son of Rudolf I of the House of Hapsburg, succeeded Adolf of Nassau as ruler of the Imperial kingdom of Germany in 1298.  He ruled until 1308.  His successor was Henry VII of Luxemburg.
1 NOTE Following a revolt in the Rhineland Albert I was assassinated by accomplices of his nephew John "the Parracide" of Swabia.  Albert I was succeeded by Henry VII.
1 NOTE Murdered when crossing the Reuss
1 NOTE Albrecht I of Habsburg (July 1255 - May 1, 1308), sometimes named as Albert I, was King of the Romans, Duke of Austria, and eldest son of German King Rudolph I of Habsburg and Gertrude of Hohenburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT The founder of the great house of Habsburg was invested with the duchies of Austria and Styria, together with his brother Rudolph II, in 1282. In 1283 his father entrusted him with their sole government, and he appears to have rul
2 CONC ed them with conspicuous success. Rudolph I was unable to secure the succession to the German throne for his son, and on his death in 1291, the princes, fearing Albert's power, chose Adolf of Nassau-Weilburg as king. A rising amon
2 CONC g his Swabian dependents compelled Albert to recognize the sovereignty of his rival, and to confine himself for a time to the government of the Habsburg territories.
2 CONT
2 CONT He did not abandon his hopes of the throne, however, which were eventually realised. In 1298, he was chosen German king by some of the princes, who were dissatisfied with Adolf. The armies of the rival kings met at the Battle of G
2 CONC öllheim near Worms, where Adolf was defeated and slain. Submitting to a new election but securing the support of several influential princes by making extensive promises, he was chosen at Frankfurt on July 27, 1298, and crowned a
2 CONC t Aachen on August 24.
2 CONT
2 CONT Albert married Elizabeth, daughter of Meinhard II, count of Gorizia and Tyrol, who was a descendant of the Babenberg margraves of Austria who predated the Habsburgs' rule. The baptismal name Leopold, patron saint margrave of Austr
2 CONC ia, was given to one of their sons. Elisabeth was in fact better connected to mighty German rulers than her husband: a descendant of earlier kings, for example Emperor Henry IV, she was also a niece of dukes of Bavaria, Austria's 
2 CONC important neighbors.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although a hard, stern man, Albert had a keen sense of justice when his own interests were not involved, and few of the German kings possessed so practical an intelligence. He encouraged the cities, and not content with issuing pr
2 CONC oclamations against private war, formed alliances with the princes in order to enforce his decrees. The serfs, whose wrongs seldom attracted notice in an age indifferent to the claims of common humanity, found a friend in this sev
2 CONC ere monarch, and he protected even the despised and persecuted Jews. Stories of his cruelty and oppression in the Swiss cantons did not appear until the 16th century, and are now regarded as legendary.
2 CONT
2 CONT Albert sought to play an important part in European affairs. He seemed at first inclined to press a quarrel with France over the Burgundian frontier, but the refusal of Pope Boniface VIII to recognize his election led him to chang
2 CONC e his policy, and, in 1299, he made a treaty with Philip IV of France, by which his son Rudolph was to marry Blanche, a daughter of the French king. He afterwards became estranged from Philip, but in 1303, Boniface recognized him 
2 CONC as German king and future emperor; in return, Albert recognized the authority of the pope alone to bestow the imperial crown, and promised that none of his sons should be elected German king without papal consent.
2 CONT
2 CONT Albert had failed in his attempt to seize Holland and Zeeland, as vacant fiefs of the Empire, on the death of Count John I in 1299, but in 1306 he secured the crown of Bohemia for his son Rudolph on the death of King Wenceslaus II
2 CONC I. He also renewed the claim made by his predecessor, Adolf, on Thuringia, and interfered in a quarrel over the succession to the Hungarian throne. His attack on Thuringia ended in his defeat at Lucka in 1307 and, in the same year
2 CONC , the death of his son Rudolph weakened his position in eastern Europe. His action in abolishing all tolls established on the Rhine since 1250, led the Rhenish archbishops and the count palatine of the Rhine to form a league again
2 CONC st him. Aided by the towns, however, he soon crushed the rising.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was on the way to suppress a revolt in Swabia when he was murdered on May 1, 1308, at Windisch on the Reuss River, by his nephew John of Swabi, afterwards called "the Parricide" or "John Parricida", whom he had deprived of his 
2 CONC inheritance.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT Albert and his wife Elizabeth had twelve children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Rudolph III (ca. 1282 - 4 July 1307, Hora?dovice), Married but line extinct and predeceased his father. 
2 CONT Frederick I (1289 - 13 January 1330, Gutenstein). Married but line extinct. 
2 CONT Leopold I (4 August 1290 - 28 February 1326, Strassburg). Married but line extinct. 
2 CONT Albert II (12 December 1298, Vienna - 20 July 1358, Vienna). 
2 CONT Heinrich (1299 - 3 February 1327, Bruck an der Mur). Married but line extinct. 
2 CONT Meinhard, 1300 died young. 
2 CONT Otto (23 July 1301, Vienna - 26 February 1339, Vienna). Married but line extinct. 
2 CONT Anna 1280?, Vienna - 19 March 1327, Breslau), married: 
2 CONT in Graz ca. 1295 to Herman, Margrave of Brandenburg-Salzwedel; 
2 CONT in Breslau 1310 to Duke Henry VI the Good. 
2 CONT Agnes (18 May 1281 - 10 June 1364, Königsfelden), married in Vienna 13 February 1296 King Andrew III of Hungary. 
2 CONT Elisabeth (d. 19 May 1353), married 1304 Frederick IV, Duke of Lorraine. 
2 CONT Katharina (1295 - 18 January 1323, Naples), married Charles, Duke of Calabria in 1316. 
2 CONT Jutta (d. 1329), married Ludwig V, Count of Öttingen in Baden, 26 March 1319. 
1 NOTE DUKE OF AUSTRIA 1282-1298; KING OF AUSTRIA 1298-1308
