2 CONT https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anne_of_Austria,_Margravine_of_Brandenburg
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna of Austria (1275, Vienna, Austria – 1327, Legnica) was a daughter of Albert I of Germany and his wife Elisabeth of Tirol. She was a member of the House of Habsburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tenure
2 CONT 1295–1327
2 CONT Born 1275 Vienna, Austria
2 CONT Died 1327
2 CONT Legnica
2 CONT Spouse
2 CONT Herman, Margrave of Brandenburg-Salzwedel
2 CONT Henry VI the Good
2 CONT Issue
2 CONT Judith, Count of Henneberg
2 CONT John V, Margrave of Brandenburg-Salzwedel
2 CONT Matilde, Duchess of Greater Poland
2 CONT Agnes, Margravine of Brandenburg
2 CONT Elisabeth, Duchess of Oleśnica
2 CONT Euphemia
2 CONT Margaret
2 CONT House
2 CONT House of Habsburg
2 CONT
2 CONT Father
2 CONT Albert I of Germany
2 CONT Mother
2 CONT Elisabeth of Tirol
2 CONT
2 CONT First marriage
2 CONT Anna first married in 1295 in Graz. Her husband was Herman, Margrave of Brandenburg-Salzwedel.
2 CONT
2 CONT They had four children:
2 CONT Jutta of Brandenburg (1297–1353), heiress of Coburg, married to Count Henry VIII of Henneberg
2 CONT John (1302–1317), succeeded his father, but died young
2 CONT Matilde of Brandenburg (died 1323) married Henry IV the Faithful
2 CONT Agnes of Brandenburg (1297–1334), heiress of the Altmark, married with margrave Waldemar of Brandenburg (1281–1381) and in 1319 to Duke Otto of Brunswick-Göttingen (1290–1344).
2 CONT In 1308, Herman died, and their son John succeeded him.
2 CONT
2 CONT Second marriage
2 CONT In 1310 Anna married Henry VI the Good, Duke of Wrocław,[1][better source needed] son of Henry V the Fat and his wife Elisabeth of Greater Poland.[citation needed] 
2 CONT
2 CONT They had three daughters:
2 CONT Elisabeth of Brieg (ca. 1311 – 20 February? 1328), married before 10 January 1322 to Duke Konrad I of Oleśnica.
2 CONT Euphemia of Brieg (Ofka) (ca. 1312 – 21 March after 1384), married before 29 November 1325 to Duke Bolesław the Elder of Niemodlin (Falkenberg).
2 CONT Margaret of Brieg (ca. 1313 – 8 March 1379), Abbess of St. Clara in Wrocław (1359).
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna died in 1327 in Legnica. When Henry died ten years later without any male heirs, Wrocław was merged with the Bohemian crown.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------
