1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Amicie de Courtenay''' (1253–1275) was a French noblewoman and a member of the Capetian House of Courtenay, a cadet line of the House of Capet.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amicie_de_Courtenay
2 CONT
2 CONT Her father was Peter of Courtenay,[1] Lord of Conches and Mehun (1218-1250); he fell in the Battle of Al Mansurah during the Seventh Crusade. Amicie's mother was Pétronille of Joigny, the daughter of Gaucher de Joigny and Amicie d
2 CONC e Montfort.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT She married Count Robert II of Artois (1250-1302),[1] together they had three children:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Mahaut (1268–1329)[1]
2 CONT # Philip (1269–1298)[1]
2 CONT # Robert (1271–1272)
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert II of Artois
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1262 in Paris Robert married Amicie de Courtenay (1250–1275), by whom he had three children: Mahaut (1268–1329), Philip (1269–1298), and Robert (born 1271, died young).
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References
2 CONT # to: a b c d Dunbabin 2011, p. xiii.
2 CONT # Dunbabin 2011, p. xv.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Sources
2 CONT # Dunbabin, Jean (2011). The French in the Kingdom of Sicily, 1266–1305. Cambridge University Press.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Links
2 CONT * http://washington.ancestryregister.com/CAPET00006.htm
2 CONT --------------------
