1 NOTE Henry VII, German emperor (1308-13), son of the Count of Luxemburg, was born in 1269. He died in Italy, whither he had gone to establish the imperial authority, disputed by the Pope, and by the Anjou dynasty in Naples. [World Wide
2 CONC  Illustrated Encyclopedia, 1935]
1 NOTE "OF GERMANY"
1 NOTE Henry VII (Holy Roman Empire), called Henry of Luxemburg (circa 
2 CONT 1275-1313), German king and Holy Roman emperor (1308-13), first of the 
2 CONT line of Luxemburg, born in Buonconvento, Italy. On the death of Emperor 
2 CONT Albert I, in 1308, Henry was elected by the German princes to succeed him. 
2 CONT In 1310 Henry entered Italy; he was crowned king of the Lombards the 
2 CONT following year and Holy Roman emperor in 1312. While in Italy he attempted 
2 CONT to end the strife between the Guelphs and the Ghibellines. Declaring 
2 CONT himself to be impartial, he secured the homage of both factions; but the 
2 CONT Guelphs soon rebelled, and Henry was forced to side with the Ghibellines. 
2 CONT Allied with Frederick II, king of Sicily, in 1313 Henry was preparing an 
2 CONT attack on the Guelph leader Robert, duke of Anjou and king of Naples 
2 CONT (1275-1343), when he died. He was succeeded as emperor by Louis IV.
