2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:  
2 CONT * 1. [http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=gonefishin&id=I098325]
2 CONT * 2.  [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hugh_Bigod_(Justiciar)]
2 CONT * 3- http://www.tudorplace.com.ar/BIGOD.htm#John BIGOD of Tuttington1
2 CONT
2 CONT Note:
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh Bigod (c. 1211 – 1266) was Justiciar of England from 1258 to 1260.[1] He was a younger son of Hugh Bigod, 3rd Earl of Norfolk.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1258 the Provisions of Oxford established a baronial government of which Hugh's elder brother Roger Bigod, 4th Earl of Norfolk was a leading member, and Hugh was appointed Chief Justiciar. He also had wardship of the Tower of L
2 CONC ondon, and, briefly, of Dover Castle. But at the end of 1260 or in early 1261 he resigned these offices, apparently due to dissatisfaction with the new government. Thus in 1263 he joined the royalists, and was present on that side
2 CONC  at the Battle of Lewes.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1243 Hugh married Joan de Stuteville, niece of Dervorguilla of Galloway and widow of Hugh Wake, Lord of Bourne.[3] Together, Hugh and Joan had at least eight children. Their eldest son Roger, subsequently became Earl of Norfolk
2 CONC .[2] There is no contemporary evidence for the assertion, first recorded in the seventeenth century, that he had an earlier wife called Joanna Burnard (or Burnet or Burnell); if indeed a Hugh Bigod married Joanna, it probably was 
2 CONC his father that did so.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh Bigod (c. 1211 – 1266) was Justiciar of England from 1258 to 1260.[1] He was a younger son of Hugh Bigod, 3rd Earl of Norfolk.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1258 the Provisions of Oxford established a baronial government of which Hugh's elder brother Roger Bigod, 4th Earl of Norfolk was a leading member, and Hugh was appointed Chief Justiciar. He also had wardship of the Tower of L
2 CONC ondon, and, briefly, of Dover Castle. But at the end of 1260 or in early 1261 he resigned these offices, apparently due to dissatisfaction with the new government. Thus in 1263 he joined the royalists, and was present on that side
2 CONC  at the Battle of Lewes.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1243 Hugh married Joan de Stuteville, niece of Dervorguilla of Galloway and widow of Hugh Wake, Lord of Bourne.[3] Together, Hugh and Joan had at least eight children. Their eldest son Roger, subsequently became Earl of Norfolk
2 CONC .[2] There is no contemporary evidence for the assertion, first recorded in the seventeenth century, that he had an earlier wife called Joanna Burnard (or Burnet or Burnell); if indeed a Hugh Bigod married Joanna, it probably was 
2 CONC his father that did so.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources: M. Morris, The Bigod Earls of Norfolk in the Thirteenth Century, pp. 54–5 1.^ a b "TITLE OF "JUSTICIAR" (PRIME MINISTER)". Baronial Order of Magna Charta. Retrieved 2008-02-15. 2.^ a b "Hugh le Bigod, Chief Justiciar of E
2 CONC ngland". My Lines. Retrieved 2008-02-27. 3.^ Richardson, D. (2011) Magna Carta Ancestry (via Google)
