2 TITL Filips de Goede - Wikipedia
1 NOTE duc de Bourgogne (1419-1467), comte de Flandre, d`Artois, de Boulogne og de Bourgogne (1419-1467), comte de Namur (1421-1467), duc de Brabant (1430), duc de Limbourg (1430-1467), comte de Hainaut, de Hollande og de ZΘlande (1428-1
2 CONC 467), duc de Luxembourg (1451-1467), chevalier de la Toison d`Or (1., 1430), souverain de la maison de Bourgogne (1.)
1 NOTE He was the creator of one of the most powerful states in 15th-century 
2 CONT Europe. Philip succeeded as duke when his father was killed by a group of 
2 CONT French nobles. In retaliation, Philip allied himself with Henry V of 
2 CONT England, France's enemy in the Hundred Years' War. In 1430 he captured 
2 CONT Joan of Arc, who had been leading the French, and turned her over to the 
2 CONT English. Later, when the war began to go against England, he changed 
2 CONT sides, concluded the Treaty of Arras (1435) with Charles VII of France, 
2 CONT and received in return most of the province of Picardy. Philip extended 
2 CONT Burgundian rule in the Netherlands, acquiring Holland, Zeeland, and 
2 CONT Hainaut in 1428; Brabant and Limburg in 1433; and Luxembourg in 1443. In 
2 CONT 1453 he conquered the rebellious towns of Flanders. By 1460 he ruled what 
2 CONT is now Belgium and Luxembourg, together with most of the Netherlands and 
2 CONT large areas of northern and eastern France. Philip's court was the most 
2 CONT brilliant of its time. Known for his lavish entertainments and his 
2 CONT devotion to the rituals of chivalry, he instituted (1430) the Order of the 
2 CONT Golden Fleece, one of Europe's most prestigious knightly brotherhoods. In 
2 CONT 1465 he handed the dukedom over to his son, Charles the Bold.
1 NOTE Pour venger l'assassinat de son páere, il s'allie aux Anglais et participe au traitâe de Troyes, qui dâeshâerite le Dauphin Charles (futur Charles VII) en 1420. Apráes avoir luttâe contre Charles VII, il se râeconcilie par la paix
2 CONC  d'Arras, en 1435, qui lui accorde les villes de la Somme et le dispense d'hommage de vassalitâe pour la Flandre.
2 CONT
2 CONT Il annexe presque tous les Pays-Bas : Brabant, Hainaut, Limbourg, Hollande, Zâelande, Luxembourg. Il impose son protectorat aux seigneuries ecclâesiastiques de Liáege, Cambrai, Utrecht.
2 CONT Il  constitue ainsi un âetat formâe de deux groupes de territoires sâeparâes par la Lorraine indâependante :
2 CONT - Les Pays de par-deðcáa (Pays-Bas)
2 CONT - Les Pays de par-deláa (Bourgogne).
2 CONT
2 CONT Alors maãitre de la Bourgogne, de la Franche-Comtâe, de la Flandre, de l'Artois et des provinces belges, il est le plus puissant souverain d'Europe.
2 CONT
2 CONT Il crâee l'ordre de la Toison d'Or.
