1 NAME Otto (Albert) /van Cuyck/ heer van Cuijk en Grave, heer van Mierlo, Neerloon, Zelem, Merum
2 GIVN Otto (Albert)
2 SURN van Cuyck
2 NSFX heer van Cuijk en Grave, heer van Mierlo, Neerloon, Zelem, Merum
2 _MARNM van Cuyck
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1260
2 ADDR
3 CITY Grave
3 STAE North Brabant
3 CTRY Netherlands
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1350
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Otto van Cuijk
2 CONT
2 CONT Otto was Lord of Cuijk, Merum, Neerloon, Mierlo and though three marriages also of Zelem and Heverlee. He also held senior positions in the Duchy of Brabant and was until 1350 a member of the Council of Brabant. He was even mentio
2 CONC ned as the highest advisor to the Duke. In 1338 he reprents the Duke at the Reichstag at Koblenz.
2 CONT
2 CONT He also was a diplomat and in 1307 he negotiated on behalf of the Duke with the city of Mechelen. He travelled to France, Namur, Gelre, England and Holland to arrange marriages, contracts, business deals, financial and military af
2 CONC fairs and truce. King Edward III had made him a permanent advisor and ambassador of his court. When Count (later Duke) Reinoud II of Gelre married the sister of the English King it was Otto who represented both England and Gelre i
2 CONC n the formulation of the marriage contract. This shows the complex relationships that existed in the Middle Ages. But all of this international work had a negative effect on the Land of Cuijk, slowly but surely it was loosing its 
2 CONC independent postion between the rising regional powers.
2 CONT
2 CONT For example, because of increased hostilities between Brabant and Gelre, Grave had become an even more strategic position for Brabant. As a result and, because Otto – thanks to his high profile work – was in permanent need of mone
2 CONC y he, in 1323, handed over Grave, for 5000 pounds,  to the Duke of Brabant to receive it back from him in fief. This was a reason for Emperor Louis of Bavaria to confirm his rights over the Land of Cuijk.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the same time he was happy to lend King Edward a hand in his 100 year war with France and amongst others he participated in the Battle of Crecy in1346.
2 CONT
2 CONT Otto died in 1350 without leaving a male heir behind. [11. Het Geslacht van Cuijk, Ger Graat, 2008, 21]
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Buddy, Paul. “Low Countries – River Lands – Paul Budde History, Philosophy, Culture.” Paulbuddehistory.com, 2025, paulbuddehistory.com/europe/14-low-countries-river-lands/. Accessed 4 Nov. 2025.
2 CONT …..
2 CONT 3.         OTTO van Kuyc (-1350).  Heer van Kuyc.  “...Heren Janne den Here van Cuyke...Heren Otten van Cuycke...” are named as present in a charter dated 7 Apr 1306 under which “Gheraerd Here van Hoirne” confirmed land to “Woldri
2 CONC chem“, as granted by “onse Here onse vader ende Hair Willaem onse broeder“[1323].  “Johannes dns de Cuyk” established jurisdictional limits in his lands, with the consent of “collateralis nostre...Juthe dne de Cuyk nec non filioru
2 CONC m nostrorum Johannis Sancti Servatii Trajectensis et Lovaniensis ecclesiarum ppti. et Ottonis dni de Zeelehem militis, ac nepotis nostri Johannis de Cuyk”, by charter dated 20 Apr 1308[1324].  The testament of “Jutta de Nassau rel
2 CONC icta domini Johannis quondam domini de Kuyc bone memorie”, dated 25 Jan 1312, bequeathed property to “dominum de Kuyc meum nepotem”, sealed by “dominorum Johannis...prepositi et Ottonis militis de Kuyc meorum liberorum, Johannis d
2 CONC omini de Kuyc mei nepotis”[1325].  “Otte Here van Kuyc” granted property “van den Ghestichte van Utrecht...” to “Heren Ghysebrecht Here van Yselsteyn” by charter dated 7 Nov 1319[1326].  “Otto Dominus de Kuyck et Heverle” conferre
2 CONC d “oppidum nostrum de Gravia” on Jean Duke of Brabant and received it back as a fief by charter dated 24 Jun 1323, witnessed by “Florentius ad prædicti Ottonis fratris Domini de Kuyck”[1327].  Heer van Mierlo en Zeelem.  "Conradus
2 CONC  dominus de Dycka" acknowledged holding “de fundo sive domistadio, supra quod castrum dictum Capelle abolim constructum fuit” in fief from “dominum Ottonem dominum de Cuke consanguineum nostrum”, by charter dated 11 Sep 1347[1328]
2 CONC .  The precise family relationship between Otto Heer van Kuyc and Konrad Herr von Dyck [see the document FRANCONIA LOWER RHINE] has not been ascertained but, as Otto’s nephew Jan van Kuyc-Hoogstraeten was also named as Konrad’s re
2 CONC lative 7 Jan 1351 (N.S.?) (see above), the connection was presumably through a relative who was common to both Otto and Jan.  
2 CONT m firstly (before 24 Jun 1323) ---.  The primary source which confirms the name of Otto’s first wife has not been identified.  Butkens names her “Jenne Dame de Heverle”[1329].  
2 CONT m secondly ---.  The primary source which confirms the name of Otto’s second wife has not been identified.  Butkens does not name a second wife.  
2 CONT m thirdly (1336) as her second husband, '''JEANNE de Flandre, widow of GERHARD Heer van Diest Borchgraeve van Antwerpen, daughter of GUILLAUME de Flandre Heer van Dendermonde & his wife Alix de Clermont-en-Beauvaisis (-after 1342)
2 CONC '''.  A register of fiefs compiled in 1408 by André de Maubeuge records that “Geert” married secondly “de dochter heer Willem van Vlaenderen”[1330].  The Chronicon Diestense records that “Gerardus primogenitus...domini Arnoldi” ma
2 CONC rried secondly “dominam Johannam de Flandria filiam Wilhelmi comitis appellati sonder lant”[1331].  "Gerardus dominus de Diest et castellanus Antverpiensis…necnon…domina Johanna de Flandria eius coniunx" founded the church of Zeel
2 CONC hem, for the souls of "dominæ Mariæ piæ memoriæ dominæ quondam de Diest", by charter dated 1 Feb 1328[1332].  “Johannes...episcopus Trajectensis, Thomas et Arnoldus milites et confratres” confirmed dower for life to “dominam et so
2 CONC rorem nostram dominam Johannam de Flandria dominam de Dyst, quondam...uxorem...fratris nostri domini Gerardi domini de Dyst bone memorie” by charter dated 14 May 1334[1333].  The Chronicon Diestense records that, after the death o
2 CONC f her first husband, “Johannam” married secondly “Ottoni domino de Cuyck”[1334]. 
2 CONT ('''Curator Note: note the difference in description of the 3rd wife, Johanna van Cuyck (van Dampierre van Flanders) vs 'JEANNE de Flandre, widow of GERHARD Heer van Diest Borchgraeve van Antwerpen, daughter of GUILLAUME de Flandr
2 CONC e Heer van Dendermonde & his wife Alix de Clermont-en-Beauvaisis.  Is this the same person?''')
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Internal Sourcing:'''
2 CONT [1323] Mieris (1754), Tweede deel, p. 53. 
2 CONT [1324] Wap (1858), Oorkonden, p. 245. 
2 CONT [1325] Wap (1858), Oorkonden, p. 257. 
2 CONT [1326] Mieris (1754), Tweede deel, p. 226. 
2 CONT [1327] Mieris (1754), Tweede deel, p. 319. 
2 CONT [1328] Niederrheins Urkundenbuch, Band III, 448, p. 360. 
2 CONT [1329] Butkens (1724), Vol. II, p. 57. 
2 CONT [1330] Raymaekers ‘Chronicon Diestense’ (1861), p. 404. 
2 CONT [1331] Raymaekers ‘Chronicon Diestense’ (1861), p. 405. 
2 CONT [1332] Foppens (1734), Tome III, Pars I, CLXXX, p. 157. 
2 CONT [1333] Raymaekers ‘Chronicon Diestense’ (1861), XLIV, p. 498. 
2 CONT [1334] Raymaekers ‘Chronicon Diestense’ (1861), p. 408. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Cawley, Charles. “DUTCH NOBILITY.” Fmg.ac, 2025, fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/DUTCH%20NOBILITY.htm#_Toc62491900.
