1 TITL graaf
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [http://www.theologienet.nl/documenten/Schelven%20Marnix.pdf '''Zie: Theologienet (Nederlands/Dutch info)]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT From the English Wikipedia page on Philips of Marnix, Lord of Saint-Aldegonde:
2 CONT * [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philip_van_Marnix '''Philip van Marnix''']
2 CONT
2 CONT Philips of Marnix, Lord of Saint-Aldegonde, Lord of West-Souburg (Dutch: Filips van Marnix, heer van Sint-Aldegonde, heer van West-Souburg, French: Philippe de Marnix, seigneur de Sainte-Aldegonde; Brussels, 7 March/20 July 1540 –
2 CONC  Leiden, 15 December 1598) was a Flemish and Dutch writer and statesman, and the probable author of the text of the Dutch national anthem, the Wilhelmus. He was born at Brussels, the son of Jacob of Marnix, baron of Pottes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography - career
2 CONT
2 CONT Marnix of St. Aldegonde studied theology under John Calvin and Theodore Beza at Geneva. Returning to the Netherlands in 1560, he threw himself energetically into the cause of the Reformation, taking an active part in the compromis
2 CONC e of the nobles in 1565 and the assembly of Sint-Truiden. He made himself conspicuous by issuing a pamphlet in justification of the iconoclastic movement Beeldenstorm which devastated many churches in Flanders in 1566, and on the 
2 CONC Duke of Alba's arrival next year had to flee the country.
2 CONT
2 CONT After spending some time in Friesland and in the Palatinate he was in 1570 taken into the service of William, prince of Orange, and in 1572 was sent as his representative to the first meeting of the States-General assembled at Dor
2 CONC drecht. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1573 he was taken prisoner by the Spaniards at Maaslandsluys, but was exchanged in the following year. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was sent as the representative of the insurgent provinces to Paris and London, where he attempted in vain to secure the effective assistance of Queen, Elizabeth I of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1578 he was at the Diet of Worms, where he made an eloquent, but fruitless, appeal for aid to the German princes. Equally vain were his efforts in the same year to persuade the magistrates of Ghent to cease persecuting the Cath
2 CONC olics in the city. He took a conspicuous part in arranging the Union of Utrecht. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1583 was chosen burgomaster of Antwerp. In 1585 he surrendered the city, after the months' siege of Antwerp, to the Spaniards. Attacked by the English and by his own countrymen for this act, he retired from public affairs and, 
2 CONC save for a mission to Paris in 1590, lived henceforth in Leiden or on his estate in Zeeland, where he worked at a translation of the Bible. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died at Leiden on the 15th of December 1598.
2 CONT
2 CONT Literary work
2 CONT
2 CONT St. Aldegonde, or Marnix (by which name he is very commonly known), is celebrated for his share in the great development of Dutch literature which followed the classical period represented by such writers as the poet and historian
2 CONC  Pieter Hooft. Of his works, the best known is the Roman Bee-hive (De roomsche byen-korf), published in 1569 during his exile in Friesland, a bitter satire on the faith and practices of the Roman Catholic Church. This was translat
2 CONC ed, or adapted, in French, German and English. He also wrote an educational treatise dedicated to John, Count of Nassau. 
2 CONT
2 CONT As a poet, St. Aldegonde is mainly known through his metrical translation of the Psalms (1580/1591); and, the Dutch national anthem Wilhelmus van Nassouwe is also ascribed to him. His complete works, edited by Lacroix and Quinet, 
2 CONC were published at Brussels in 7 volumes (1855-1859), and his religious and theological writings, edited by Van Toorenenbergen, at The Hague, in 4 volumes (1871-1891).
2 CONT
2 CONT Marnix wrote one of the earliest Bible translations into Dutch.
2 CONT
2 CONT Less known to the general public is his work as a cryptographer. St. Aldegonde is considered to be the first Dutch cryptographer (cfr. The Codebreakers). For Stadholder William the Silent, he deciphered secret messages that were i
2 CONC ntercepted from the Spaniards. His interest in cryptography possibly shows in the Wilhelmus, where the first letters of the couplets form the name Willem van Nassov, i.e. William 'the Silent' of Nassau, the Prince of Orange, but s
2 CONC uch musical games -often far more intricate- were commonly practiced by polyphony composers since the Gothic period. There is a marble sculpture of him by Paul de Vigne in Brussels.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources and references
2 CONT
2 CONT This article incorporates text from a publication now in the public domain: Chisholm, Hugh, ed (1911). Encyclopædia Britannica (eleventh ed.). Cambridge University Press.
2 CONT
2 CONT E. Quinet, Marnix de St Aldegonde (Paris, 1854)
2 CONT
2 CONT Juste, Vie de Marnix (The Hague, 1858); Frédéricq, Marnix en zijnenederlandsche geschriften (Ghent, 1882)
2 CONT
2 CONT Tjalma, Philips van Marnix, heer van Sint-Aldegonde (Amsterdam, 1896)
2 CONT
2 CONT 'On the Education of Youth', trans. Robert de Rycke in 'History of Education Quarterly', Summer 1970)
