1 OCCU Countess of Brienne and Lecce, Countess of Brienne, Lecce, and Conversano, Conversano, and Lecce (1356 - 1360), and Conversano (Sep. 19, 1356 - 1360); Countess consort of Enghein, Contessa of Brienne and Conversano
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isabella,_Countess_of_Brienne
2 CONT --------
2 CONT
2 CONT Duchess Isabella of Brienne (1306- 1360) was Countess of Lecce and Conversano, claimant to the Duchy of Athens and Kingdom of Jerusalem, etc.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was daughter of Walter V of Brienne, Duke of Athens, who was killed at the Battle of Halmyros near Thebes, Greece, in 1311. As the granddaughter of Hugh of Brienne, Count of Lecce etc, she was a descendant of the Kings of Jeru
2 CONC salem and of Cyprus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her children were:
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. Walter of Enghien (June 5, 1322 – November 18, 1340)
2 CONT
2 CONT    2. Isabeau of Enghien (d. December 28, 1357), Abbess of Flines
2 CONT
2 CONT    3. Sohier of Enghien (d. March 21, 1364), Count of Brienne, titular Duke of Athens.
2 CONT
2 CONT    4. John of Enghien, (d. 1380), Count of Lecce and Lord of Castro
2 CONT
2 CONT    5. Marguerite of Enghien, married Pierre de Préaux
2 CONT
2 CONT    6. Louis of Enghien (d. March 17, 1394), Lord (later Count) of Conversano, later Count of Brienne and titular Duke of Athens. Married Giovanna of Sanseverino, by whom he had four daughters, including his heiress, Marguerite, wh
2 CONC o in her own turn married John of Luxembourg, Sire of Beauvois. Mary, Queen of Scots and Queen consort Elizabeth Woodville were notable descendants.
2 CONT
2 CONT    7. Jacques of Enghien, a canon in Liège
2 CONT
2 CONT    8. Guy of Enghien,(d. 1377), Lord of Argos and Nauplia
2 CONT
2 CONT    9. Engelbert I of Enghien (c. 1330–February 20, 1403), Lord of Ramerupt, La Follie, and Seneffe
2 CONT
2 CONT   10. Françoise of Enghien, married Peter, Count of Montebello
2 CONT
2 CONT   11. Jeanne of Enghien, a nun at Flines
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Isabella of Brienne
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Duchess Isabella of Brienne (1306- 1360) was Countess of Lecce and Conversano, claimant to the Duchy of Athens and Kingdom of Jerusalem, etc.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was daughter of Walter V of Brienne, Duke of Athens, who was killed at the Battle of Halmyros near Thebes, Greece, in 1311. As the granddaughter of Hugh of Brienne, Count of Lecce etc, she was a descendant of the Kings of Jeru
2 CONC salem and of Cyprus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her father's life was largely spent in Greece, where he tried to win back his mother's inheritance, the Duchy of Athens. Walter V of Brienne hired the Catalan Company, a group of mercenaries founded by Roger des Flors, to fight ag
2 CONC ainst the Byzantine successor states of Epirus and Nikaia, but when he tried to cheat and kill them in 1311, they slew him at the Battle of Halmyros and took over the Duchy, making Catalan the official language and replacing the F
2 CONC rench and Byzantine-derived laws of the Principality of Achaea with the laws of Catalonia, soon making it up as Duchy of Neopatras. When the duke Gauthier was killed, Isabella's mother Jeanne de Chatillon (died 1354), daughter of 
2 CONC count of Porcien, may have tried to hold the Acropolis of Athens against the attacking catalans, but eventually surrendered it. The Brienne family retainers continued to hold Argos and Nauplia under Walter of Foucherolles. Duchess
2 CONC  Joanna however fled with her two young children to France, where the family had properties in Champagne, around Brienne-le-Chateau. The impoverished family was not able to provide better, and Isabella married a Walloon knight, Wa
2 CONC lter III of Enghien, whose lordships in Hainaut and therearound were not unsubstantial (Condé, Enghien).
2 CONT
2 CONT Her brother Walter VI of Brienne gradually gained better positions, and by allying with the Angevin kings of Naples, recovered some Italian fiefs. As constable of France, he was killed in the battle of Poitiers in 1356. He had als
2 CONC o been Lord of Florence, Marshal of France, Count of Lecce, Conversano etc. He was an ally of the Angevins of Naples, and participated their policies in Italy. His children died young, both with his first wife Margherita of Anjou-
2 CONC Tarent and his second, his distant kinswoman Jeanne de Brienne. His sister Isabella's children therefore succeeded to his possessions and claims. Already by 1350, Isabella's heir Sohier of Enghien resided in Greece and held Argos 
2 CONC and Nauplia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella survived her brother, whom she succeeded, and died 1360. Her husband Walter of Enghien had died in 1345.
2 CONT
2 CONT For a few years, she was Countess of Lecce and Brienne, as well as titular Duchess of Athens and of other claimed titles. Since her eldest son Gauthier had died before her brother, her heir was her second son Sohier of Enghien. Sh
2 CONC e allowed her inherited lands to be divided between her numerous children during her own lifetime.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella's brother Gauthier had left Greece sometime in the 1340s, and her eldest surviving son Sohier of Enghien apparently was resident lord of Argos and Nauplia from c. 1350. In the division of the inheritance in 1356-60, he re
2 CONC ceived the title of Duke of Athens, and certain rights in Argos-Nauplia. However, her sixth son Guy of Enghien received the lordship of Argos and Nauplia in the division and resided there as the lord from 1356 to 1377. Guy was als
2 CONC o sometimes titled Duke of Athens, although the title officially belonged to his elder brother and the latter's son, and they as Dukes of Athens were overlords of Guy and Guy's heirs.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella's fourth son Louis of Enghien, Lord of Conversano, received the (titular) Duchy of Athens in 1381, when his nephew's inheritance was divided.
2 CONT
2 CONT Guy's daughter Maria d'Enghien, Isabella's granddaughter, married Pietro Cornaro, a Venetian. They sold the lordship to Venice in 1388.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her main inheritance went, after some interludes, to the children of her third son John of Enghien, Peter I of Enghien, who died childless, and Mary of Enghien (1367-1446).
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Children
2 CONT
2 CONT Her children were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Walter of Enghien (June 5, 1322 – November 18, 1340)
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabeau of Enghien (d. December 28, 1357), Abbess of Flines
2 CONT
2 CONT Sohier of Enghien (d. March 21, 1364), Count of Brienne, titular Duke of Athens.
2 CONT
2 CONT John of Enghien, (d. 1380), Count of Lecce and Lord of Castro
2 CONT
2 CONT Marguerite of Enghien, married Pierre de Préaux
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis of Enghien (d. March 17, 1394), Lord (later Count) of Conversano, later Count of Brienne and titular Duke of Athens. Married Giovanna of Sanseverino, by whom he had four daughters, including his heiress, Marguerite, who in h
2 CONC er own turn married John of Luxembourg, Sire of Beauvois. Mary, Queen of Scots and Queen consort Elizabeth Woodville were notable descendants.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacques of Enghien, a canon in Liège
2 CONT
2 CONT Guy of Enghien,(d. 1377), Lord of Argos and Nauplia
2 CONT
2 CONT Engelbert I of Enghien (c. 1330–February 20, 1403), Lord of Ramerupt, La Follie, and Seneffe
2 CONT
2 CONT Françoise of Enghien, married Peter, Count of Montebello
2 CONT
2 CONT Jeanne of Enghien, a nun at Flines
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Duchess Isabella of Brienne (1306–1360) was suo jure  Countess of Lecce and Conversano, claimant to the Duchy of Athens and Kingdom of Jerusalem, etc.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family and early years
2 CONT
2 CONT She was daughter of Walter V of Brienne, Duke of Athens, who was killed at the Battle of Halmyros near Thebes, Greece, in 1311. As the granddaughter of Hugh of Brienne, Count of Lecce etc., she was a descendant of the Kings of Jer
2 CONC usalem and of Cyprus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her father's life was largely spent in Greece, where he tried to win back his mother's inheritance, the Duchy of Athens. Walter V of Brienne hired the Catalan Company, a group of mercenaries founded by Roger des Flors, to fight ag
2 CONC ainst the Byzantine successor states of Epirus and Nikaia, but when he tried to cheat and kill them in 1311, they slew him at the Battle of Halmyros and took over the Duchy, making Catalan the official language and replacing the F
2 CONC rench and Byzantine-derived laws of the Principality of Achaea with the laws of Catalonia, soon making it up as Duchy of Neopatras. When the duke Gauthier was killed, Isabella's mother Jeanne de Châtillon (died 1354), daughter of 
2 CONC count of Porcien, may have tried to hold the Acropolis of Athens against the attacking Catalans, but eventually surrendered it. The Brienne family retainers continued to hold Argos and Nauplia under Walter of Foucherolles. Duchess
2 CONC  Joanna however fled with her two young children to France, where the family had properties in Champagne, around Brienne-le-Chateau. The impoverished family was not able to provide better, and Isabella married a Walloon knight, Wa
2 CONC lter III of Enghien, whose lordships in Hainaut and therearound were not unsubstantial (Condé, Enghien).
2 CONT
2 CONT Countess of Lecce and Brienne
2 CONT
2 CONT Her brother Walter VI of Brienne gradually gained better positions, and by allying with the Angevin kings of Naples, recovered some Italian fiefs. As constable of France, he was killed in the battle of Poitiers in 1356. He had als
2 CONC o been Lord of Florence, Marshal of France, Count of Lecce, Conversano etc. He was an ally of the Angevins of Naples, and participated their policies in Italy. His children died young, both with his first wife Margherita of Anjou-
2 CONC Tarent and his second, his distant kinswoman Jeanne de Brienne. His sister Isabella's children therefore succeeded to his possessions and claims. Already by 1350, Isabella's heir Sohier of Enghien resided in Greece and held Argos 
2 CONC and Nauplia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella survived her brother, whom she succeeded, and died 1360. Her husband Walter of Enghien had died in 1345.
2 CONT
2 CONT For a few years, she was Countess of Lecce and Brienne, as well as titular Duchess of Athens and of other claimed titles. Since her eldest son Gauthier had died before her brother, her heir was her second son Sohier of Enghien. Sh
2 CONC e allowed her inherited lands to be divided between her numerous children during her own lifetime.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella's brother Gauthier had left Greece sometime in the 1340s, and her eldest surviving son Sohier of Enghien apparently was resident lord of Argos and Nauplia from c. 1350. In the division of the inheritance in 1356-60, he re
2 CONC ceived the title of Duke of Athens, and certain rights in Argos-Nauplia. However, her sixth son Guy of Enghien received the lordship of Argos and Nauplia in the division and resided there as the lord from 1356 to 1377. Guy was als
2 CONC o sometimes titled Duke of Athens, although the title officially belonged to his elder brother and the latter's son, and they as Dukes of Athens were overlords of Guy and Guy's heirs.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella's fourth son Louis of Enghien, Lord of Conversano, received the (titular) Duchy of Athens in 1381, when his nephew's inheritance was divided.
2 CONT
2 CONT Guy's daughter Maria d'Enghien, Isabella's granddaughter, married Pietro Cornaro, a Venetian. They sold the lordship to Venice in 1388.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her main inheritance went, after some interludes, to the children of her third son John of Enghien, Peter I of Enghien, who died childless, and Mary of Enghien (1367–1446).
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT
2 CONT Her children were:
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. Walter of Enghien (June 5, 1322 – November 18, 1340)
2 CONT
2 CONT    2. Isabeau of Enghien (d. December 28, 1357), Abbess of Flines
2 CONT
2 CONT    3. Sohier of Enghien (d. March 21, 1364), Count of Brienne, titular Duke of Athens.
2 CONT
2 CONT    4. John of Enghien, (d. 1380), Count of Lecce and Lord of Castro
2 CONT
2 CONT    5. Marguerite of Enghien, married Pierre de Préaux
2 CONT
2 CONT    6. Louis of Enghien (d. March 17, 1394), Lord (later Count) of Conversano, later Count of Brienne and titular Duke of Athens. Married Giovanna of Sanseverino, by whom he had four daughters, including his heiress, Marguerite, wh
2 CONC o in her own turn married John of Luxembourg, Sire of Beauvois. Mary, Queen of Scots and Queen consort Elizabeth Woodville were notable descendants.
2 CONT
2 CONT    7. Jacques of Enghien, a canon in Liège
2 CONT
2 CONT    8. Guy of Enghien,(d. 1377), Lord of Argos and Nauplia
2 CONT
2 CONT    9. Engelbert I of Enghien (c. 1330–February 20, 1403), Lord of Ramerupt, La Follie, and Seneffe
2 CONT
2 CONT   10. Françoise of Enghien, married Peter, Count of Montebello
2 CONT
2 CONT   11. Jeanne of Enghien, a nun at Flines
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT '''Relationship to Lady Jane Grey'''
2 CONT
2 CONT -565 - 7th Great-grandmother
2 CONT
2 CONT 1013 - 7th Great-grandmother
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Alternate Spellings of Given Name:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * Isabeau
2 CONT * Isabella
2 CONT * Isabel
