0 @N6000000005076735963@ NOTE Alexander of Scotland, Prince of Scotland was born on 21 January 1264 at Jedburgh, Roxburghshire, Scotland.2 He was the son of Alexander III 'the Glorius', King of Scotland and Margaret of England, Princess of England.1 He married
1 CONC  Margareta de Dampierre, daughter of Guy de Dampierre, Comte de Flandre and Isabel de Luxembourg, in 1282.3 He died on 17 January 1284 at age 19 at Lindores Abbey, Fife, Scotland.2 He was also reported to have died on 28 January 1
1 CONC 284 at Lindores Abbey, Fife, Scotland. He was buried at Dunfermline Abbey, Dunfermline, Fife, Scotland.2
1 CONT      Alexander of Scotland, Prince of Scotland gained the title of Prince Alexander of Scotland.
1 CONT Citations
1 CONT [S106] Royal Genealogies Website (ROYAL92.GED), online ftp://ftp.cac.psu.edu/genealogy/public_html/royal/index.html. Hereinafter cited as Royal Genealogies Website.
1 CONT [S11] Alison Weir, Britain's Royal Family: A Complete Genealogy (London, U.K.: The Bodley Head, 1999), page 200. Hereinafter cited as Britain's Royal Family.
1 CONT [S16] Jirí Louda and Michael MacLagan, Lines of Succession: Heraldry of the Royal Families of Europe, 2nd edition (London, U.K.: Little, Brown and Company, 1999), table 12. Hereinafter cited as Lines of Succession.
0 @N6000000002187788200@ NOTE Maria van Arkel
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Maria van Arkel (ca. 1380–19 July 1415), only daughter and heiress of Count John XII of Arkel and Joanna of Jülich.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Family and children
1 CONT He married 24 June 1409 to John II of Egmond, regent of Guelders. They children were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Arnold of Egmond 
1 CONT Willem IV, Herr van Egmond and Ijsselstein, Stattholder of Geldern. 
