0 @N6000000003051152949@ NOTE [Wikipedia, "John I of Avesnes", retrieved 22 Dec 07]
1 CONT John I of Avesnes (May 1, 1218-December 24, 1257) was the count of Hainaut from 1246 to his death. Born in Houffalize, he was the eldest son of Margaret II of Flanders by her first husband, Bouchard IV of Avesnes. As the marriage 
1 CONC of Margaret and Bouchard was papally dissolved, he was considered illegitimate.
1 CONT
1 CONT His mother remarried to William II of Dampierre and bore more children who could claim her inheritance. Thus, John and his brother Baldwin undertook to receive imperial recognition of their legitimacy and did so from the Emperor F
1 CONC rederick II in March 1243. On 5 December 1244, Margaret inherited Flanders and Hainaut and designated her eldest son by her second husband, William III of Dampierre, as her heir. Immediately a war, called the War of the Succession
1 CONC  of Flanders and Hainault, was set off over the rights of inheritance, pitting John against William.
1 CONT
1 CONT After two years of fighting, in 1246, Louis IX of France intervened to settle the conflict. He granted Hainaut to John and Flanders to William. Nonetheless, conflict was reopened. In 1250, Margaret was forced to give over the gove
1 CONC rnment of Hainaut to John. On 6 June 1251, William was assassinated and it was shown that the Avesnes had financed the crime. On 4 July 1253, John defeated the armies of Margaret and her second Dampierre son, Guy, at the Battle of
1 CONC  West-Capelle. Guy was imprisoned and Margaret agreed to sell her rights to Hainaut to Charles of Anjou if he would reconquer it from John. John's brother-in-law William II, Count of Holland, the elected rex Romanorum, was convinc
1 CONC ed to grant Hainaut (an imperial fief) and those Flemish lands within the Empire to John. Charles was defeated and King Louis, returning from the Seventh Crusade, ordered his brother to abide by his arbitration of 1246. On 22 Nove
1 CONC mber 1257, Guy finally relinquished Hainaut, but John died on Christmas Eve in Valenciennes.
1 CONT
1 CONT Family and children
1 CONT He married Adelaide of Holland in 1246 and had the following issue:
1 CONT - John II, Count of Hainaut and Holland (1247-1304)
1 CONT - Joanna, Abbess of Flines (died 1304)
1 CONT - Bouchard, Bishop of Metz (1251-1296)
1 CONT - Guy, Bishop of Utrecht (1253-1317)
1 CONT - William, Bishop of Cambrai (1254-1296)
1 CONT - Floris, stadholder of Zeeland and Prince of Achaea
