0 @N6000000003658774791@ NOTE From Plantagenet Ancestry page 785:
1 CONT
1 CONT Jean/John de Fiennes, Knt., seigneur of Fiennes and Tingry (Pas-de-Calais), and of Wendover, Buckinghamshire, and Chokes and Gayton, Northamptonshire, Castelain of Bourbourg (descendant of King Henry II)
1 CONT
1 CONT From Plantagenet Ancestry page 156:
1 CONT
1 CONT JEAN (or JOHN) DE FIENNES, Knt., seigneur of Fiennes and Tingry (Pas-de-Calais), and, in England, of Wendover, Buckinghamshire, and Chokes and Gayton, Northamptonshire, castelain of Bourbourg, born in France before 1281 (of full a
1 CONC ge at his father's death).  He married in 1307 ISABELLE DE DAMPIERRE, 6th daughter of Guy (or Gui) de Dampierre ... by his 2nd wife, Isabelle. ... They had one son ... and two daughters. ... IN 1314 he leased the manor of Wendover
1 CONC , Buckinghamshire to King Edward II for ten years.  His wife, Isabelle, died in 1323.  In 1323, his nephew, Roger de Mortimer, Earl of March, escaped from the Tower of London and sought refuge with him in Picardy.  The king wrote 
1 CONC to John and his brother, Robert, in Picardy and commanded them to give up the rebels.  John subsequently made his peace with the king.  In 1328 he leased the manor of Martock, Somerset for life to Bennet de Folsham.  His English p
1 CONC ossessions were confiscated permanently in 1337, in consequence of his allegiance to France.  SIR JEAN DE FIENNES died in 1340.
0 @N6000000002187788145@ NOTE Arnold of Egmond
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Arnold of Egmond (1410 – 1473) Duke of Guelders, Count of Zuetphen. He was son of John II of Egmond and Maria van Arkel.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1423, on the death of Duke Reinoud IV, the towns raised to the ducal dignity his sister's grandson Arnold of Egmond, who was still a boy in years. Although the Emperor Sigismund had invested the Duke of Berg with the duchy of G
1 CONC elders, Arnold retained the confidence of the Estates by enlarging their privileges, and enjoyed the support of Duke Philip of Burgundy, to whose niece, the daughter of Duke Adolf of Cleves, he was betrothed, and afterwards united
1 CONC  in marriage. Subsequently, however, Duke Arnold fell out with his ally as to the succession to the see of Utrecht; whereupon Philip joined with the four chief towns of Gelderland in the successful attempt of Arnold's son Adolf to
1 CONC  substitute his own for his father's authority. But when in 1467 Charles the Bold became Duke of Burgundy, who could not bring himself to befriend a friend of the towns, Adolf after rejecting a compromise was thrown into prison, a
1 CONC nd his incapable father, against the will of the towns and the law of the land, pledged his duchy to Charles for 300,000 Rhenish florins (1471). Upon Arnold's death two years later, Charles took possession of the duchy.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Family and children
1 CONT He was married in Cleves on 26 January 1430 to Catherine of Cleves, daughter of Duke Adolf IV, Duke of Cleves-Mark and Mary of Burgundy. Their children were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Willem, Prince of Geldern, died young. 
1 CONT Adolf of Egmond. 
1 CONT Catherina of Geldern (1439 – 1496), Regent of Geldern in 1477–1481. She was married secretly in 1464 to Louis of Bourbon, Bishop of Liege. 
1 CONT Mary of Gueldres 
1 CONT Margaretha (ca. 1436–2 November 1486, Simmern), married on 16 August 1454 to Friedrich I of Simmern. 
1 CONT He also had numerous illegitimate children.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT External links
1 CONT Genealogie des Mittelalters 
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Reinoud IV Duke of Guelders
1 CONT 1423–1465 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Adolf 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Adolf Duke of Guelders
1 CONT 1471–1473 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Charles the Bold 
