0 @N6000000002188307783@ NOTE Charles I of Hungary
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Charles I of Hungary (Hungarian: Károly Róbert, Croatian: Karlo Robert, Slovak: Karol Róbert), (1288, Naples, Italy – 16 July 1342, Visegrád, Hungary), also called Charles Robert, Carobert, Charles I Robert and Charles Robert I, K
1 CONC ing of Hungary[1] and Croatia (1308-1342).
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Childhood
1 CONT     * 2 Struggle for Hungary
1 CONT     * 3 Struggles with the magnates
1 CONT     * 4 The restoration of the royal power
1 CONT           o 4.1 Deterioration of the southern frontier
1 CONT     * 5 Death
1 CONT     * 6 Ancestors
1 CONT     * 7 References
1 CONT     * 8 External links
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Childhood
1 CONT
1 CONT Charles was the only son of Charles Martel of Anjou and his wife Clementia of Habsburg, a daughter of King Rudolph I of Germany. His paternal grandmother, Mary of Hungary, a daughter of King Stephen V of Hungary, declared her clai
1 CONC m to Hungary following the death of her brother, King Ladislaus IV of Hungary, but the majority of the country accepted the rule of her distant cousin, King Andrew III of Hungary. Mary, Queen Consort of Naples transferred her clai
1 CONC m to Hungary to her eldest son, Charles Martel of Anjou on 6 January 1292, who was also the heir to the Kingdom of Naples, but he was never able to enforce his claim against King Andrew III and died on 19 August 1295.
1 CONT
1 CONT After his father's death, Charles inherited the claim to Hungary, but his grandfather, King Charles II of Naples appointed his younger son (Charles' paternal uncle), Robert of Naples to his heir in Naples on 13 February 1296. This
1 CONC  decree was confirmed by Pope Boniface VIII, the overlord of the Kingdom of Naples, on 27 February 1297, so Charles lost his claim to the throne of Naples.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Struggle for Hungary
1 CONT
1 CONT In the beginning of 1300, a powerful magnate, Paul Šubic accepted Charles' title to the Kingdom of Hungary. His grandfather granted Charles a smaller amount of money and sent him to Hungary to enforce his claim against King Andrew
1 CONC  III. Charles disembarked in Split in August 1300 and he went to Zagreb where he was accepted as King of Hungary by Ugrin Csák, another influential magnate of the kingdom.
1 CONT
1 CONT When King Andrew III died on 14 January 1301, his partisans took Charles to Esztergom where the Archbishop Gregory Bicskei crowned him with an occasional crown because the Holy Crown of Hungary was guarded by his opponents. The ma
1 CONC jority of the magnates of the kingdom, however, did not accept his rule and proclaimed Wenceslaus III of Bohemia, the son of Wenceslaus II of Bohemia king. Wenceslaus III engaged the daughter of King Andrew III and he was crowned 
1 CONC with the Holy Crown in Székesfehérvár by Archbishop John of Kalocsa.
1 CONT
1 CONT After his opponent's coronation, Charles withdrew to Slavonia where his partisans strengthened his rule. In September 1302, he laid siege to Buda, but he could not occupy the capital of the kingdom and had to withdraw to Slavonia 
1 CONC again. Pope Boniface VIII confirmed Charles' claim to Hungary on 31 May 1303 and his maternal uncle, King Albert I of Germany also provided him military assistance. In the summer of 1304, King Wenceslaus II of Bohemia arrived to H
1 CONC ungary in order to help his son to strengthen his rule in the kingdom. The King of Bohemia, however, realised soon that his son's position in Hungary was unstable; therefore he decided to retreat and his son followed him. On heari
1 CONC ng his opponents retreat, Charles made an alliance with Duke Rudolph I of Bohemia and they attacked Bohemia but they could not occupy Kutná Hora and Charles had to retreat to Hungary.
1 CONT
1 CONT Nevertheless, the majority of the Hungarian magnates did not accept Charles' rule. In August 1305, Wenceslaus III of Bohemia, who had inherited Bohemia from his father, renounced his claim to Hungary on behalf of Otto III, Duke of
1 CONC  Bavaria, who was a grandson of King Béla IV of Hungary. Otto arrived to Hungary soon and he was crowned with the Holy Crown of Hungary in Székesfehérvár by the Bishops of Veszprém and Csanád on 6 December. However, Otto was not a
1 CONC ble to strengthen his rule, either. In the course of 1306, Charles occupied Esztergom, Spiš Castle, Zvolen and some other fortresses in the northern parts of the kingdom, and in the next year he also occupied Buda.
1 CONT
1 CONT In June 1307, Duke Otto III visited the powerful Voivode of Transylvania, Ladislaus Kán, but the latter arrested him. On 10 October 1307, the magnates presented at the assembly in Rákos proclaimed Charles King of Hungary, but the 
1 CONC most powerful aristocrats (Matthew Csák, Amadé Aba and Ladislaus Kán) ignored him. At the end of the year Ladislaus Kán set Otto free and this left the country, but the Voivode of Transylvania denied to hand over the Holy Crown of
1 CONC  Hungary to Charles, whose legitimacy could be questioned without the coronation with the Holy Crown.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Struggles with the magnates
1 CONT
1 CONT After Otto's escape, Charles stood alone as claimant to the throne of Hungary, but large parts of his kingdom were under the rule of powerful aristocrats and even his alleged partisans ignored his royal prerogatives. His position 
1 CONC slightly strengthened when the legate of Pope Clement V arrived to Hungary in June 1308, who persuaded Matthew Csák to recognise Charles' reign on their meeting in the Monastery of Kékes. On 27 November 1308, Matthew Csák was also
1 CONC  present at the assembly in Pest where Charles was again proclaimed King of Hungary. Following the assembly, the synod of the prelates in Buda confirmed the theory of the inviolability of the king and the bishops also summoned Lad
1 CONC islaus Kán to return the Holy Crown of Hungary to Charles. However, the Voivode of Transylvania denied the request; therefore the Papal legate had a new crown made for Charles and he was crowned with the new crown by Archbishop Ta
1 CONC más of Esztergom on 15 June 1309. Finally, under the threats of the Papal legate, Ladislaus Kán handed over the Holy Crown and Charles was, for the third time, crowned with the Holy Crown on 27 August 1310 by the Archbishop of Esz
1 CONC tergom.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the summer of 1311, Matthew Csák laid siege to Charles' capital, Buda, but Charles forced back his attack. Shortly afterwards, the citizens of Košice murdered Amade Aba, who had been one of Charles' main partisans, but Charles 
1 CONC took the side of the citizens against Amade Aba's sons; consequently, the latters allied themselves with Matthew Csák. Charles laid siege to Matthew Csák's castle, Šariš Castle in May 1312, but he was forced to retreat by the troo
1 CONC ps of the powerful magnate. Then the allied armies of Matthew Csák and Amade Aba's sons marched against Košice but Charles defeated them in the Battle of Rozhanovce on 12 July 1312. After the victory, Charles managed to occupy som
1 CONC e castles of Amade Aba's sons in the counties of Abaúj, Torna and Sáros.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1314, Charles reoccupied from Austria Devín Castle, taking advantage of the internal conflicts in the Holy Roman Empire, and in the first half of 1315 he managed to reoccupy also the Castle of Visegrád from Matthew Csák. At abo
1 CONC ut that time, he married Mary of Bytom, a daughter of Duke Casimir of Bytom. They had a daughter, Catherine (d. c. 1355), married in 1338 Henry II, Duke of Swidnica.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the course of May 1316 Charles was struggling against the family Koszegi, but some magnates of the eastern part of his kingdom, lead by Kopasz Borsa, rebelled against him and offered the Holy Crown to King Andrew of Halych, who
1 CONC  himself was also a descendant of King Béla IV of Hungary. Charles' troops, however, defeated the rebels and occupied their castles in the counties of Bihar, Szolnok, Borsod and Kolozs. In May 1317, his armies also suppressed the 
1 CONC revolt of Amade Aba's sons, and he could occupy the Castle of Komárno from Matthew Csák in October.
1 CONT
1 CONT Charles raised the funds of his military actions by seizing ecclesiastical properties; therefore the prelates made an alliance in 1318 and they demanded that the Estates of the realm be summonded to a general assembly. However, th
1 CONC e Estates did not raised objections against the king's policies at their assembly in July. During the year, his troops occupied several fortresses of Ladislaus Kán's sons in Transylvania.
1 CONT
1 CONT After his first wife's death, the widowed Charles married Beatrix of Luxemburg, daughter of Henry VII, Holy Roman Emperor and sister of King John I of Bohemia, probably in September 1318. In the summer of 1319, he lead his armies 
1 CONC against King Stefan Uroš II Milutin of Serbia, who had occupied the southern part of the Kingdom of Hungary, and defeated the Serbian troops near Macva. After his victory he reoccupied Beograd and also the territory of the Banovin
1 CONC a of Macva.
1 CONT
1 CONT Beatrix and her only child died at its birth on October 11, 1319. Charles, having widowed for the second time, married Elisabeth of Poland, a daughter of King Wladislaus I of Poland on 6 July 1320. They had five sons:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Charles (1321)
1 CONT     * Ladislaus (Belgrade, November 1, 1324–February 24, 1329)
1 CONT     * Louis I of Hungary (1326–1382)
1 CONT     * Andrew, Duke of Calabria (1327–1345)
1 CONT     * Stephen, Duke of Slavonia (1332–1354)
1 CONT
1 CONT At that time, Charles began to reorganise the financial basis of the royal power by declaring that it is only the king who is entitled to open new custom-houses in the kingdom.
1 CONT
1 CONT The death of Matthew Csák on 21 March 1321, the most powerful aristocrat in the kingdom, resulted in the disintegration of his provinces and Charles' troops could occupy all the fortresses of the deceased baron till the end of the
1 CONC  year. In January 1322, the towns in Dalmatia rebelled against the rule of Ban Mladen Subic, whose family had been among Charles' first supporters in the Kingdom of Hungary. Charles, taking advantage of the situation, went to Dalm
1 CONC atia and arrested the powerful Ban and enstrengthened his power in Croatia and Dalmatia.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] The restoration of the royal power
1 CONT
1 CONT In the beginning of 1323, Charles renounced the royal prerogative of undermining the currency and introduced a new tax (lucrum camaræ) in order to ensure the permanency of the royal revenues. In the same year, Charles transferred 
1 CONC his seat to Visegrád from Temesvár. Charles lead, in 1324, his armies against Voivode Basarab I of Wallachia who had occupied the Banat of Severin from the Kingdom of Hungary and defeated him.
1 CONT Coats of Arms of Charles I of Anjou, King of Hungary
1 CONT Coats of Arms of Charles I of Anjou, King of Hungary
1 CONT
1 CONT Charles restored order by absolute rule. The Diet was still summoned occasionally at very irregular intervals, but the real business of the state was transacted in the royal council. To impose limitations on the barons, the lesser
1 CONC  gentry were protected against the tyranny of the magnates, encouraged to appear at court and taxed for military service by the royal treasury so as to draw them closer to the King. The royal court was famous throughout Europe as 
1 CONC a school of chivalry.
1 CONT
1 CONT Charles also carried out numerous important political and economical reforms. He established the so called honor system. Instead of large donations faithful servants of the king were given an office (in Latin honor, in old Hungari
1 CONC an becsü). Powerful officials of the kingdom like the count palatine were appointed count (Lat. comes, Hung. ispán) to several counties. They became the keeper of royal property (including castles) in their counties and the repres
1 CONC entative of the king. The barons administered these possessions by their own men (familiares, roughly: vassals). Honor ensured real power. While most of the aristocrats had only two or three castles (even the exceptionally powerfu
1 CONC l Lackfi family had only seven castles) the possession of a greater honor ensured power over ten or twenty castles. These offices were not given for eternity. The king could deprive the baron of his honor any time. Most powerful h
1 CONC onors often rotated among the members of aristocracy.
1 CONT
1 CONT Charles successfully curbed inflation, introducing new coins with a constantly high purity of gold. Florins minted in a newly established mint in Kremnica became soon the popular international means of payment throughout Europe. T
1 CONC he reform of the currency and of the whole fiscal system greatly contributed to enrich both the merchant class and the treasury. Towns grew and crime reduced owing to Charles's fiscal care. He encouraged trade and imposed taxes to
1 CONC  support his army, which he used to expand his territory, making Hungary a major European power. His achievements were continued by his son King Louis I of Hungary.
1 CONT
1 CONT Charles's foreign policy largely stemmed from dynastic alliances. His most successful achievement was the mutual defense union with Poland and Bohemia against the Habsburgs, accomplished by the convention of Trencín in 1335, confi
1 CONC rmed the same year at the brilliant two-month congress of Visegrád. Not only did all the princes of Central Europe compose their differences and enjoy splendid entertainment during the months of October and November: the immediate
1 CONC  result of the congress was a combined attack by the Hungarians and Poles upon Louis IV, Holy Roman Emperor and his ally the Habsburg Duke Albert II of Austria, which resulted in favor of Charles in 1337.
1 CONT
1 CONT Charles's desire to unite the kingdoms of Hungary and Naples under his eldest son Louis I was dashed by Venice and by the Pope, who both feared Hungary might become the dominant Adriatic power. Nevertheless he was more than compen
1 CONC sated for this disappointment by his compact in 1339 with his ally and brother-in-law, Casimir III of Poland, whereby it was agreed that Louis should succeed to the Polish throne on the death of the childless Casimir. Finally his 
1 CONC younger son, Andrew, Duke of Calabria was promised the crown of Naples.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Deterioration of the southern frontier
1 CONT Posada Battle
1 CONT Posada Battle
1 CONT
1 CONT The Árpád kings had succeeded in encircling their whole southern frontier with six military colonies or banates, comprising, roughly speaking, Little Wallachia (southern part of present-day Romania) and the northern parts of prese
1 CONC nt-day Bulgaria, Serbia and Bosnia. Charles redistributed these territories and proselytized the residents of the region to consolidate his reign.
1 CONT
1 CONT Although he managed to expand his kingdom, the adverse effect was converting most of the old banates into semi-independent and violently anti-Hungarian principalities. The predominant religion of the area was Greek-Orthodox, and f
1 CONC orceful proselytization to Catholicism provoked rebellion. Natural dynastic competition with the Orthodox Serbian and Bulgarian tsars and the emergence of a new Wallachia also contributed to the uprising.
1 CONT
1 CONT Prior to 1320, Western Wallachia (Oltenia) was regarded by the Hungarians as part of the banate of Szörény (Severin). When the Wallachian ruler, Basarab I showed signs of disobedience, Charles lead his army into Wallachia, though 
1 CONC poor supplies caused him to return after occupying several towns. On his return November 9, 1330, the Hungarian army got lost between the mountains. The Wallachians and Hungarians signed a peace treaty and Basarab vowed to show th
1 CONC em out of the mountain pass, but in fact the Wallachians trapped the Hungarians in an ambush at Posada. In the Battle of Posada, King Charles barely escaped, by exchanging clothes with one of his knights. This incident marked the 
1 CONC beginning of Wallachia as an independent voivodeship.
1 CONT
1 CONT Unknown to Charles, the Ottoman Turks had already secured Asia Minor under the sultans Osman I and Orhan I and planned to invade south-eastern Europe to consolidate their realm. The south-eastern European sovereignties were keener
1 CONC  on securing their regimes than on co-ordinating their defences. Their diversity helped the Ottomans expand their dominion into the region.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Death
1 CONT
1 CONT Charles died on July 16, 1342, and was laid beside the high altar in Székesfehérvár, the ancient burial place of the Árpáds.
1 CONT
