0 @N6000000002188307802@ NOTE Clémence d'Anjou
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Clémence d'Anjou, also called Clementia of Hungary (French: Clémence de Hongrie) (1293-October 12, 1328), Queen consort of France and Navarre, was the second wife of King Louis X of France.
1 CONT
1 CONT She was the daughter of Charles Martel of Anjou, titular King of Hungary, and Clementia of Habsburg, the daughter of Holy Roman Emperor Rudolf I. She was the niece of Charles of Valois by his first wife, Marguerite of Anjou and Si
1 CONC cily.
1 CONT
1 CONT When Philip IV of France died, his eldest son Louis of Navarre became King of France. His wife, Marguerite, had been locked up in Château-Gaillard since 1314, having been found guilty of adultery by the King, on the testimony of, 
1 CONC amongst others, her sister-in-law, Isabelle; since there had been no formal divorce, Marguerite technically became Queen consort with Philip's death, but was kept locked up. In 1315, however, the Queen died - allegedly strangled o
1 CONC r otherwise murdered to allow her husband to remarry - and Louis was able to remarry. He chose Clemence, and they married on August 19, 1315; she was crowned Queen at Reims on August 24.
1 CONT
1 CONT Louis died in June 1316, leaving Clemence several months pregnant. Her brother-in-law, Philippe, became regent, denying the rights of Clemence's step-daughter Jeanne, who was too young and whose paternity was doubtful, and of Clem
1 CONC ence herself, who was considered unsuitable to be regent. She gave birth to a son named John in November 1316. King from the moment of his birth, he lived only four days, whereupon the throne was seized by his uncle, who became Ph
1 CONC ilip V.
1 CONT
1 CONT Clemence then left the court for Avignon. She then in 1318 went into retirement in the Dominican convent of Aix-en-Provence. She returned to Paris several years later, and died on October 12, 1328, at age thirty five. She was buri
1 CONC ed on October 15 in the Jacobin convent.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT Weir, Alison, Isabella
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Margaret of Burgundy Queen consort of Navarre
1 CONT 1315–1316 Succeeded by
1 CONT Jeanne de Bourgogne
1 CONT Queen of France
1 CONT 1315–1316
0 @N6000000002244382765@ NOTE She was the daughter of Charles I, King of Hungary
0 @N6000000002188625378@ NOTE Charles, Duke of Calabria
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Charles, Duke of Calabria (1298 – November 9, 1328) was the son of King Robert of Naples and Yolanda of Aragon.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Biography
1 CONT
1 CONT Born in Naples, he became Duke of Calabria in 1309 on his father's accession, and was created Vicar-General of the Kingdom of Sicily (Naples). His father intended him to lead the force sent to aid Florence in 1315, but was constra
1 CONC ined by time to send Robert's brother, Philip I of Taranto, instead. The Florentine-Neapolitan coalition was badly beaten at the ensuing Battle of Montecatini.
1 CONT
1 CONT The victory of Castruccio Castracani at Altopascio in 1325 led the Florentines to elect Charles signore (lord) of the city for ten years in 1326. At the time, he was unsuccessfully attempting to seize Sicily from his first cousin 
1 CONC Frederick III, and sent Walter VI of Brienne as his deputy until he could arrive, where Walter made a (misleadingly) favorable impression. While Charles' arrival checked Castruccio, he exacted onerous taxes from the Florentines, u
1 CONC ntil he was recalled to Naples in December 1327 due to the advance of Emperor Louis IV into Italy. There he died in 1328. He left as heir his eldest surviving daughter, Joan; a posthumous daughter, Marie, was born in 1329.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family and ancestry
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1316, he married Catherine of Habsburg (1295–1323), daughter of Albert I of Germany. After her death, he married Marie of Valois (1309–1332), daughter of Charles of Valois. They had five children:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Eloisa (d. December 1325)
1 CONT     * Marie (1326–1328)
1 CONT     * Charles Martel (Florence, April 13, 1327 – April 21, 1327, Florence)
1 CONT     * Joan I of Naples (1328–1382)
1 CONT     * Marie of Calabria (1329–1366), Countess of Alba. Married Charles, Duke of Durazzo.
0 @N6000000002188625501@ NOTE Sancha of Majorca
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Sancha of Majorca (c. 1285 – 28 July 1345, Naples) was the second wife but only Queen consort of Robert of Naples.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family
1 CONT
1 CONT She was a daughter of James II of Majorca and his Queen consort Esclaramunda of Foix.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her paternal grandparents were James I of Aragon and Violant of Hungary. Her maternal grandparents were Roger IV of Foix and Brunissenda of Cardona.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Marriage
1 CONT
1 CONT In July, 1304, Sancha married Robert of Naples. He was the eldest surviving son of Charles II of Naples and Maria Arpad of Hungary. He was about twenty-seven years old and his first wife Yolanda of Aragon, daughter of Peter III of
1 CONC  Aragon and Constantia of Sicily, had died in August, 1302.
1 CONT
1 CONT Charles II died on 5 May 1309 and Robert succeeded him. Sancha served as his Queen consort for the rest of his reign but remained childless.
1 CONT
1 CONT On 20 January 1343, Robert died and was succeeded by his granddaughter Joan I of Naples. Sancha became Queen Dowager and retired to a local monastery on 20 January 1344. She died as a nun the following year.
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Maria Arpad of Hungary Queen Consort of Naples
1 CONT 5 May 1309 –20 January 1343 Succeeded by
1 CONT Andrew, Duke of Calabria
0 @N6000000002322305245@ NOTE By his marriage to Joan I, he became King (consort) of Naples after a
1 CONT struggle with King Louis I of Hungary. Louis of Taranto was believed to have
1 CONT played a major role in the murder of Louis I's brother, Andrew, the first
1 CONT husband of Joan I. Louis, second husband of Joan, having usurped the royal
1 CONT power of Joan, regained much of the Island of Sicily, including the capitol
1 CONT of Palermo, A baron's revolt, however, forced him to return to the mainland,
1 CONT where he defeated his enemies. His sudden death prevented return to Sicily.
