0 @N6000000002187840583@ NOTE Charles IV, Holy Roman Emperor
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Charles IV (May 14, 1316 – 29 November 1378), of the House of Luxembourg, King of the Romans (as Charles IV, 1346 – 1378), Holy Roman Emperor (Charles IV, 1355 – 1378), King of Bohemia Charles I 1346 – 1378), Count of Luxembourg (
1 CONC 1346 – 1353), Margrave of Brandenburg (1373 – 1378). He was born in Prague as Wenceslaus, he later chose Charles for his confirmation name.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Life
1 CONT From 1333 Charles started to administer his father's Crown lands due to the King's frequent absence and in 1334 he became Margrave of Moravia. He was elected as a rival King of the Romans to Emperor Louis IV and succeeded his fath
1 CONC er John of Luxemburg as King of Bohemia and Count of Luxembourg in 1346 as his mother was Elizabeth (Eliška), heiress of Bohemia, daughter of King Wenceslaus II and sister of King Wenceslaus III of the Premyslid dynasty. Charles w
1 CONC as crowned as emperor of the Holy Roman Empire in 1355. After 1349, Charles IV was uncontested ruler of the Holy Roman Empire till his death in 1378.
1 CONT
1 CONT  Statue near Charles Bridge, Prague (cr.1848).During his reign imperial policy refocused on the dynastic sphere and abandoned the ideal of the Holy Roman Empire as a universal monarchy. In 1353 Luxembourg was granted to his brothe
1 CONC r Jobst. Charles IV concentrated his energies chiefly on the economic and intellectual development of Bohemia, founding the Charles University of Prague in 1348 and encouraging the early humanists — he is known to have corresponde
1 CONC d with Petrarch, whom he invited to visit his residence in Prague. Petrarch, however hoped (to no avail) to make Charles move his residence to Rome, to take up the tradition of the ancient Roman Empire. As he became fond of Prague
1 CONC , art and architecture flourished in his capital, owing to his activity as a builder and patron; construction of the Charles Bridge and of the Hradcany, completion of Saint Vitus Cathedral by Peter Parler are among the best exampl
1 CONC es. From the reign of Charles IV dates the first flowering of manuscript painting in Prague. In 1356 he issued the Golden Bull, which codified the procedures for imperial elections, but had the disastrous effect of causing minor p
1 CONC rinces who were left out of the electoral process to lose allegiance to the empire. In 1373 he inherited the Margravate of Brandenburg.
1 CONT
1 CONT  The Golden Bull of 1356 issued by Holy Roman Emperor Charles IV.His French education left a lasting mark on Charles. His father, known as John the Blind, king of Bohemia, was an ardent francophile and patron of the composer and p
1 CONC oet Guillaume de Machaut — he died at Crécy in 1346 while fighting on the French side. Charles's sister Bona, married the eldest son of Philip VI of France, the future John II of France, in 1335. Thus, Emperor Charles IV of Luxemb
1 CONC ourg was the maternal uncle of King Charles V of France, who solicited his relative's advice at Metz in 1356 during the Parisian Revolt. This family connection was celebrated publicly when Charles IV made a solemn visit to his nep
1 CONC hew in 1378, just months before his death. A detailed account of the occasion, enriched by many splendid miniatures, can be found in Charles V's copy of the Grandes Chroniques de France.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Emperor was literate and fluent in five languages - Latin, Czech, German, French and Italian.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Family and children
1 CONT Charles married four times. His first wife was Blanche, 1316–1348), daughter of Charles, Count of Valois, a half-sister of Philip VI of France. They had two daughters,
1 CONT
1 CONT Margaret (1335 - 1349), who married Louis I of Hungary; and 
1 CONT Katharina (1342 - 1395), who married Rudolf IV of Austria and Otto V, Duke of Bavaria, Elector of Brandenburg. 
1 CONT He secondly married Anne (Anna), (1329–1353), daughter of the Count Palatine Rudolph II and they had one son,
1 CONT
1 CONT Wenceslas, who died young. 
1 CONT  Meeting between Charles V of France and the Emperor Charles IV in Paris in 1378, from a 15th century manuscript in the Library of the Arsenal, Paris.His third wife was Anne of Schweidnitz, (1339–1362), daughter of Duke Henryk II 
1 CONC of Schweidnitz and Katharina of Anjou, by whom he had two children,
1 CONT
1 CONT Wenceslaus (1361–1419), Charles's successor as Emperor and king of Bohemia, and 
1 CONT Elisabeth (19 April 1358–4 September 1373), who married to Albert III of Austria. 
1 CONT His fourth wife was Elizabeth of Pomerania, 1345 or 1347–1393), daughter of Duke Bogislaw V of Hind Pomerania and Elisabeth of Poland. They had six children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Anne of Bohemia (1366–1394), who married Richard II of England 
1 CONT Sigismund (1368–1437), emperor, king of Hungary and Bohemia and margrave of Brandenburg. 
1 CONT John, Duke of Görlitz (1370–1396). 
1 CONT Charles (13 March 1372–24 July 1373). 
1 CONT Margaret (1373–1410), who married John III, Burgrave of Nuremberg. 
1 CONT Heinrich (1377–1378). 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT In memoriam
1 CONT In the present Czech Republic, he is still regarded as Father of the Country (otec vlasti, pater patriae), a title first coined by Vojtech Rankuv of Ježov (Adalbertus Ranconis de Ericinio), a significant intellectual and the only 
1 CONC Czech rector of the Sorbonne, at the emperor's funeral.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Louis IV King of Germany
1 CONT Also Holy Roman Emperor
1 CONT 1347–1378 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Wenceslaus 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT John of Luxembourg King of Bohemia
1 CONT 1346–1378 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Otto V Margrave of Brandenburg
1 CONT 1373–1378 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Sigismund 
0 @N6000000002322332023@ NOTE He was elected King of Germany in 1346 in opposition to excommunicated Louis
1 CONT IV; succeeded as King of Bohemia in 1347. He was Holy Roman Emperor as
1 CONT Charles IV who was one of the most learned and diplomatically skilled
1 CONT soverigns of his time. He gained more through diplomacy than others did by
1 CONT war, and through purchases, marriages, and inheritances, he enlarged his
1 CONT dynasty power. Under Charles's rule, Prague became the political, economic,
1 CONT and cultural centre of the Holy Roman Empire, and, eventully, its capital.
