0 @N6000000002188409720@ NOTE Louis II, Duke of Bourbon
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Louis II of Bourbon, called the Good (1337 – 1410, Montlucon) was the third Duke of Bourbon.
1 CONT
1 CONT Duke Louis is reported to have been mentally somewhat instable, a trait of nervous breakdowns presumably hereditary that showed clearly for example in his sister Joanna of Bourbon, the queen, and already in their father, Duke Pete
1 CONC r, and in their grandfather, Louis I, Duke of Bourbon.
1 CONT
1 CONT The teenage Louis inherited the duchy from his father Duke Peter I after his death in the Battle of Poitiers in 1356.
1 CONT
1 CONT On August 19, 1371, he married Anna of Auvergne (1358–1417), Countess of Forez and a daughter of Beraud II, Dauphin of Auvergne, and his wife the Countess of Forez, and they had four children:
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. Catherine of Bourbon (b. 1378), d. young
1 CONT    2. John of Bourbon (1381–1434), Duke of Bourbon
1 CONT    3. Louis of Bourbon (1388 – 1404), Sieur de Beaujeu
1 CONT    4. Isabelle of Bourbon (1384 – aft. 1451)
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1390, Duke Louis launched a crusade against the Hafsids of Tunis, in conjunction with the Genoese. Its objective was to suppress piracy based in the city of Mahdia, but the siege was unsuccessful.
1 CONT French nobility
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT John II Count of Forez
1 CONT 1372 – 1410
1 CONT with Anna Succeeded by
1 CONT John I/III
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Peter I Duke of Bourbon
1 CONT 1356 – 1410
1 CONT Count of Clermont-en-Beauvaisis
1 CONT 1371 – 1400
0 @N6000000002188307908@ NOTE Joanna of Bourbon
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Jeanne de Bourbon (Vincennes, February 3, 1338 – February 6, 1378, Paris) was the Queen consort of France, due to her marriage to King Charles V. Jeanne was the daughter of Peter I, Duke of Bourbon and Isabelle de Valois, a half-s
1 CONC ister of Philip VI of France as the daughter of Charles of Valois and his third wife Mahaut of Chatillon.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her father, grandfather, and brother were all somewhat mentally unstable, and Jeanne seems to have inherited this family ailment. She suffered a complete nervous breakdown after the birth of her seventh child. Her eldest surviving
1 CONC  son, Charles VI, was famous for his insanity. From her marriage to Charles V of France (1350, Tain-en-Viennois) were born nine children:
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. Jeanne (1357–1360)
1 CONT    2. Jean (1359–1364)
1 CONT    3. Bonne (1360)
1 CONT    4. Jean (1366)
1 CONT    5. Charles VI of France (1368–1422)
1 CONT    6. Marie (1370–1377)
1 CONT    7. Louis of Valois, Duke of Orléans (1372–1407)
1 CONT    8. Isabelle (1373–1378)
1 CONT    9. Catherine (1378–1388)
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT New Creation
1 CONT (Beatrice of Baux) Dauphine of France
1 CONT 22 August 1350–8 April 1364 Succeeded by
1 CONT Margaret of Burgundy
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Jeanne d'Auvergne Queen of France
1 CONT 1364 – 1378 Succeeded by
1 CONT Isabeau of Bavaria
