0 @N6000000002187755903@ NOTE Maria of Brabant
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Maria of Brabant (13 May 1254, Leuven – January 10, 1321, Murel), Queen consort of France.
1 CONT
1 CONT She was a daughter of Henry III, Duke of Brabant and Adelaide of Burgundy, daughter of Hugh IV, Duke of Burgundy.
1 CONT
1 CONT Maria became the second wife of Philip III of France on 21 August 1274 at Vincennes. She was crowned at Ste-Chapelle on 24 June 1275. She was the mother of Louis d'Evreux, Marguerite (wife of Edward I of England), and Blanche, wif
1 CONC e of Rudolf I of Germany. Due to her influence Philip became involved in the affairs of the Angevins and in hostilities with the Kingdom of Aragon.
1 CONT
1 CONT Following the death of her husband the King in 5 October 1285, she withdrew to a convent near Meulan. She died there 36 years later, and was buried in the Cordeliers convent in Paris.
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Isabella of Aragon Queen of France
1 CONT 1274 – 1285 Succeeded by
1 CONT Jeanne de Navarre
