0 @N6000000002187750001@ NOTE Marguerite of Provence
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Marguerite of Provence (Forcalquier, c. 1221 – December 21, 1295, Paris) was the eldest daughter of Ramon Berenguer IV, Count of Provence and Beatrice of Savoy.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her maternal grandparents were Thomas I of Savoy and Marguerite of Geneva, daughter of William I of Geneva.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her younger sisters were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Eleanor of Provence became the Queen consort of Henry III of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sanchia of Provence who became the Queen consort of Richard, Earl of Cornwall and rival King of the Germans.
1 CONT
1 CONT Beatrice of Provence who was the Queen consort of Charles I of Sicily
1 CONT
1 CONT Marguerite herself became the Queen consort of Louis IX of France and mother to eleven children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Blanche (1240–April 29, 1243) 
1 CONT Isabelle (March 2, 1241–January 28, 1271), married Theobald II of Navar
1 CONT Louis (February 25, 1244–January 1260) 
1 CONT Philippe III (May 1, 1245–October 5, 1285) 
1 CONT Jean (born and died in 1248) 
1 CONT Jean Tristan (1250–August 3, 1270) 
1 CONT Pierre (1251–1284) 
1 CONT Blanche (1253–1323), married Ferdinand de la Cerda, Infante of Castile 
1 CONT Marguerite (1254–1271), married John I, Duke of Brabant 
1 CONT Robert, Count of Clermont (1256–February 7, 1317). He was the ancestor of King Henry IV of France. 
1 CONT Agnes of France (c. 1260–December 19, 1327), married Robert II, Duke of Burgundy 
1 CONT Marguerite, like her sisters, was noted for her beauty and in the early years of their marriage she and Louis enjoyed a warm relationship. Her Franciscan confessor, William de St. Pathus, related that on cold nights Marguerite wou
1 CONC ld place a robe around Louis' shoulders, when her deeply religious husband rose to pray. Another anecdote recorded by St. Pathus related that Marguerite felt that Louis' plain clothing was unbecoming to his royal dignity, to which
1 CONC  Louis replied that he would dress as she wished, if she dressed as he wished. However, the chronicler Joinville noted with disapproval that Louis rarely asked after his wife and children, and in later years Louis became vexed wit
1 CONC h Marguerite's ambition.
1 CONT
1 CONT She accompanied Louis on his first crusade and was responsible for negotiations and ransom when he was captured. She was thus for a brief time the only woman ever to lead a crusade.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the death of Louis on his second crusade, during which she remained in France, she returned to Provence.
1 CONT
1 CONT She was devoted to her sister Queen Eleanor of England, and they stayed in contact until Eleanor's death.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT Murray, Jacqueline. Conflicted Identities and Multiple Masculinities, 1999 
1 CONT Marguerite of Provence
1 CONT Capetian Dynasty
1 CONT Born: 1221 Died: December 21 1295 
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Blanche of Castile Queen of France
1 CONT 1234–1270 Succeeded by
1 CONT Isabella of Aragon 
