0 @N6000000003949876321@ NOTE Daughter of Enrique I, King of Navarre and Blanche d'Artois, Jeanne succeeded her father in 1274 and became Queen of Navarre aged just three. On 16 August 1284 in the Notre Dame in Paris Jeanne married the future Philippe IV, King
1 CONC  of France and they had seven children. In 1285 she became queen consort of France. Jeanne brought to the French crown her rights to Navarre and the countships of Champagne and Brie. On her death in childbirth in 1305 these rights
1 CONC  were transmitted to her son, the future Louis X. Jeanne was a woman of great intelligence and vivacity, a lover of arts and letters who founded the famous College of Navarre
0 @N6000000002322286825@ NOTE As eldest son, Louis took the title of King of Navarre upon the death of his
1 CONT mother. On Apr-04-1314 however, he resigned the title to his next brother, the
1 CONT future Philip V of France. Louis sold the serfs their liberty, the beginning
1 CONT of the eventual end of serfdom.
0 @N6000000002187755517@ NOTE Louis X of France
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Louis X the Quarreller, also called the Headstrong or the Stubborn, (French: Louis X le Hutin, Spanish: Luis el Obstinado) (October 4, 1289 – June 5, 1316), King of France from 1314 to 1316, was a member of the Capetian Dynasty.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was born in Paris, France, son of Philip IV of France and Jeanne of Navarre.
1 CONT
1 CONT He inherited the title king of Navarre on the death of his mother, on April 2, 1305.
1 CONT
1 CONT On the death of his father in 1314, he became king of France and was officially crowned at Reims in August 1315.
1 CONT
1 CONT On September 21, 1305 he married Marguerite de Bourgogne (Burgundy) and they had a daughter, Jeanne (January 28, 1312 - October 6, 1349). Louis accused his wife of adultery and she was imprisoned and died in the chateau Gaillard. 
1 CONC On August 13, 1315 he married Clemence d'Anjou (1293 - 1328), daughter of Charles Martel and sister of Charles I of Hungary.
1 CONT
1 CONT The reign of Louis X was short and unremarkable, dominated by the continued feuding with the noble factions within the kingdom.
1 CONT
1 CONT Louis died at Vincennes, Val-de-Marne, following a game of Jeu de Paume (some say from dehydration) in 1316. He was interred with his second wife Clemence in Saint Denis Basilica.
1 CONT
1 CONT At the time of Louis's death, his wife Clemence was pregnant, making it impossible to know Louis's successor until the time his child was born. If the child were a son, he would succeed Louis as king: had the child been a daughter
1 CONC , there would be a question between Louis's (eldest) daughter Joan and his brother Philip V. (John I's half-sister Jeanne, as a female, had a disputed claim to the throne of France: a female could not succeed to the throne of Fran
1 CONC ce if following the Salic law; she did, however, have rights in the succession of Navarre where females are allowed - witness that kingdom being brought to Capetians by Louis' own mother). It must be underscored that at that time,
1 CONC  there existed these two ideas of succession, and it was not at all clear that Salic Law should be followed. This legal question had never earlier been resolved regarding the throne of France.
1 CONT
1 CONT Louis's next brother, the future Philip V was appointed regent (this act working in favor of the idea of him being the heir presumptive and the Salic Law being in control - however, Joan was at that time a minor, thus not a very g
1 CONC ood candidate for regentship - and apparently, the pregnant widow, queen Clemence, was not regarded a good choice for position of the regent), for the five months remaining until the birth of his brother's child, who turned out to
1 CONC  be male. Thus Louis was succeeded by his posthumous son John I (Jean I), who lived only five days. Louis X's brother Philip then became king.
0 @N6000000002176643332@ NOTE As eldest son, Louis took the title of King of Navarre upon the death of his
1 CONT mother. On Apr-04-1314 however, he resigned the title to his next brother, the
1 CONT future Philip V of France. Louis sold the serfs their liberty, the beginning
1 CONT of the eventual end of serfdom.
