0 @N6000000003300412115@ NOTE Daughter of Philippe IV, king of France706 . Isabella married Edward II o f England513 in 1308 and their eldest son became Edward III580 . Isabell a was unhappily married to Edward and through a rebellion in 1327 had hi m imprison
1 CONC ed and ultimately executed and so she became know as the she-wo lf of France. Isabella believed because she was the daughter of Philipp e IV, she had a claim to the French throne after all her brothers had die d without producing 
1 CONC a male heir. Edward III was to push this claim a fe w years later, starting the 'Hundred Years War'.
0 @N6000000002187747684@ NOTE Isabella of France
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Isabella of France (c.1295 – August 22, 1358), known as the She-Wolf of France, was the Queen consort of Edward II of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT Isabella was born in Paris in somewhere between 1288 an 1296, the daughter of King Philip IV of France and Queen Jeanne of Navarre, and the sister of three French kings. When she was only an infant, her father arranged a marriage 
1 CONC between Isabella and Edward, the son and heir of Edward I of England. The marriage had been under negotiation for several years. Its political intent was to settle the conflicts between the two kingdoms which had arisen over the E
1 CONC nglish continental possessions, namely Gascony but also English claims to Anjou and Normandy as well as larger areas of Aquitaine. Pope Boniface VIII had urged the marriage as early as 1298. In the intervening years there had been
1 CONC  a great deal of wrangling over the precise conditions of the marriage contract, and the English king attempted to break this engagement several times. Only after Edward I died in 1307 did the wedding go forward.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her groom, the new King Edward II, looked the part of a Plantagenet king to perfection. He was tall and athletic, and wildly popular at the beginning of his reign. She married Edward at Boulogne-sur-Mer on January 25, 1308. He had
1 CONC  ascended the throne the previous year, hence Isabella never was titled only as Princess of Wales, as was wrongly portrayed in the film Braveheart.
1 CONT
1 CONT At the time of her marriage Isabella was about twelve, described by Geoffrey of Paris as "the beauty of beauties...in the kingdom if not in all Europe." This may not merely have been a chronicler's politeness, as Isabella's father
1 CONC  and brother were likewise very handsome men. Despite her youth and beauty, King Edward paid little attention to his bride, bestowing her wedding gifts upon his favorite, Piers Gaveston.
1 CONT
1 CONT Despite Edward's homosexuality (perhaps bisexuality), he and Isabella produced four children, and she suffered at least one miscarriage. The itineraries of Edward II and Queen Isabella also show that they were together 9 months pr
1 CONC ior to the births of all four surviving offspring. Their children were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward, born 1312 
1 CONT John, born 1316 
1 CONT Eleanor, born 1318, married Reinoud II of Guelders 
1 CONT Joan, born 1321, married David II of Scotland 
1 CONT Although Isabella produced four children, the king was popularly believed to be homosexual, and lavished his attention on a succession of male favourites, including Piers Gaveston and Hugh le Despenser the younger. He neglected Is
1 CONC abella, once even abandoning her in a dangerous situation in Scotland, at Tynemouth. She barely escaped Robert the Bruce's army, fleeing along the coast to English soil. Isabella despised his favorite, Hugh the younger Despenser, 
1 CONC and in 1321, while pregnant with her youngest child, she dramatically begged Edward to banish Despenser from the kingdom. Despenser was exiled, but Edward recalled him later that year, and this act seems to have finally turned Isa
1 CONC bella against him all-together. She may have helped Roger Mortimer escape from the Tower of London in 1323.
1 CONT
1 CONT When her brother, King Charles IV of France, seized Edward's French possessions in 1325, she returned to France and gathered an army to oppose Edward, in alliance with Roger Mortimer, 1st Earl of March, who had become her lover. E
1 CONC nraged by this, Edward demanded that Isabella return to England. Her brother, King Charles, replied, "The queen has come of her own will and may freely return if she wishes. But if she prefers to remain here, she is my sister and 
1 CONC I refuse to expel her."
1 CONT
1 CONT Isabella and Mortimer left the French court in summer 1326 and went to William III, Count of Hainaut and Holland, whose wife was Isabella's cousin. He provided them with eight men-of-war in return for a marriage contract between h
1 CONC is daughter Philippa and Isabella's son, Edward. On September 21, 1326 Isabella and Mortimer landed at Suffolk with their mercenary army. King Edward offered a reward for their deaths, and even carried a knife in his hose with whi
1 CONC ch to kill his wife, and said that if he had no other weapon he would crush her with his teeth.
1 CONT
1 CONT King Edward's few allies deserted him; the Despensers were killed, and Edward himself was captured and abdicated in favour of his eldest son, Edward III of England. Since the young king was only fourteen when he was crowned on Jan
1 CONC uary 25, 1327, Isabella and Mortimer ruled as regents in his place.
1 CONT
1 CONT Isabella and Mortimer famously plotted to murder the deposed king in such a way as not to draw blame on themselves, sending the famous order "Edwardum occidere nolite timere bonum est" which depending on where the comma was insert
1 CONC ed could mean either "Fear not the killing Edward, it is good" or "No killing of Edward, it is good to fear".
1 CONT
1 CONT When Edward III attained his majority, he remembered their disloyalty, and had both Isabella and Mortimer taken prisoner, despite Isabella's cries of "Fair son, have pity on gentle Mortimer". Mortimer was executed for treason, but
1 CONC  Isabella's life was spared and she was allowed to retire to Castle Rising in Norfolk. She did not, as legend would have it, go insane; she enjoyed a comfortable retirement and made many visits to her son's court, doting on her gr
1 CONC andchildren. Isabella took the habit of the Poor Clares before she died on August 22, 1358, and her body was returned to London for burial at the Franciscan church at Newgate. She was buried in her wedding dress, with Edward's hea
1 CONC rt interred with her.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Isabella in Fiction
1 CONT Queen Isabella appears as a major character in Christopher Marlowe's play Edward II, and in Derek Jarman's 1991 film based on the play and bearing the same name. She is played by actress Tilda Swinton in this version.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the historically-based but fictional film Braveheart, directed by and starring Mel Gibson, Queen Isabella was a major character, played by the French actress Sophie Marceau. In the film, Isabella is depicted as having a romanti
1 CONC c affair with the Scottish hero William Wallace. This is entirely fictional, as there is no evidence whatsoever that the two people ever met one another. Wallace was executed in 1305, a date when Isabella was not yet married and t
1 CONC herefore had practically nothing to do with Northern Britain (not speaking of the fact she was not anywhere close to Scotland at that time). When Wallace died, Isabella was, if we use the later dates of birth (1293-1296), about 10
1 CONC  years old. It is unlikely she was involved in any romantic or sexual relationships at that age. If we use the earlier dates of birth (1288-1291) she may have. All of Isabella's children were born several years after Wallace's dea
1 CONC th, thus any fictional implications that Wallace was the father of Edward III are entirely false.
1 CONT
1 CONT There was another French princess in England during the last years of William Wallace: Marguerite of France, an aunt of Isabella, who was Edward I's second wife.
1 CONT
1 CONT Isabella has also been the subject of a number of historical novels, including Margaret Campbell Barnes' Isabel the Fair, Hilda Lewis' Harlot Queen, Maureen Peters' Isabella, the She-Wolf, Brenda Honeyman's The Queen and Mortimer,
1 CONC  and Jean Plaidy's The Follies of the King. She is the title character of The She-Wolf of France by the well-known French novelist Maurice Druon. The series of which the book was part, The Accursed Kings, has been adapted for Fren
1 CONC ch television in 1972 and 2005. Most recently, Isabella figures prominently in The Traitor's Wife: A Novel of the Reign of Edward II by Susan Higginbotham.
1 CONT
1 CONT See also: Hundred Years' War
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT Blackley, F.D. Isabella of France, Queen of England 1308-1358, and the Late Medieval Cult of the Dead. (Canadian Journal of History) 
1 CONT Doherty, P.C. Isabella and the Strange Death of Edward II, 2003 
1 CONT McKisack, May. The Fourteenth Century 1307-1399, 1959. 
1 CONT Woods, Charles T. Queens, Queans and Kingship, appears in Joan of Arc and Richard III: Sex, Saints and Government in the Middle Ages, 1988. 
1 CONT Geoffrey le Baker 
1 CONT Vita Edward Secundi 
1 CONT Father:
1 CONT Philip IV of France Mother:
1 CONT Jeanne of Navarre 
