0 @N6000000001563284692@ NOTE Being a fierce opponent to the Roman Catholic Pope, he opened the last medieval battle between emperorship and popehood.  Despite being excommunicated by Pope John XXII,  he became Roman Emperor of the German Reich in 1328.
1 CONT
1 CONT He set out to aggrandize his branch of the family.  He invested his eldest son with the Margravate of Brandenburg when the Ascanian house became extinct.  He saw to it that the sons by his 2nd marriage (to Margaret of Holland) suc
1 CONC ceeded to the country of Holland.  However, when Ludwig died his sons began a process of dividing Bavaria into individual fiefs. This continued until it was pretty much reunited in 1506.
0 @N6000000001578926464@ NOTE Being a fierce opponent to the Roman Catholic Pope, he opened the last medieval battle between emperorship and popehood.  Despite being excommunicated by Pope John XXII,  he became Roman Emperor of the German Reich in 1328.
1 CONT
1 CONT He set out to aggrandize his branch of the family.  He invested his eldest son with the Margravate of Brandenburg when the Ascanian house became extinct.  He saw to it that the sons by his 2nd marriage (to Margaret of Holland) suc
1 CONC ceeded to the country of Holland.  However, when Ludwig died his sons began a process of dividing Bavaria into individual fiefs. This continued until it was pretty much reunited in 1506.
0 @N6000000002322312632@ NOTE Duke of Upper Bavaria from 1294 and united Bavaria 1340-1347, first of the
1 CONT Wittlesbach line of German Emperors, his reign was marked by incessant
1 CONT diplomatic and military struggles to defend the right of the empire to elect
1 CONT an emperor independently of the papacy, to consolidate his own position, and
1 CONT to improve the status of his family. Louis finally managed to force John of
1 CONT Bohemia to nenounce all claims to Italy and to acknowledge Louis emperor in
1 CONT 1339. See message with third wife, Margaret.
0 @N6000000002187825509@ NOTE Louis IV, Holy Roman Emperor
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Louis IV, Holy Roman Emperor.Louis IV of Bavaria of the House of Wittelsbach (born 1282) was duke of Bavaria from 1294/1301 together with his brother Rudolf I, also count of the Palatinate until 1329 and, German king since 1314 an
1 CONC d crowned as Holy Roman Emperor in 1328. Louis died on October 11, 1347 when he suffered a stroke during a bear-hunt in Puch near Fürstenfeldbruck. He is buried in the Frauenkirche in Munich.
1 CONT
1 CONT Louis was the son of Louis II, Duke of Upper Bavaria, and Mechthild, a daughter of King Rudolph I.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Early reign 
1 CONT 2 Conflict with the Pope 
1 CONT 3 Dynastic policy 
1 CONT 4 Family and children 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Early reign
1 CONT Though Louis was partly educated in Vienna and became co-regent of his brother Rudolf I in Bavaria in 1301 with the support of his Habsburg mother Mechthild and her brother King Albert I, he quarrelled with the Habsburgs from 1307
1 CONC  over possessions in Lower Bavaria. The war against his brother Rudolf due to new disputes on the partition of their lands was ended in 1313, when peace was made at Munich.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the death of Holy Roman Emperor Henry VII the Luxemburg party among the prince electors set aside Henry's son, John of Luxembourg, because of his youth and chose Louis as rival king to the Habsburg Frederick the Handsome in 
1 CONC 1314. Short before this election Louis had defeated his cousin Frederick in the battle of Gammelsdorf. In 1316 Louis reckognized the independence of Switzerland from Habsburg. In the battle of Mühldorf 1322 Frederick was beaten ag
1 CONC ain and arrested but soon released and accepted as co-regent until he died in 1330.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Conflict with the Pope
1 CONT
1 CONT Golden Bull of Louis IV 1326Despite this victory, Pope John XXII still refused to ratify Louis’s election and in 1324 he excommunicated Louis. In opposite to previous disputes between the emperor and the papacy the ban did not lon
1 CONC ger react. After the reconciliation with Habsburg in 1326 Louis marched to Italy and was crowned Italian king in Milan in 1327. In January 1328 he entered Rome and had himself crowned emperor by the aged senator Sciarra Colonna, c
1 CONC alled captain of the Roman people. Three months later Louis published a decree declaring "Jacque de Cahors" (Pope John XXII) deposed on grounds of heresy. He then installed a Spiritual Franciscan, Pietro Rainalducci as Antipope Ni
1 CONC cholas V, who was deposed after Louis left Rome in early 1329.
1 CONT
1 CONT Philosophers like Michael of Cesena, Marsilius of Padua and William of Ockham were protected at the emperor's court in Munich. The failure of later negotiations with the papacy led 1338 to the declaration at Rhense by six electors
1 CONC  to the effect that election by all or the majority of the electors automatically conferred the royal title and rule over the empire, without papal confirmation. Louis also allied in 1337 with Edward III of England against Pope Be
1 CONC nedict XII in Avignon and his protector Philip VI of France. But in 1341 Louis deserted Edward and came finally to terms with Philip.
1 CONT
1 CONT Louis IV himself was a protector of the Teutonic knights. In 1337 he bestowed the Teutonic Order a privilege to conquer Lithuania and Russia. Later he forbade the Order to stand trial before foreign courts in their territorial con
1 CONC flicts with foreign rulers.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Dynastic policy
1 CONT In 1323 Louis acquired Brandenburg for his eldest son Louis V and in 1329 the emperor returned the Palatinate to his nephews. The duchy of Carinthia was also released to his Habsburg relatives in 1335. Then Louis inherited Upper B
1 CONC avaria in 1340 and re-united the duchy of Bavaria. In 1342 he also acquired Tyrol by voiding the first marriage of Margarete Maultasch and marrying her to his own son Louis V, thus alienating the house of Luxemburg.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1345 Louis further antagonized the lay princes by conferring Hainaut, Holland, Zeeland and Friesland upon his wife Margaret of Holland. The acquisition of these territories and his restless foreign policy had provided Louis wit
1 CONC h many enemies among the German princes. In 1346 the Luxemburg Charles IV was elected rival king with support of Pope Clement VI. Louis himself obtained much support from the Imperial Free Cities and the knighthood and was success
1 CONC fully resisting Charles, who was widely regarded as a papal puppet ("rex clericorum").
1 CONT
1 CONT Louis' sudden death the following year avoided a longer civil war. The sons of Louis supported Günther von Schwarzburg as new rival king but joined the Luxemburg party after Günther's early death in 1349 and divided the Wittelsbac
1 CONC h possesions among each other again.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Family and children
1 CONT He was first married to Beatrix von Silesia-Glogau. Their children were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Mathilde (aft. June 21, 1313 – July 2, 1346, Meißen), married at Nürnberg July 1, 1329 Friedrich II, Markgraf of Meißen (d. 1349) 
1 CONT a child (b. September 1314) 
1 CONT Anna (c. 1316 – January 29, 1319, Kastl) 
1 CONT Louis V the Brandenburger (1316–1361), duke of Upper Bavaria, margrave of Brandenburg, count of Tyrol 
1 CONT Agnes (b. c. 1318) 
1 CONT Stephen II (1319–1375), duke of Lower Bavaria 
1 CONT In 1324 he married Margaret of Holland, countess of Hainaut and Holland. Their children were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Margarete (1325–1374), married: 
1 CONT in 1351 in Ofen Stephen, Duke of Slavonia (d. 1354); 
1 CONT 1357/58 Gerlach von Hohenlohe. 
1 CONT Anna (c. 1326 – June 3, 1361, Fontenelles) married Johann of Lower Bavaria (d. 1340) 
1 CONT William V of Holland (1327–1388), as William I duke of Lower Bavaria, as Wiliam V count of Hainaut and Holland 
1 CONT Elisabeth (1329 – August 2, 1402, Stuttgart), married with: 
1 CONT Cangrande II della Scala, Lord of Verona (d. 1359) in Verona on November 22, 1350; 
1 CONT Count Ulrich of Württemberg (d. 1388) in 1362. 
1 CONT Louis VI the Roman (1330–1365), duke of Upper Bavaria, elector of Brandenburg. 
1 CONT Albert I of Holland (1336–1404), duke of Lower Bavaria, count of Hainaut and Holland 
1 CONT Otto V the Bavarian (1340–1379), duke of Upper Bavaria, elector of Brandenburg 
1 CONT Beatrix (1344 – December 25, 1359), married bef. October 25, 1356 Eric XII of Sweden 
1 CONT Agnes (Munich, 1345 – November 11, 1352, Munich) 
1 CONT Louis (October 1347 – 1348) 
1 CONT
1 CONT
