0 @N6000000000437348453@ NOTE Name Prefix:<NPFX> Prince Name Suffix:<NSFX> VI "The Black" prince of Wales,surnamed the Black Prince, son of Edward III.,  <kingedw.html>was born in 1330;and accompanying his father to France in 1346, took a leading part in gaini
1 CONC ngthe victory of Crecy. During his stay in France he performed many other acts ofheroism, till at length, in 1356, he won the great battle of Poitiers, when hetook King John <kingj.html> and his son prisoners, and distinguished hi
1 CONC mself as much by his courtesy to his captives as he had in the field by his unrivalledvalour. In 1361 he married Joan, called the Fair Maid of Kent, daughter of theEarl of Kent, and widow, first, of Sir Thomas Holland, and then of
1 CONC  the Earl ofSalisbury, and was soon after created by his father Prince of Aquitaine. Bordeaux then became the seat of his government. In 1367 he went to the assistance of Pedro the Cruel, king of Castile, who had been dethroned by
1 CONC  his brother, Henry of Trastamare. The latter was defeated, and Pedro re-established, but only for a short time. Prince Edward was soon after involved in disputes with his subjects, which occasioned the renewal of war between Fran
1 CONC cs and England. He died in 1376, aged 45.
0 @N6000000002322291818@ NOTE Known as the Black Prince, he was also the Duke of Cornwall, Earl of Chester
1 CONT and heir apparent to the throne of England. One of the outstanding commanders
1 CONT during the Hundred Years' War with France, winning his major victory at the
1 CONT Battle of Poitiers in 1356. He enjoyed jousting, falconry, hunting and
1 CONT gaming. His rule as Prince of Aquitaine was a failure, however great a leader
1 CONT of armies he was. As well, he was a man constantly living beyond his means.
1 CONT His sobriquet, the "Black Prince", has no contemporary justification.
0 @N6000000002187746101@ NOTE Edward, the Black Prince
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward of Woodstock, Prince of Wales, KG, known as the Black Prince (June 15, 1330 – June 8, 1376) was the eldest son of King Edward III of England and Philippa of Hainault. Born at Woodstock Palace in Oxfordshire, he was created 
1 CONC earl of Chester in 1333, Duke of Cornwall in 1337, becoming the first English Duke created, and Prince of Wales in 1343, and proved to have a prodigious military talent, as shown by his bravery at the Battle of Crécy when he was o
1 CONC nly sixteen. He followed this up ten years later with victory at the Battle of Poitiers, and in 1367 with the victory at the Battle of Najera in northern Castile.
1 CONT
1 CONT He continued the practice of using Welsh and English archers in the English army, and the English owed much of their subsequent military success to the supremacy of the English longbow over the continental crossbow. He was also on
1 CONC e of the first military leaders to introduce a uniform: he did so for his Cheshire and Flintshire troops, who were clothed in green and white cloth.
1 CONT
1 CONT The emblem of the Prince of Wales's feathers and its accompanying motto, Ich dien (German: "I serve"), are said to have been inherited by the prince from King John of Bohemia, against whom he fought in the Battle of Crécy. Accordi
1 CONC ng to legend, after the battle, the prince walked over to the king of Bohemia's dead body. The King had ridden into battle despite his blindness, and Edward admired his bravery. He picked up the King's helmet, lined with ostrich f
1 CONC eathers, and took the King's motto, Ich dien. However, this emblem and motto were not exclusively used by the Black Prince, but also by his brothers.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Iconic image of the Black PrinceEdward had been brought up with his cousin Joan "The Fair Maid of Kent". After marrying her in October, 1361 (a controversial match), Edward was sent to rule the province of Aquitaine as prince on b
1 CONC ehalf of his father. In this period, he had two sons, Edward (who died in infancy) and Richard, who would later rule as Richard II of England. He was given the lordship of Biscay by Pedro I of Castile in 1367.
1 CONT
1 CONT His military campaigning on behalf of Pedro of Castile, known to his enemies as 'Pedro the Cruel', ruined Edward's health as well as his finances, and he was forced to give up the administration of Aquitaine in 1371 and return to 
1 CONC England. Whilst making an attempt to involve himself in the government of England, he soon had to give up any hope of resuming military activity, and he died at Westminster on Trinity Sunday, June 8, 1376, leaving his young son as
1 CONC  heir to the throne. He is buried in Canterbury Cathedral.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 The name "Black Prince" 
1 CONT 2 Edward in popular culture 
1 CONT 3 Further reading 
1 CONT 4 See also 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT The name "Black Prince"
1 CONT Edward is almost always now called the "Black Prince," but he was never known as such during his lifetime (instead being known as Edward of Woodstock after his place of birth). The "Black Prince" title does not appear in writing u
1 CONC ntil at least 200 years after his death. Its origin is uncertain; while some later historians have speculated that he may have worn black armour (possibly presented to him by his father at the battle of Crécy), or a black surcoat 
1 CONC over his armour, it is also likely that the name was first coined by French chroniclers in reference to the ruinous military defeats he had inflicted on France.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Edward in popular culture
1 CONT In the movie A Knight's Tale, Edward is a fairly minor character who nonetheless plays a pivotal role at the climax of the story. He is shown earlier in the film disguising himself as a minor nobleman to compete in jousting tourna
1 CONC ments without receiving special treatment due to his royal status.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Further reading
1 CONT Richard Barber, The Life and Campaigns of the Black Prince, ISBN 0851154697 
1 CONT
1 CONT Effigy on the Black Prince's tomb in Canterbury CathedralTuchman, Barbara. _A Distant Mirror - The Calamitous 14th Century_. Alfred A. Knopf, New York City, 1978.* 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT See also
1 CONT HMS Black Prince, for the ships named in his honour
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Edward II Prince of Wales
1 CONT 1330–1376 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Richard II 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT New Creation Duke of Cornwall
1 CONT 1337–1376 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT New Creation Prince of Aquitaine
1 CONT 1361–1372 Succeeded by:
1 CONT merged with the crown 
