0 @N6000000002322297058@ NOTE The union of John of Gaunt and Catherine called the family of Beaufort. John
1 CONT was the son of King Edward III, so the blood line continued through his
1 CONT ancestors to King Henry VII, the blood line having stopped with Henry VI. He
1 CONT was also the father of King Henry IV.
0 @N6000000001744969278@ NOTE In 1362, got title of Duke of Lancaster through marriage to the joint-heiress of Henry of Grosmont. In 1371, married the daughter of Edward III's old ally Peter I, and through her claimed the throne of Castile. In 1372 French forc
1 CONC es took over much of the northern part of Aquitaine, confinig English control to a narrow strip of Norman coastline. John of Gaunt led the only significant counter-measure, a large expedition through France in 1373, which achieved
1 CONC  no military advantage.
1 CONT
1 CONT John was also the king's representative at the 'Good Parliament' of 1376. The House of Commons chose Sir Peter de la Mare of Herefordshire to negotiate a proposal to John of Gaunt. This became a grievance which turned into a scnda
1 CONC l for impeachment: the charges were tried and heard by the lords, presided over by John of Gaunt. These included accusations against Latimer, Neville, Alice Perrers and Richard Lyons and other financiers. They were dismissed from 
1 CONC office or imprisoned. This represents the first time the commons challenged the royal court.
1 CONT
1 CONT See W. M. Ormrod, The Reign of Edward III (New Haven: Yale, 1990).
1 CONT
1 CONT Geneal. source: Family tree of Edward Phipps, 39500 Stevenson Place #204, Fremont, CA 95439, 510-794-6850; email <ariod@ix.netcom.com>
