0 @N6000000006444526695@ NOTE Philip III of Navarre
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Philip III of Navarre (also known as Philip of Evreux) (1301 – September 16, 1343), Philippe, comte d'Évreux in French, was the elder son of Louis, Count of Évreux (1276–1319), and Marguerite d’Artois (1285–1311), and therefore a 
1 CONC grandson of King Philip III of France. Because of this descent, he was a possible heir to the throne of France.
1 CONT
1 CONT In due course, he inherited the County of Évreux, in Normandy, from his father (1319), and ten years later he became Philip III, king consort of Navarre, by virtue of his marriage to Queen Joan II of Navarre. She held extensive fi
1 CONC efdoms in northern France, as well as Navarre. Because of their lands and their many royal relatives, Philip and his wife were influential in both France and Navarre. They produced 8 children.
1 CONT
1 CONT While taking part in the Reconquista in 1343, he was mortally wounded and died in Jerez de la Frontera.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Children
1 CONT Maria of Navarre (1329/30–1347), first wife of King Pedro IV of Aragon (1319–1387). 
1 CONT Blanche d’Évreux (1331–1398), second wife of the much older King Philip VI of France (1293-1350). 
1 CONT Charles II of Navarre (1332–1387), King of Navarre, known as Charles le Mal, i.e. Charles the Bad. 
1 CONT Agnés (1334–1396), married Gaston III, Count of Foix (1331–1391). 
1 CONT Philip, Count of Longueville (1336–1363), married Yolande de Dampierre (1331–1395) 
1 CONT Joanna (1338–1387), a nun at Longchamps. 
1 CONT Joanna of Navarre (1339–1403), married John I, Viscount of Rohan (d. 1395). 
1 CONT Louis, Count of Beaumont-le-Roger (1341–1372), married 1358 Maria de Lizarazu, married 1366 Joanna of Durazzo (1344–1387) 
