0 @N6000000005598859013@ NOTE Henry II of Brabant (French: Henri II de Brabant, Dutch: Hendrik II va n Brabant, 1207 - February 1, 1248 in Leuven) was Duke of Brabant andL othier after the death of his father Henry I in 1235.  He ruled as Duk e from 1235 until
1 CONC  1247.  He was offered the Imperial crown when PopeGr egory IX deposed and excommunicated Emperor Frederick II.  Instead of accepting the crown, he aided in the election of his nephew WilliamII Count of Holand who was chosen as Ge
1 CONC rman King.
0 @N6000000006127668596@ NOTE Maria von Brabant (* 1226; † 18. Januar 1256) war Herzogin von Bayern, Gattin von Herzog Ludwig II., dem Strengen.Sie war die Tochter des Herzogs Heinrich II. von Brabant und Lothringen aus seiner 1. Ehe mit Maria, Tochter von Kön
1 CONC ig Philipp von Schwaben. Am 2. August 1254 heiratete sie in Landshut Ludwig II., Herzog von Bayern. Dieser ließ sie am 18. Januar1256 in Donauwörth (damals "Werd") auf der Burg Mangoldstein aus Eifersucht enthaupten. Die Ehe bieb 
1 CONC kinderlos.Geschichte Herzog Ludwig II. war Anfang 1256 in der Rheinpfalz auf Kriegszug. Maria von Brabant schrieb ihm und einem befreundeten Ritter einen Brief um ihren Mann Ludwig II. zur Rückkehr zu bewegen. Der Bote verwechselt
1 CONC e die beiden Briefe. Wohl aus einermissverstandenen Formulierung vermutete Ludwig II., dass ihm seine Frau untreu war. Er brach sofort auf nach Donauwörth, wo er aus Zorn seine Frau Maria von Brabant enthaupten ließ. Auch zwei Hof
1 CONC damen und der Burgvogt wurden in der Nacht vom 17.auf den 18. Januar 1256 Opfer der blinden Eifersucht von Ludwig II..Später erkannte Ludwig II., dass er seine Frau wohl zu Unrecht verdächtigt und hingerichtet hatte. Als Buße stif
1 CONC tete er das Zisterzienserkloster Fürstenfeld (das heutige Kloster Fürstenfeldbruck). Sein Beiname "der Strenge" wurde ihm, wohl auchwegen des Mordes an seiner ersten Frau, erst später gegeben.Neuere Untersuchungen werfen jedoch ei
1 CONC n anderes Licht auf den Fall, wonach die vermeintliche Untreue nur ein Vorwand gewesen sein könnte und eher politische Motive ausschlaggebend waren. Bedeutend ist an dieser Stelle die Verwandtschaft Maria vonBrabants mit dem herrs
1 CONC chenden deutschen König, Wilhelm von Holland.Herzogin Maria von Brabant wurde im Donauwörther Kloster Hl. Kreuz beigesetzt.
0 @N6000000002188569429@ NOTE Yolande of Aragon
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Aragonese and Valencian Royalty
1 CONT House of Barcelona
1 CONT
1 CONT Alfonso II
1 CONT Children include
1 CONT    Peter (future Peter II of Aragon)
1 CONT    Alfonso II, Count of Provence
1 CONT Peter II
1 CONT Children include
1 CONT    James (future James I of Aragon, Valencia and Majorca)
1 CONT James I
1 CONT    Peter (future Peter III of Aragon and I of Valencia and Sicily)
1 CONT    James II of Majorca
1 CONT    Isabella, Queen of France
1 CONT Peter III (I of Valencia and Sicily)
1 CONT Children include
1 CONT    Alfonso (future Alfonso III of Aragon and I of Valencia)
1 CONT    James (future James I of Sicily and II of Aragon and Valencia)
1 CONT    Frederick II of Sicily
1 CONT    Elizabeth, Queen of Portugal
1 CONT Alfonso III (I of Valencia)
1 CONT James II (I of Sicily)
1 CONT Children include
1 CONT    Alfonso (future Alfonso IV of Aragon and II of Valencia)
1 CONT Alfonso IV (II of Valencia)
1 CONT Children include
1 CONT    Peter (future Peter IV of Aragon and II of Valencia)
1 CONT Peter IV (II of Valencia)
1 CONT Children include
1 CONT    John (future John I of Aragon and Valencia)
1 CONT    Martin (future Martin II of Sicily and I of Aragon and Valencia)
1 CONT    Eleanor, Queen of Castile
1 CONT Grandchildren include
1 CONT    Ferdinand (future Ferdinand I of Aragon, Valencia and Sicily)
1 CONT John I
1 CONT    Yolande, Queen of France
1 CONT Martin I (II of Sicily)
1 CONT
1 CONT Yolande of Aragon (Barcelona, 1383 – 1443), the daughter of John I of Aragon and his wife Yolande of Bar, daughter of Robert I, Duke of Bar and Marie Valois. She was also known as Jolantha de Aragon and Violant d'Aragó. She probab
1 CONC ly commissioned the famous Rohan Hours.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Claim to the throne
1 CONT     * 2 France and the House of Anjou
1 CONT     * 3 The Dauphin
1 CONT     * 4 Chronology
1 CONT     * 5 Family and children
1 CONT     * 6 Fictional portrayals
1 CONT     * 7 Sources
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Claim to the throne
1 CONT
1 CONT She played an important role in the politics of the Angevin Empire, France, and Aragon, during the first half of the 15th century. As the surviving daughter of sonless King John I of Aragon, she claimed the throne of Aragon after 
1 CONC the death of her elder sister Joan (Countess of Foix) and her uncle King Martin I of Aragon. However, unclear though they were, the succession rules of Aragon and Barcelona at that time were understood to favor all male relatives 
1 CONC before any female (this is how Yolande's uncle, Martin of Aragon, inherited the throne of Aragon). Martin died without surviving issue in 1410, and after two years without a king, the Estates of Aragon elected Ferdinand de Anteque
1 CONC ra as the next King of Aragon. Ferdinand was the second son of Eleanor of Aragon and John I of Castile.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Anjou candidate was Yolande's eldest son Louis III of Anjou, Duke of Calabria, whose claim lost in the Pact of Caspe. Yolande and her sons regarded themselves as heirs of higher claim, and began to use the title of Kings of Ar
1 CONC agon. From this "inheritance" forward (Aragon added to other Anjou King titles), Yolande was called the "Queen of Four Kingdoms" - the four being apparently Sicily, Jerusalem, Cyprus and Aragon; another interpretation has been Nap
1 CONC les separate from Sicily, and then probably excluding Cyprus. However, the truth was that Yolande and her family held lands in any of the said kingdoms only for short intervals, and the island of Sicily as well as Cyprus-Jerusalem
1 CONC  apparently was never held by them in reality. Their true realm was Anjou fiefdoms around France: they held uncontestably the provinces of Provence and Anjou, also at times Bar, Maine, Touraine and Valois. Her son, René, through h
1 CONC is marriage became ruler of Lorraine.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] France and the House of Anjou
1 CONT
1 CONT In the emerging second phase of the Hundred Years' War, Yolande chose to support the French (for example, the Armagnac party) against the English and against the Burgundians; she supported the claim of Dauphin Charles who, relying
1 CONC  upon Yolande's resources and help, succeeded in becoming crowned Charles VII of France. As Charles' own mother, Isabeau of Bavaria, worked against Charles' claims, it has been said that Yolande was the person who kept the adolesc
1 CONC ent Charles alive and protected him when all sorts of machinations and poisonings were attempted, and acted as mother to young Charles. She removed Charles from his parents' Court and kept him in her own castles, usually in the Lo
1 CONC ire Valley, where Charles received Joan of Arc. Yolande married young Charles to her daughter, Mary of Anjou, thus becoming Charles' mother-in-law. This led to Yolande's personal, and crucial, involvement in the struggle for the s
1 CONC urvival of the House of Valois in France.
1 CONT
1 CONT Yolande's marriage to Louis II of Anjou, at Arles in December 1400, was part of an effort, made also in earlier such marriages, to resolve the contested claims upon the kingdom of Sicily and Naples between the houses of Anjou and 
1 CONC Aragon. Louis spent much of his life fighting in Italy for his claim to the Kingdom of Naples. In France, Yolande was the Duchess of Anjou and the Countess of Provence. She preferred to hold court in Angers and Saumur.
1 CONT
1 CONT With the victory of the English over the French at Agincourt in 1415, the Duchy of Anjou was threatened. The French king, Charles VI, was mentally ill and his realm was in civil war between the Burgundians and the Orleanists (Arma
1 CONC gnacs). The situation was made worse by the alliance between the Duke of Burgundy, John the Fearless, and the English, and by the French queen, Isabeau of Bavaria, submitting to the Duke of Burgundy's scheme to deny the crown of F
1 CONC rance to the children of Charles VI. Fearing the abusive power building behind the Duke of Burgundy, Louis II had Yolande move with her children and future son-in-law, Charles, to Provence in southern France.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] The Dauphin
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1416, the Dauphin Louis, Charles de Ponthieu's oldest brother, died. In 1417, his second-oldest brother (and subsequent Dauphin), Jean, died. Both brothers had been in the care of the Duke of Burgundy. Yolande was the protectre
1 CONC ss of her son-in-law, Charles, who became the new Dauphin. She refused Queen Isabeau's orders to return Charles to the French Court, reportedly replying, "We have not nurtured and cherished this one for you to make him die like hi
1 CONC s brothers or to go mad like his father, or to become English like you. I keep him for my own. Come and take him away, if you dare."
1 CONT
1 CONT On 29 April 1417, Louis II d'Anjou died of illness, leaving Yolande, at age 33, in control of the House of Anjou. She acted as a Regent for her son because of his youth. She also had the fate of the French royal house of Valois in
1 CONC  her care. Her young son-in-law, the Dauphin Charles, was exceptionally vulnerable to designs of the English King, Henry V, and to his older cousin, John the Fearless. Charles' nearest older relatives, the Dukes of Orléans and of 
1 CONC Bourbon, had been made prisoners at Agincourt and were held captive by the English. With his mother, Queen Isabeau, and the Duke of Burgundy allied with the English, the dauphin Charles had no power to support him other than that 
1 CONC of the House of Anjou and the smaller House of Armagnac (which had taken up the Orleanists' cause).
1 CONT
1 CONT Following the assassination of Jean the Fearless at Montereau in 1419, Jean's son, Philippe le Bon, succeeded as the Duke of Burgundy, and with Henry V of England forced the Treaty of Troyes (21 May 1420) on the mentally-ill King 
1 CONC Charles VI. The treaty designated Henry as "Regent of France" and Heir to the French throne. Following this, in 1421, the Dauphin Charles was declared as disinherited. When both Henry V of England and Charles VI died (31 August an
1 CONC d 21 October, respectively) in 1422, legitimately the Dauphin Charles, at age 19, became Charles VII of France. Charles' title was challenged by the English (and their Burgundian allies), who supported the infant son of Henry V an
1 CONC d Catherine, Henry VI of England. This was the stage for the last phase of the Hundred Years' War, or the "War of Charles VII".
1 CONT
1 CONT In this struggle, Yolande played a prominent role in surrounding the young Valois King with advisors and servants associated with the House of Anjou. She maneuvered the duke of Brittany to break an alliance with the English, and w
1 CONC as responsible for a soldier from the Breton ducal family, Arthur de Richemont, becoming Constable of France in 1425. Yolande's early and strong support of Joan of Arc, when others had doubts, suggests the Duchess' possible larger
1 CONC  role in the orchestrating Joan's appearance on the scene. Yolande unquestionably practiced realistic politics. Using the Constable de Richemont, Yolande was behind the forceful removal of several of Charles VII's less desirable a
1 CONC dvisors. The worst, La Trémoille, was attacked and forced from the court in 1433. Yolande was not averse to "plant", or to make use of, mistresses of influential men. She had a network of such women in the courts of Lorraine, Burg
1 CONC undy, Brittany, and even in that of her son-in-law.
1 CONT
1 CONT The contemporary chronicler and Bishop of Beauvais, Jean Juvenal des Ursins (1433-44), described Yolande as "the prettiest woman in the kingdom." Bourdigné, chronicler of the house of Anjou, says of her: "She who was said to be th
1 CONC e wisest and most beautiful princess in Christendom." Later, King Louis XI of France recalled that his grandmother had "a man's heart in a woman's body." A twentieth-century French author, Jehanne d'Orliac, wrote one of the few wo
1 CONC rks specifically on Yolande, and noted that the duchess remains unappreciated for her genius and influence in the reign of Charles VII. "She is mentioned in passing because she is the pivot of all important events for forty-two ye
1 CONC ars in France", while "Joan [of Arc] was in the public eye only eleven months."
1 CONT
1 CONT Yolande retired to Angers, and then to Saumur, where she died at the Château de Tuce-de-Saumur on 14 December 1443.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Chronology
1 CONT
1 CONT     1379 Yolande born at Zaragoza, Aragon.
1 CONT     1400 Yolande married Louis II of Anjou, at Arles, France in December.
1 CONT     1410 Death of King Martin of Aragon.
1 CONT     1412 Yolande's son, Louis, was not recognized as King of Aragon; instead their kinsman, Ferdinand I, became King there.
1 CONT     1413 Louis II d'Anjou aligned with the Orleanist [later known as 'Armagnac'] faction in opposition to Burgundian faction.
1 CONT     1413 Yolande took Charles, her prospective son-in-law, to her Court in Angers.
1 CONT     1417 Yolande was widowed 29 April.
1 CONT     1419 On 29 June, Yolande gained an audience with Charles VI and prevailed upon him to sign the decree making his son 'Lieutenant-General of the Kingdom' and gave the reason that Charles was the 'son of the king', and the monar
1 CONC ch's acknowledgment of him as his son and rightful heir. The act removed Isabeau from any claim to be Regent. Yolande retired to Provence.
1 CONT     1423 Yolande returned from Provence. She initiated the first of a few short-lived treaties with Brittany.
1 CONT     1424-1427 Yolande presided over the Estates-General. Yolande again obtained a treaty with the Duke of Brittany and enlisted the Duke's brother, Arthur de Richemont to support the Valois cause.
1 CONT     1427 The English Regent in France, John, Duke of Bedford, moved to take the Duchy of Anjou for himself. Yolande responded with a series of Valois Court appointments and marriage agreements among various noble houses that frust
1 CONC rated English and Burgundian initiatives, and sustained the threatened Valois crown until more dramatic reversal could be established. Discord between la Trémoille, a key advisor to Charles VII, and the Constable Richemont led to 
1 CONC Richemont beng banished from the Valois Court.
1 CONT     1429 Yolande was placed in charge of one of the examinations of Joan of Arc, whom the Duchess strongly supported. Yolande arranged for financing 'Jeanne d'Arc's army' that went to relieve Orléans.
1 CONT     1431 Yolande resided at Saumur, where Charles VII met with his Assembly. Yolande's youngest daughter, Yolanda, married the Hereditary Prince of Brittany. Yolande's son René inherited the Duchy of Lorraine, but was made prisone
1 CONC r at the Battle of Bulgnéville on June 30, 1431.
1 CONT     1433 Richemont [who had returned to the court in 1432] overthrew La Trémoïlle. Yolande's youngest son, Charles, Comte de Le Maine, assumed the position as chief advisor to Charles VII.
1 CONT     1434 Yolande's son, Louis III d'Anjou, died, and René became Duc d'Anjou, as well as Heir to the titular claim to Sicily. [Queen Joan of Naples and Sicily had made Louis III her co-regent and heir and, after Louis' death, she 
1 CONC named René as his brother's heir.]
1 CONT     1437 René was released from a Burgundian prison for a substantial ransom. He went to Italy in 1438 and engaged in a war against Alfonso V of Aragon for the disputed title to the Kingdom of Naples. René was forced to abandon Na
1 CONC ples in the summer of 1442. Soon afterward, Yolande died.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family and children
1 CONT
1 CONT She was betrothed to the heir of Anjou, Louis (who had one year earlier succeeded in conquering Naples and become in reality King Ludovico II of Naples) in 1390, and married him on 2 December 1400 at Montpellier. Their children we
1 CONC re:
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. Louis III (1403-1434), duke of Anjou, King of Naples
1 CONT    2. Marie (1404-1463), married to King Charles VII of France
1 CONT    3. [?] (1406-), married to count of Geneva
1 CONT    4. René (1408-1480) - duke of Anjou, Duke of Bar, Duke Consort of Lorraine, titular King of Sicily and Naples
1 CONT    5. Yolande (1412-1440), married to Francis I, Duke of Brittany
1 CONT    6. Charles (1414-1472), Count of Maine (who never was duke of Anjou, but his namesake son was)
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Fictional portrayals
1 CONT
1 CONT Yolande appears as a character in the 1999 film The Messenger: The Story of Joan of Arc, in which she is played by actress Faye Dunaway.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Jehanne d'Orliac, Yolande d'Anjou, la reine des quatre royaumes (Paris, Plon, 1933)
1 CONT     * Philippe Erlanger, Charles VII et son mystère (Paris, Gallimard 1945, Perrin 1973, 1981)
1 CONT     * Philippe Erlanger, 9 femmes qui ont fait la France, Historia (Septembre 1971, pp.40-53)
0 @N6000000002188026025@ NOTE Louis III of Naples
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Louis III (1403 – 12 November 1434) was titular King of Naples 1417–1426, Count of Provence, Forcalquier, Piedmont, and Maine and Duke of Anjou 1417–1434, and Duke of Calabria 1426–1434.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the eldest son and heir of Louis II of Anjou and Yolande of Aragon, Queen of Four Kingdoms.
1 CONT
1 CONT The throne of Aragon fell vacant in 1410 (Louis was then 6 years old) when king Martin I of Aragon deceased. Louis' mother Yolande was the surviving daughter of sonless King John I of Aragon, Martin's predecessor. They claimed the
1 CONC  throne of Aragon to young Louis.
1 CONT
1 CONT However, unclear though they were, the succession rules of Aragon and Barcelona at that time were understood to favor all male relatives before any female, (this is how Yolande's uncle, Martin of Aragon, inherited the throne of Ar
1 CONC agon). Martin died without surviving issue in 1410, and after two years without a king, the Estates of Aragon by Compromise of Caspe in 1412 elected Ferdinand de Antequera as the next King of Aragon. Ferdinand was the second son o
1 CONC f Eleanor of Aragon and John I of Castile. The 8-year-old Louis at that point lost one of his four kingdoms. The family however had secured some Aragonese lands in Montpellier and Roussillon.
1 CONT
1 CONT Yolande and her sons regarded themselves as heirs of higher claim, and began to use the title of Kings of Aragon. From this "inheritance" forward (Aragon added to other Anjou King titles), Louis and Yolande were called the King an
1 CONC d Queen of Four Kingdoms - the four being apparently Sicily, Jerusalem, Cyprus and Aragon; another interpretation has been Naples separate from Sicily, and then probably excluding Cyprus. However, the truth was that Yolande and he
1 CONC r family held lands in any of the said kingdoms only for short intervals, and the island of Sicily as well as Cyprus-Jerusalem apparently was never held by them in reality.
1 CONT
1 CONT Pope Martin V invested Louis III on December 4, 1419 as 'King of Sicily' (Naples). This was in contrast with the will of the childless and aged queen of the Italian kingdom, Joanna II, who had adopted Alfonso V of Aragon as her he
1 CONC ir. In 1420 Louis disembarked in Campania and besieged Naples, but had to flee at the arrive of an Aragonese fleet. Alfonso entered the city in 1421 and Louis lost the support of the Pope, tired by the costs of the war. However, w
1 CONC hen the relationships between Alfonso and queen suddenly worsened. After the arrest of Joanna's lover and prime minister, Gianni Caracciolo, the queen moved to Aversa where Louis joined her. He was adopted and named heir in lieu o
1 CONC f Alfonso, giving him the title of Duke of Calabria. When Alfonso had to return in Spain, the kingdom was pacified. Louis moved to his feudal possession in Calabria, where he lived with Margaret of Savoia (1416-1479), daughter of 
1 CONC Amedeo VIII of Savoy. They had no children.
1 CONT
1 CONT Louis could never become king effectively, as he died of malaria at Cosenza in 1434. After Joanna's death the following year, his brother René of Anjou was named King of Naples.
1 CONT This biography of a member of a European royal house is a stub. You can help Wikipedia by expanding it.
