0 @N6000000002187582358@ NOTE Albert II of Austria
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Albert II of Austria (Habsburg, December 12, 1298 – Vienna, August 16, 1358, known as the Wise or the Lame) was Duke of Austria.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Life
1 CONT Albert II was born as the son of Emperor Albert I on December 12, 1298. He became the joint ruler of all Habsburg lands with his younger brother, Otto the Merry in 1330, while increasing his possessions by the inheritance of his w
1 CONC ife Joan, which was made up of the County of Pfirt and several cities. Furthermore, Albert succeeded in establishing his claims on Carinthia and Carniola against John of Bohemia. Albert's high reputation is shown by the fact that 
1 CONC Pope Benedict XII asked him to mediate in the church's conflict with Emperor Louis the Bavarian in 1335. Also, King Philippe VI of France 1337 asked him for his help against Emperor Louis and against King Edward III of England. Ne
1 CONC vertheless, Albert remained faithful to the Emperor until Louis' death. He established the "Albertinian House Rule" (Albertinische Hausordnung) to predetermine the rules of succession in the Austrian lands, which, however, was dis
1 CONC regarded after his death and only renewed by Emperor Maximilian I. It later became a part of the Pragmatic Sanction, so that it effectively remained one of the basic laws of Austria until 1918. Styria owes him its (former) constit
1 CONC ution, the so-called "Mountain Book" (Bergbüchel); the same is true for Carinthia. Under Albert's rule, the construction of the Gothic Choir in the Cathedral of Saint Stephan in Vienna were started, which is why it is also known a
1 CONC s Albertinian Choir. There are only suspicions about his temporal paralysis, but usually polyarthritis is mentioned as the cause. However, it did not prevent him from fathering four children: Rudolf, who succeeded him as duke, Fre
1 CONC derick, Albert, and Leopold. His land grieved when Albert died on August 16, 1358.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Family and children
1 CONT He married 15 February 1324 to Countesse Johanna of Pfirt, daughter of Count Ulrich III of Pfirt and had the following children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Rudolf IV of Austria November 1, 1339,Vienna–July 27, 1365, Milan). 
1 CONT Frederick III of Austria (1347, Vienna–1362, Vienna). 
1 CONT Albert III of Austria (September 9, 1349,Vienna–August 29, 1395, Castle Laxenburg). 
1 CONT Leopold III(November 1, 1351,Vienna–July 9, 1386, Sempach). 
1 CONT Katharina (1342, Vienna–10 January 1381, Vienna), Abbess of St.Klara in Vienna. 
1 CONT Margarete (1346, Vienna–14 January 1366, Brünn), married to: 
1 CONT in Passau 4 September 1359 to Count Meinhard III of Gorizia-Tyrol; 
1 CONT in Vienna 1364 to Margrave Johann Heinrich of Moravia. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Frederick I Duke of Austria and Duke of Styria
1 CONT jointly with
1 CONT Otto the Merry Succeeded by:
1 CONT Rudolf IV 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Henry V Duke of Carinthia
1 CONT Originally co-ruler to
1 CONT Otto the Merry 
0 @N6000000006128279893@ NOTE Albrecht II. von Österreich (* 12. Dezember 1298 auf der Habsburg (Aargau); † 16. August 1358 in Wien; genannt der Weise oder Lahme) war ein deutscher Fürst und Herzog von Österreich.Albrecht II., genannt der Weise oder Lahme, wur
1 CONC de als Sohn des römisch-deutschen Königs Albrecht I. am 12. Dezember 1298 geboren. Er gelangte gemeinschaftlich mit seinem jüngsten Bruder Otto 1330 an die Regierung allerhabsburgisch-österreichischen Länder. Diese vermehrte er du
1 CONC rch die Güter seiner Gemahlin Johanna (Grafschaft Pfirt und einige Städte).Ferner setzte Albrecht seine Ansprüche auf Kärnten gegen Johann von Böhmen durch: Nach dem Tod Heinrichs von Kärnten übertrug Kaiser Ludwig der Bayer den B
1 CONC rüdern Otto und Albrecht am 2. Mai 1335 in Linz die Gebiete Kärnten sowie den südlichen Teilvon Tirol als Reichslehen. Albrechts hohes Ansehen beweist der Umstand, dass ihn nicht nur Papst Benedikt XII. 1335 zur Vermittlung der Au
1 CONC ssöhnung Kaiser Ludwigs mit der Kirche aufforderte, sondern auch König Philipp VI. von Frankreich 1337 umseine Hilfe gegen Kaiser Ludwig und König Eduard von England bat.Dessen ungeachtet blieb der Erzherzog dem Kaiser bis zum Tod
1 CONC e unverbrüchlich treu und war später auch mit dessen Sohn Ludwig dem Brandenburger eng verbündet. Nach der zweiten Belagerung Zürichs durch Albrecht kam es durch die Vermittlung Ludwigszwischen den Konfliktparteien am 1. September
