0 @N6000000002188113700@ NOTE Eberhard II, Count of Württemberg
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Eberhard II, called "der Greiner" (the Jarrer)(after 1315–15 March 1392, Stuttgart), Count of Württemberg from 1344 until 1392.
1 CONT
1 CONT Eberhard II was son of Count Ulrich III of Württemberg and Sofie of Pfirt. He married Countess Elizabeth von Henneberg-Schleusingen on September 17, 1342. They had two children. Ulrich, father of his successor Eberhard III, Eberha
1 CONC rd III and Sophie, latter duchess of Lorraine.
1 CONT
1 CONT From the beginning of his regency in 1344 up to 1361 he reigned together with his brother Ulrich IV, whom he forced to sign a treaty which confirmed the unseperability of Württemberg. Soon afterwards Ulrich IV renounced his co-rul
1 CONC e on May 1, 1362. To strenghten his might and to enlarge the territory of Württemberg Eberhard II joined several pacts and clashes with Emperor Charles IV. As a result he and his brother received extra privilegies, for example the
1 CONC  sole jurisdiction, which newly defined the County of Württemberg.
1 CONT
1 CONT A sensation was created 1367 by the assault of Count Wolf von Eberstein on Eberhard II and his son Ulrich during their stay "in Wildbad" (presumably Wildbad or Teinach). Both could flee and and sieged castle Neueberstein imediatel
1 CONC y with a large amount of men. However this siege turned out to be unsuccessful.
1 CONT
1 CONT During his regency he strongly aligned his policy gainst the Free Imperial Cities, which stood in the way of the extension of Württemberg`s territory. He fought battles against the towns united in the Schwäbischer Städtebund (Swab
1 CONC ian City League) in 1376, 1372 in Altheim, 1377 in Reutlingen and 1388 near Döffingen. The result of this was a stalemate which secured the independency of the towns. Nevertheless the territorial gains during his rule were substan
1 CONC tial, for example Böblingen and Calw.
1 CONT
1 CONT His heir apparent, Ulrich was killed in the victorious battle of Döffingen.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Reception
1 CONT
1 CONT Eberhard entered literature through Schiller and Uhland.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
