1 CONC s daughter of "seniori Walerano filio Henrici" & his wife Ermesinde[134].  She was Regent of Lorraine for her son from 1251 until 21 Mar 1255[135].  Duke Mathieu II & his wife had five children:
0 @N6000000000337198882@ NOTE (Research):born c. 1269/74, Valenciennes, Hainaut died Aug. 24, 1313, Buonconvento, near Siena, Italy   count of Luxembourg (as Henry IV), German king (from 1308), and Holy Roman emperor (from 1312) who strengthened the position o
1 CONC f his family by obtaining the throne of Bohemia for his son. He failed, however, in his attempt to bind Italy firmly to the empire.   Henry succeeded his father, Henry III, as count of Luxembourg in 1288. He was chosen German king
1 CONC  in November 1308 at Frankfurt and was crowned at Aachen the following January, becoming the first German king of the House of Luxembourg.    The Columbia Encyclopedia, Sixth Edition.  2001.   Henry VII, Holy Roman emperor and Ger
1 CONC man king c.1275-1313, Holy Roman emperor (1312-13) and German king (1308-13). A minor count of the house of Luxembourg, Henry was elected German king on the death of King Albert I after the electors had set aside the two main cont
1 CONC enders, Albert's eldest son, Frederick of Austria, and the French prince Charles of Valois. By accepting Elizabeth of Bohemia's offer (1310) to marry his son, John of Luxembourg, he gained Bohemia for his house and made it the mai
1 CONC n rival to the house of Hapsburg. He secured the German princes' approval for the acquisition by lavishly distributing the imperial domain. Henry's chief concern, however, was to renew the Hohenstaufen policy of making Italy the m
1 CONC ain source of imperial power. Pope Clement V and, among others, Dante welcomed his rule as a means of ending the by now almost meaningless strife of the Guelphs and Ghibellines. Entering the peninsula in 1310, Henry proclaimed him
1 CONC self above all parties and received the homage of leaders of both of the chief factions; in Jan., 1311, he was crowned king of the Lombards at Milan, a Guelph city. A revolt occurred in Milan, however, when Henry levied taxes on t
1 CONC he city to support his army; although the revolt was suppressed, it drove Henry into the Ghibelline camp and precipitated war with the Guelph cities. Henry did not reach Rome until the following year, where on June 29, 1312, he wa
1 CONC s crowned Holy Roman emperor. Leaving Rome, he besieged Florence, but without success; in 1313, having allied himself with King Frederick II of Sicily, he pronounced the ban of the empire against King Robert of Naples, who opposed
1 CONC  Henry's policy in Italy. While preparing to attack Robert, Henry died of fever. Henry VII's abortive Italian campaign only served to prove the futility of any attempt to revive the ancient imperial policy at a time when the papac
1 CONC y and S Italy were controlled by France and the N Italian towns were autonomous. Henry was succeeded by Holy Roman Emperor Louis IV.    1  See W. M. Bowsky, Henry VII in Italy (1960).    2    The Columbia Encyclopedia, Sixth Editi
1 CONC on. Copyright c 2001 Columbia University Press.
0 @N6000000002187840638@ NOTE Henry VII, Holy Roman Emperor
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry VII, (In German: Heinrich; in Italian: Arrigo), ca. 1275-1313, of the House of Luxembourg, was Holy Roman Emperor from 1312 to 1313, and also King of the Romans from 1308.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Life
1 CONT He was a son of Count Henry VI of Luxemburg and Beatrice of Avesnes. His son, John of Luxembourg, was elected as king of Bohemia in 1310. Henry VII then travelled to Rome to be crowned as emperor, the title having been vacant sinc
1 CONC e the death of Frederick II. His coronation was on June 29, 1312.
1 CONT
1 CONT As Emperor he planned to restore the glory of the Holy Roman Empire, and indeed he restored imperial power in parts of northern Italy, fighting against the anti-imperial commune of Florence. However, he quarrelled with the Guelphs
1 CONC  and Ghibellines, especially in the free cities in Tuscany, and King Robert of Naples and Pope Clement V were both worried about his firm imperial policies. Henry wanted to punish Robert of Naples for his disloyal actions (Robert 
1 CONC was technically Henry's vassal), but he died on August 24, 1313, near Siena.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry is the famous alto Arrigo in Dante's Paradise. Dante also alludes to him numerous times in "Purgatorio" as the savior who will bring imperial rule back to Italy, and end the inappropriate temporal control of the Church. Henr
1 CONC y VII's success in Italy was not lasting, however, and after his death the anti-imperialist forces regained control.
1 CONT
1 CONT The title of Emperor, disputed by rival claimants, remained vacant for most of the next decade.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Family and children
1 CONT He was married in Tervueren 9 July 1292 with Margaret of Brabant, daughter of John I, Duke of Brabant and had the following children:
1 CONT
1 CONT John I, Count of Luxemburg (10 August 1296 – 26 August 1346), 
1 CONT Marie (1304–26 March 1324, Issoudun-en-Berry), married in Paris 21 September 1322 to King Charles IV of France. 
1 CONT Beatrix (1305–11 November 1319), married 1318 to King Charles I of Hungary. 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Reference
1 CONT William M. Bowsky, Henry VII in Italy, 1960. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Albert I King of Germany
1 CONT 1308–1313 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Louis IV 
1 CONT King of the Romans
1 CONT 1308–1313 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Louis IV 
1 CONT Holy Roman Emperor
1 CONT 1312–1313 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Henry V Count of Luxemburg
1 CONT 1288–1313 Succeeded by:
1 CONT John I 
