0 @N6000000002187825430@ NOTE Agnes of Burgundy
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Agnes of Burgundy (1407- December 1, 1476) was the daughter of John I, Duke of Burgundy (1371-1419) and Margaret of Bavaria. Her maternal grandparents were Albert I, Duke of Bavaria and Margarete of Brieg.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Marriage and Issue
1 CONT Agnes was married to Charles I, Duke of Bourbon (1401-1456), and together they had eleven children:
1 CONT
1 CONT John II, Duke of Bourbon (1426–1488). 
1 CONT Mary of Bourbon (1428–1448). 
1 CONT Philip of Bourbon (1430–1440). 
1 CONT Charles II, Duke of Bourbon (1434–1488). 
1 CONT Isabella of Bourbon (1436–1465). 
1 CONT Peter II, Duke of Bourbon (1438–1503). 
1 CONT Louis of Bourbon (1438-1482). 
1 CONT Margaret of Bourbon (1439–1483). 
1 CONT Catherine of Bourbon (1440–1469). 
1 CONT Joanna of Bourbon (1442–1493). 
1 CONT Jacques of Bourbon (1445–1468). 
0 @N6000000000337130798@ NOTE (Research):Amadeus VII Encyclop‘dia Britannica Article    born 1360, Chamb‚ry, Savoy [now in France] died Nov. 1, 1391, Ripaille byname Amadeus The Red Count,  Italian Amedeo Il Conte Rosso, French Am‚d‚e Le Comte Rouge count of S
1 CONC avoy (1383-91), during whose short rule the county of Savoy acquired Nice and other Proven‡al towns.   Son of Amadeus VI and Bonne of Bourbon, Amadeus married (1377) the daughter of Jean, duc de Berry, brother of the king of Franc
1 CONC e. His father, the "Green Count," wore his customary emerald-green livery at the wedding, and the groom earned his own sobriquet by wearing bright red. Invested with the traditional fief of Savoyard heirs apparent, the seigneury o
1 CONC f Bresse (west of Geneva), he was soon embroiled in an intermittent six-year war against a vassal, Edward of Beaujeu, who refused him homage. In 1382 he led Savoyard troops against Flemish rebels at the Battle of Rozebeke.   Amade
1 CONC us died suddenly at the castle of Ripaille, south of Lake Geneva, at the age of 31, apparently as a result of a doctor's blunder.
0 @N6000000000337130761@ NOTE (Research):Amadeus VIII Encyclop‘dia Britannica Article    born 1383, Chamb‚ry, Savoy [now in France] died Jan. 7, 1451, Geneva [now in Switzerland]   byname Amadeus The Peaceful,  Italian Amedeo Il Pacifico, French Am‚d‚e Le Pais
1 CONC ible,  also called Pope Felix V count (1391-1416) and duke (1416-40) of Savoy, first member of the house of Savoy to assume the title of duke. His 42-year reign saw the extension of his authority from Lake Neuchƒtel on the north t
1 CONC o the Ligurian coast, and under the title of Felix V he was an antipope for 10 years (1439-49).   The sudden death of Amadeus VII in 1391 left his eight-year-old son under a regency. In 1393 Amadeus married Mary, daughter of Phili
1 CONC p the Bold, duke of Burgundy. During the early 1400s he increased the territories of Savoy, laying claim to Geneva in 1401 and buying other nearby lands in 1402 and 1406. In 1416 the Holy Roman emperor Sigismund elevated the count
1 CONC y of Savoy to the rank of a duchy.   In 1434, after 42 years on the throne, Amadeus retired to a monastery at Ripaille. His retirement was only partial, however, and he continued to exercise power, with his son Louis (Ludovico) ac
1 CONC ting as his lieutenant. During that period Amadeus' daughter Margherita was betrothed to Louis III of Anjou, pretender to the throne of Naples. Louis III died suddenly in 1434, and Amadeus briefly claimed Naples for Margherita but
1 CONC  in the end abandoned the kingdom to Alfonso V of Aragon. In 1440 Amadeus abdicated. One adventure remained to the aging duke: when Pope Eugenius IV broke off relations with the rebellious Council of Basel in 1439, the mutinous pr
1 CONC elates elected Amadeus pope under the name of Felix V. Amadeus-Felix resigned 10 years later, under pressure from the kings of France, England, and Sicily, and was a cardinal for his last two years.
0 @N6000000006127680598@ NOTE Felix V. (* 4. Dezember 1383; † 7. Januar 1451) - eigentlich Graf Amadeus VIII., "der Friedfertige", von Savoyen - war 1439-1449 der letzte katholische Gegenpapst.1416 von Kaiser Sigismund zum Herzog erhoben und auch sonst politis
1 CONC ch durchaus erfolgreich, übergab er 1434 seinem Sohn die Amtsgeschäfte und zog sich mit sechs Rittern in ein Kloster zurück, wo er den Moritzorden, einen Ritterorden von mäßiglangem Bestand gründete.Als Papst Eugen IV. 1439 das Ko
1 CONC nzil von Basel nach Ferrara verlegte, spaltete sich das Konzil und der in Basel verbliebene Teil setzte am 5. November Eugen ab und wählte in einem nur wenige Stunden dauernden Konklave Amadeus zum Papst. Erresidierte in Genf, Lau
1 CONC sanne und Basel, wurde aber nur in Aragonien, Ungarn, Bayern und der Schweiz anerkannt, weswegen er am 7. April 1449 resignierte, wobei ihm der Rücktritt mit der Gewährung mehrerer Privilegien erleichtert wurde. Bis zuseinem Tod 1
1 CONC 451 war er Kardinalbischof von Sabina und Genf. Er gilt als der letzte historische Gegenpapst.
0 @N6000000002188376876@ NOTE John IV of Armagnac
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT John IV (born October 15, 1396 in Rodez, died on November 5, 1450 in the Isle-Jordan) was a count of Armagnac, of Fézensac and Rodez from 1418 to 1450. He was the son of Bernard VII of Armagnac, count d' Armagnac, of Fézensac, Par
1 CONC diac and Rodez and the brother of Bernard of Armagnac, count de Pardiac, of La Marche and duke of Nemours.
1 CONT
1 CONT His father had taken by the force the county of Comminges, but Jean IV could not prevent the remarriage of Marguerite with Mathieu de Foix in 1419. Subsequently they took the County of Comminges. In 1425, he recognized the king of
1 CONC  Castille as overlord of Armagnac. The king of France, occupied fighting the English, could not intervene, but did not forget the affront. Later, Jean IV negotiated the marriage of his daughter Isabelle with Henri VI, but he back 
1 CONC off from this plan after the threats of the king of France. In 1440, he took part in a revolt of the barons and the Dauphin of France, but the coalition was overcome by Charles VII, who granted forgiveness to the insurrectionists.
1 CONC  He asked the count of Armagnac to give up its kingly formula, but this last refused.
1 CONT
1 CONT Charles VII then asked the Dauphin to punish the recalcitrant Jean IV. Besieged in the Isle-Jordan, Jean was made prisoner and imprisoned in Carcassonne in 1443. He was pardoned three years latter, but his counties were directed b
1 CONC y royal officers, and he did not have any more capacities until his death.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Marriages and children
1 CONT
1 CONT He married Blanche of Brittany (1395-1419) on June 16th, 1407, daughter of John V, Duke of Brittany and Jeanne d' Evreux, and had:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Bonne (b. 1416; † before 1448)
1 CONT
1 CONT He remaried on May 10 1419, shortly after the death of his first wife. This time he married Isabelle d' Évreux (b. 1395; † 1450), girl of Charles III, king of Navarre, and of Eleanor of Castille. They had:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Marie of Armagnac (b. 1420; † 1473), married in 1437 with John II of Alençon (b. 1409; † 1476), Duke of Alençon; maternal great-grandparents of King Henry IV of France.
1 CONT     * Jean V of Armagnac (b. 1420; † 1473), Viscount of Lomagne, then count d' Armagnac, of Fézensac and Rodez.
1 CONT     * Eléonore (b. 1423; † 1456), married in 1446 with Louis de Chalon (b. 1389; † 1463), prince d'Orange, lord of Arlay and Arguel.
1 CONT     * Charles Ier (b. 1425; † 1497), Viscount of Fézensaguet, then count d' Armagnac, of Fézensac and Rodez.
1 CONT     * Isabelle (b. 1430; † 1476), lady of the Four-Valleys.
0 @N6000000002187862141@ NOTE John II, Duke of Brabant
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Jan II van Brabant (September 27, 1275 – October 27, 1312, Tervuren), also called John II, the Peaceful, was Duke of Brabant, Lothier and Limburg (1294–1312). He was the son of John I of Brabant and Margaretha of Flanders, daughte
1 CONC r of Guy of Dampierre.
1 CONT
1 CONT On July 8, 1290, Jan married Margaret Plantagenet in Westminster Abbey, London. She was a daughter of King Edward I of England and his first Queen consort Eleanor of Castile. Only one child was born out of this marriage, John III,
1 CONC  Duke of Brabant.
1 CONT
1 CONT During the reign of Jan II, Brabant continues supporting a coalition to stop French expansion. He tried to conquer South Holland (district of medieval Holland) from the pro-french count John II of Holland, but was not successful. 
1 CONC John, who suffered from kidney stones and wanted his duchy to be peacefully handed over to his son upon his death, in 1312 signed the famous Charter of Kortenberg.
1 CONT
1 CONT After his death Jan II was buried in the St. Michael and Gudula Cathedral in Brussels.
1 CONT
1 CONT See also: Dukes of Brabant family tree
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT John I Duke of Brabant, Lothier, and Limburg
1 CONT 1294–1312 Succeeded by
1 CONT John III
